WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.455 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:02.455 --> 00:00:04.419 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:04.419 --> 00:00:09.329 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:09.329 --> 00:00:16.610 align:middle line:90%


00:00:16.610 --> 00:00:17.360 align:middle line:90%
GLENN ELLISON: OK.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:18.330 align:middle line:90%
Welcome back.

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:20.270 align:middle line:84%
So if you remember,
last class, I

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:25.460 align:middle line:84%
was talking about search theory,
and discussed two main things.

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:29.750 align:middle line:84%
One is that search
costs can complement

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:32.720 align:middle line:84%
product differentiation and
raise prices in markets.

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:35.510 align:middle line:84%
And the other is that in
Bertrand-like markets,

00:00:35.510 --> 00:00:38.270 align:middle line:84%
search costs can also
lead to price dispersion.

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:41.885 align:middle line:84%
In most of this class, we've
had a pattern of two lectures,

00:00:41.885 --> 00:00:43.760 align:middle line:84%
one where I do the
theory, and then one where

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:45.410 align:middle line:90%
I talk about the empirics.

00:00:45.410 --> 00:00:47.250 align:middle line:90%
On search, it's a big topic.

00:00:47.250 --> 00:00:48.450 align:middle line:90%
We decided to do three.

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:53.630 align:middle line:90%
So I did the theory last time.

00:00:53.630 --> 00:00:56.030 align:middle line:84%
Today I'm going to
talk about reduced form

00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:57.938 align:middle line:84%
evidence on empirical
evidence on search,

00:00:57.938 --> 00:01:00.230 align:middle line:84%
and then we're going to have
a third lecture on search,

00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:02.772 align:middle line:84%
where Tobias is going to talk
about the more structural work.

00:01:02.772 --> 00:01:05.600 align:middle line:90%
And that will come later.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:10.160 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, empirical
work on search--

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:12.770 align:middle line:84%
again, why do we do
empirical work in IO?

00:01:12.770 --> 00:01:14.390 align:middle line:84%
We're often generally
trying to do

00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:17.140 align:middle line:84%
two different things, or maybe
a combination of two things.

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:19.620 align:middle line:84%
One is we're trying to get
evidence on the theories,

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:22.200 align:middle line:84%
whether they're
applicable, whether they're

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:24.930 align:middle line:84%
what might be
correct or incorrect,

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:26.650 align:middle line:90%
or all theories are correct.

00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:28.090 align:middle line:84%
But what is it
about the theories

00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:30.363 align:middle line:84%
we might want to modify to
have it fit applications?

00:01:30.363 --> 00:01:33.030 align:middle line:84%
And the other thing we're trying
to do is just take the theories

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:35.580 align:middle line:84%
and apply them to particular
empirical examples

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:37.950 align:middle line:84%
that we care about and estimate
parameters of interest,

00:01:37.950 --> 00:01:40.080 align:middle line:90%
how big or small effects are.

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:42.430 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to talk
about four papers today.

00:01:42.430 --> 00:01:44.790 align:middle line:84%
The first of these
is of the just

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:47.840 align:middle line:84%
gaining insights in
the theory literature.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:50.340 align:middle line:84%
Generally, when we're trying
to gain insights in the theory,

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:53.730 align:middle line:84%
we're often looking
at unusual situations

00:01:53.730 --> 00:01:56.413 align:middle line:84%
because we want to look at
markets that are particularly

00:01:56.413 --> 00:01:58.080 align:middle line:84%
simple, where the
theory is particularly

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:02.490 align:middle line:84%
stark to try to understand its
predictions, whether they apply.

00:02:02.490 --> 00:02:06.390 align:middle line:84%
Particularly, Alan in his
paper is talking about--

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:11.190 align:middle line:84%
trying to understand, do we see
the kinds of price dispersion

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:16.410 align:middle line:84%
predicted by Stahl-like models
of heterogeneous consumer

00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:17.170 align:middle line:90%
search?

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:22.080 align:middle line:84%
And is it true that frequent
violations we see of the quote,

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:25.200 align:middle line:84%
"law of one price" are because
of consumer search costs leading

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:26.610 align:middle line:90%
to heterogeneity?

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:28.920 align:middle line:84%
And so in this paper,
what Alan was trying to do

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:33.600 align:middle line:84%
is think of an application where
he could argue that the Bertrand

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:38.520 align:middle line:84%
assumptions fit very, very
well and examine in a model

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:40.900 align:middle line:84%
where Bertrand assumptions
fit very, very well,

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:42.390 align:middle line:90%
do we see price dispersion?

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:44.550 align:middle line:84%
And can we provide evidence
that price dispersion

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:46.917 align:middle line:84%
seems to be due to
consumer search costs?

00:02:46.917 --> 00:02:48.750 align:middle line:84%
You'll see that this
is a paper written when

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:51.420 align:middle line:90%
Alan was a PhD student at MIT.

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:54.810 align:middle line:84%
Very much it's a paper that
has sort of minimal data,

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:57.880 align:middle line:84%
self-collected that
anyone could write.

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:01.620 align:middle line:84%
So his idea was that we could
get evidence-- a good example

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:04.800 align:middle line:84%
to think about
Bertrand-like competition

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:06.832 align:middle line:90%
is prescription drugs.

00:03:06.832 --> 00:03:08.790 align:middle line:84%
One of the nice things
about prescription drugs

00:03:08.790 --> 00:03:12.090 align:middle line:84%
is that a bottle of
amoxicillin tablets bought

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:15.450 align:middle line:84%
from pharmacy A and a bottle
of amoxicillin tablets bought

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:18.570 align:middle line:84%
from pharmacy B should be
completely identical products.

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:19.970 align:middle line:84%
People should
not-- people should

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:22.940 align:middle line:84%
know that these things are
exactly the same product,

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:25.028 align:middle line:84%
that we're just buying
it from different stores.

00:03:25.028 --> 00:03:26.570 align:middle line:84%
So we could think
of people as having

00:03:26.570 --> 00:03:28.472 align:middle line:90%
Bertrand-like preferences.

00:03:28.472 --> 00:03:30.680 align:middle line:84%
One of the other nice things
about prescription drugs

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:33.300 align:middle line:84%
is you're always struggling
in empirical papers.

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:35.690 align:middle line:84%
How do I get the number
of degrees of freedom

00:03:35.690 --> 00:03:38.220 align:middle line:90%
to actually estimate something?

00:03:38.220 --> 00:03:39.890 align:middle line:84%
The great thing about
prescription drugs

00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:41.510 align:middle line:84%
is there are hundreds of
different prescription

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:43.790 align:middle line:84%
drugs, hundreds of different
prescription drugs given

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:46.640 align:middle line:84%
hundreds of different
observations.

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:49.820 align:middle line:84%
So what Alan did
for this paper is

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:52.910 align:middle line:84%
if you think about the
Northeastern United States,

00:03:52.910 --> 00:03:58.790 align:middle line:84%
so we're in Massachusetts,
which looks something like this.

00:03:58.790 --> 00:04:02.900 align:middle line:84%
You've got Connecticut here,
Rhode Island, New York State.

00:04:02.900 --> 00:04:08.990 align:middle line:84%
Alan noticed that New York in
the late 1990s had a law that

00:04:08.990 --> 00:04:14.870 align:middle line:84%
pharmacies had to post all of
their retail prices for the 200

00:04:14.870 --> 00:04:18.360 align:middle line:84%
most popular prescription
drugs on a poster that was

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:20.980 align:middle line:84%
in displayed in large
format in the stores.

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:24.150 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, what one could do
is go to a pharmacy in New York

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:27.360 align:middle line:84%
and very quickly find out
what prices they were charging

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:29.940 align:middle line:90%
for every different drug.

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:32.880 align:middle line:84%
In the 1990s, people did not
have phones in their pockets

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:35.340 align:middle line:90%
with high resolution cameras.

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:37.310 align:middle line:84%
So you could not go
there and just take

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:38.560 align:middle line:90%
a picture of the price photos.

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:42.450 align:middle line:84%
So what Alan did was go to New
York with a clipboard and just

00:04:42.450 --> 00:04:44.250 align:middle line:84%
copy down off
these price posters

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:46.930 align:middle line:84%
what prices the pharmacies were
charging for all the drugs.

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:51.150 align:middle line:84%
And in particular, he does this
in two small towns, Middletown

00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:52.960 align:middle line:90%
and Newburgh, New York.

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:54.540 align:middle line:84%
And these happen
to be located where

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:57.900 align:middle line:84%
if you drive from Boston
along the Mass Pike,

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:00.210 align:middle line:84%
and then get on 84, and
then go to New York,

00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:04.980 align:middle line:84%
these are two of the first towns
you come to in New York State.

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:07.020 align:middle line:84%
One thing that was
nice about them is he

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:09.930 align:middle line:84%
wanted to get all the
prices in a market.

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:12.910 align:middle line:84%
Middletown and Newburgh
are both small towns.

00:05:12.910 --> 00:05:15.630 align:middle line:84%
They have 10 or 12
pharmacies in them,

00:05:15.630 --> 00:05:18.820 align:middle line:84%
and then there are no other
pharmacies within a 15-minute

00:05:18.820 --> 00:05:21.460 align:middle line:90%
drive or 20-minute drive away.

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:23.200 align:middle line:84%
And so you could
think of anyone who

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:25.300 align:middle line:84%
lives in Middletown
as shopping--

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:27.795 align:middle line:84%
having the options to be
buying prescription drugs

00:05:27.795 --> 00:05:29.170 align:middle line:84%
all the pharmacies
in Middletown,

00:05:29.170 --> 00:05:32.060 align:middle line:84%
and in Newburgh, they can
buy the things in Newburgh.

00:05:32.060 --> 00:05:35.770 align:middle line:84%
And I think I've said
all these things.

00:05:35.770 --> 00:05:40.420 align:middle line:84%
Oh, I guess another thing about
how the world was different--

00:05:40.420 --> 00:05:42.820 align:middle line:84%
another thing about how the
world was different in late

00:05:42.820 --> 00:05:46.900 align:middle line:84%
1990s is in the late 1990s, most
people did not have prescription

00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:47.930 align:middle line:90%
drug insurance.

00:05:47.930 --> 00:05:51.670 align:middle line:84%
And so at the time, the prices
that the pharmacies charged

00:05:51.670 --> 00:05:54.130 align:middle line:84%
for a prescription drug
was what people-- customers

00:05:54.130 --> 00:05:55.310 align:middle line:90%
would be paying.

00:05:55.310 --> 00:05:57.130 align:middle line:84%
Customers would have
the same incentive

00:05:57.130 --> 00:06:00.123 align:middle line:84%
to shop for drugs they would
have for any other product.

00:06:00.123 --> 00:06:01.540 align:middle line:84%
Obviously, today,
many more people

00:06:01.540 --> 00:06:03.440 align:middle line:84%
have prescription
drug insurance.

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:05.990 align:middle line:84%
The prices at pharmacies would
not be what they're paying.

00:06:05.990 --> 00:06:09.340 align:middle line:84%
But at the time, this
thing had this advantage.

00:06:09.340 --> 00:06:13.070 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, here's an
example of the study design.

00:06:13.070 --> 00:06:16.220 align:middle line:84%
So you can see that-- this
is not the actual poster,

00:06:16.220 --> 00:06:19.290 align:middle line:84%
but there was a poster that
was very much like this.

00:06:19.290 --> 00:06:22.820 align:middle line:84%
I think it was roughly 150
drugs, but then many of them

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:25.560 align:middle line:84%
had generic as well
as branded versions.

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:29.077 align:middle line:84%
And so you were getting
over 200 prescription drugs.

00:06:29.077 --> 00:06:30.660 align:middle line:84%
And so there would
be this big poster.

00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:33.650 align:middle line:84%
It would list Accupril, 10
milligram tablets, Accupril, 20

00:06:33.650 --> 00:06:36.290 align:middle line:90%
milligram tablets, and so on.

00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:38.130 align:middle line:84%
And then it would
have the prices there.

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:40.460 align:middle line:84%
And so Alan could just
go into the pharmacies,

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:42.890 align:middle line:84%
copy down the prices
listed on this poster,

00:06:42.890 --> 00:06:46.160 align:middle line:84%
and get an example
of what they cost.

00:06:46.160 --> 00:06:48.885 align:middle line:84%
And different drugs have
different characteristics.

00:06:48.885 --> 00:06:50.510 align:middle line:84%
And so what he's
going to be able to do

00:06:50.510 --> 00:06:53.510 align:middle line:84%
is not only say how
much dispersion is there

00:06:53.510 --> 00:06:57.560 align:middle line:84%
within a drug, but then how
much dispersion is there

00:06:57.560 --> 00:06:58.818 align:middle line:90%
across drugs?

00:06:58.818 --> 00:07:00.860 align:middle line:84%
Sorry-- how does the
dispersion vary across drugs

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:03.170 align:middle line:90%
with drug characteristics?

00:07:03.170 --> 00:07:05.690 align:middle line:84%
One thing that's also nice
about this application is

00:07:05.690 --> 00:07:08.450 align:middle line:84%
you could say one reason
why drug prices should

00:07:08.450 --> 00:07:10.370 align:middle line:84%
be different across
pharmacies is

00:07:10.370 --> 00:07:14.010 align:middle line:84%
because their drugs are bundled
with pharmacy amenities.

00:07:14.010 --> 00:07:16.460 align:middle line:84%
Some pharmacy has
better customer service,

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:19.500 align:middle line:84%
is cleaner, and might be
quicker to get in and out of.

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:22.050 align:middle line:84%
People would pay extra
to go to that pharmacy.

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:26.670 align:middle line:84%
But the idea is having
200 different drugs,

00:07:26.670 --> 00:07:29.030 align:middle line:84%
you can put in
pharmacy fixed effects,

00:07:29.030 --> 00:07:31.970 align:middle line:84%
and say, the pharmacy
may be nicer,

00:07:31.970 --> 00:07:34.760 align:middle line:84%
but there's not something like,
this pharmacy is a nicer place

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:37.010 align:middle line:84%
to get amoxicillin, and that
pharmacy is a nicer place

00:07:37.010 --> 00:07:38.805 align:middle line:90%
to get cephalexin.

00:07:38.805 --> 00:07:40.430 align:middle line:84%
Pharmacy fixed effects
ought to account

00:07:40.430 --> 00:07:42.805 align:middle line:84%
for most of the amenities,
and lets him control for that.

00:07:42.805 --> 00:07:46.003 align:middle line:90%


00:07:46.003 --> 00:07:47.670 align:middle line:84%
Not much sophistication
in the analysis,

00:07:47.670 --> 00:07:49.270 align:middle line:84%
so I won't go through
how it's really done.

00:07:49.270 --> 00:07:51.395 align:middle line:84%
But let me just talk about
some of the basic facts.

00:07:51.395 --> 00:07:55.560 align:middle line:84%
So first basic fact, there's
substantial price dispersion.

00:07:55.560 --> 00:07:57.990 align:middle line:84%
The difference between the
highest and lowest price

00:07:57.990 --> 00:07:59.970 align:middle line:84%
for drug across the
drugstores in a town

00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:03.060 align:middle line:90%
is $13 for the average drug.

00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:05.320 align:middle line:84%
Some drugs have less
price dispersion.

00:08:05.320 --> 00:08:07.890 align:middle line:90%
10th percentile range is $5.

00:08:07.890 --> 00:08:11.560 align:middle line:90%
95th percentile range is $25.

00:08:11.560 --> 00:08:15.270 align:middle line:84%
So substantial price dispersion,
obviously, it's not enormous,

00:08:15.270 --> 00:08:20.400 align:middle line:84%
but $13 is about what
you can think of.

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:23.510 align:middle line:84%
The second finding is
that the price dispersion

00:08:23.510 --> 00:08:27.320 align:middle line:84%
doesn't appear to be due
to pharmacy fixed effects.

00:08:27.320 --> 00:08:29.490 align:middle line:84%
He argues this in
a couple of ways.

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:32.539 align:middle line:84%
First is just this
simple table where

00:08:32.539 --> 00:08:35.669 align:middle line:84%
he puts for every
pharmacy in these towns,

00:08:35.669 --> 00:08:38.090 align:middle line:84%
how often is their price
in the bottom third

00:08:38.090 --> 00:08:39.000 align:middle line:90%
of the distribution?

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:40.559 align:middle line:84%
Here in Middletown,
there are 10.

00:08:40.559 --> 00:08:45.030 align:middle line:84%
So how often is their price
one of the three lowest?

00:08:45.030 --> 00:08:46.590 align:middle line:90%
How often is it in the middle?

00:08:46.590 --> 00:08:48.515 align:middle line:84%
How often is it in
the three highest?

00:08:48.515 --> 00:08:50.390 align:middle line:84%
And for many of the
pharmacies, like the ones

00:08:50.390 --> 00:08:52.730 align:middle line:84%
I've highlighted, Eckerd,
Immediate, and K-Mart,

00:08:52.730 --> 00:08:54.397 align:middle line:84%
there's a substantial
number of drugs

00:08:54.397 --> 00:08:56.480 align:middle line:84%
where they're one of the
lowest priced pharmacies,

00:08:56.480 --> 00:08:58.022 align:middle line:84%
a substantial number
of drugs for one

00:08:58.022 --> 00:08:59.600 align:middle line:84%
of the highest
priced pharmacies.

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:01.710 align:middle line:90%
There are a couple exceptions.

00:09:01.710 --> 00:09:03.500 align:middle line:84%
Rite Aid seems to
be a terrible place

00:09:03.500 --> 00:09:05.390 align:middle line:84%
to buy drugs if you
live in Middletown.

00:09:05.390 --> 00:09:08.330 align:middle line:84%
It's almost always one of
the highest three prices--

00:09:08.330 --> 00:09:11.720 align:middle line:84%
also, almost one of the
highest prices in Newburgh.

00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:13.820 align:middle line:84%
Price Chopper and
Walmart seem to have

00:09:13.820 --> 00:09:16.330 align:middle line:90%
lower prices than the others.

00:09:16.330 --> 00:09:21.580 align:middle line:84%
But if you think about how
important are the pharmacy fixed

00:09:21.580 --> 00:09:23.920 align:middle line:84%
effects, he has a
simple table where

00:09:23.920 --> 00:09:29.890 align:middle line:84%
you regress the prices of drugs
in a pharmacy on drug fixed

00:09:29.890 --> 00:09:30.670 align:middle line:90%
effects.

00:09:30.670 --> 00:09:33.620 align:middle line:90%
That has an R squared of 0.907.

00:09:33.620 --> 00:09:35.890 align:middle line:84%
You include drug
and pharmacy effects

00:09:35.890 --> 00:09:38.040 align:middle line:90%
as an R squared of 0.938.

00:09:38.040 --> 00:09:41.770 align:middle line:84%
So what that's saying is
the pharmacy fixed effects

00:09:41.770 --> 00:09:44.410 align:middle line:84%
or the amenities are accounting
for about 1/3 of the price

00:09:44.410 --> 00:09:48.110 align:middle line:84%
dispersion, which leaves 2/3 of
the remaining price dispersion.

00:09:48.110 --> 00:09:51.370 align:middle line:84%
So 2/3 of the $13
appears to be pure,

00:09:51.370 --> 00:09:54.790 align:middle line:84%
idiosyncratic variation
across pharmacies,

00:09:54.790 --> 00:09:57.320 align:middle line:84%
not correlated with
pharmacy fixed effects.

00:09:57.320 --> 00:10:01.690 align:middle line:84%
And then Alan also
argues in the paper

00:10:01.690 --> 00:10:04.308 align:middle line:84%
even that may be an
underestimate of how much of it

00:10:04.308 --> 00:10:06.100 align:middle line:84%
is pharmacy fixed
effects, because he looks

00:10:06.100 --> 00:10:09.280 align:middle line:84%
at the expensive pharmacies,
like goes to the Rite Aids,

00:10:09.280 --> 00:10:11.260 align:middle line:90%
and goes to the Price Chopper.

00:10:11.260 --> 00:10:15.590 align:middle line:84%
And his informal evidence of,
how friendly is the pharmacist?

00:10:15.590 --> 00:10:16.580 align:middle line:90%
How clean is the place?

00:10:16.580 --> 00:10:17.870 align:middle line:90%
How long is the line?

00:10:17.870 --> 00:10:20.270 align:middle line:84%
He would rather go to Price
Chopper than go to Rite Aid.

00:10:20.270 --> 00:10:23.380 align:middle line:84%
So in some sense, this
high price at Rite Aid

00:10:23.380 --> 00:10:26.380 align:middle line:84%
may just be part also of
the idiosyncratic price

00:10:26.380 --> 00:10:30.980 align:middle line:84%
dispersion, not part of the
bundling with amenities.

00:10:30.980 --> 00:10:34.025 align:middle line:90%


00:10:34.025 --> 00:10:37.910 align:middle line:84%
The main thing that
Alan does to then argue

00:10:37.910 --> 00:10:40.910 align:middle line:84%
that this price dispersion
is related to search costs

00:10:40.910 --> 00:10:43.610 align:middle line:90%
is to sort of--

00:10:43.610 --> 00:10:46.110 align:middle line:84%
you want to think about-- well,
let's go back to the theory.

00:10:46.110 --> 00:10:51.320 align:middle line:84%
If you remember, what did I
teach about price dispersion?

00:10:51.320 --> 00:10:57.440 align:middle line:84%
If you have a market
and you have--

00:10:57.440 --> 00:10:59.600 align:middle line:84%
if we have very
low search costs,

00:10:59.600 --> 00:11:03.140 align:middle line:84%
and this is price equals
cost, what you would see

00:11:03.140 --> 00:11:05.450 align:middle line:84%
is with very low
search costs, we

00:11:05.450 --> 00:11:11.750 align:middle line:84%
get a distribution that's fairly
tightly distributed around--

00:11:11.750 --> 00:11:13.610 align:middle line:84%
we get a distribution
of prices that's

00:11:13.610 --> 00:11:18.740 align:middle line:84%
fairly tightly distributed
around costs, because if anyone

00:11:18.740 --> 00:11:20.750 align:middle line:84%
sees a high price, they're
going to be tempted

00:11:20.750 --> 00:11:22.520 align:middle line:90%
to go back and search again.

00:11:22.520 --> 00:11:26.510 align:middle line:84%
That gives us a search cost
distribution that's low.

00:11:26.510 --> 00:11:30.380 align:middle line:84%
Prices are close to
cost, and then not very

00:11:30.380 --> 00:11:32.422 align:middle line:84%
dispersed, because if
prices were very dispersed,

00:11:32.422 --> 00:11:34.963 align:middle line:84%
anyone seeing a high price would
just go search again and try

00:11:34.963 --> 00:11:35.770 align:middle line:90%
to get a low price.

00:11:35.770 --> 00:11:38.100 align:middle line:84%
So we get very little
price dispersion.

00:11:38.100 --> 00:11:43.350 align:middle line:84%
When we have high search
costs, what you get instead

00:11:43.350 --> 00:11:46.650 align:middle line:84%
is more dispersed
distribution that

00:11:46.650 --> 00:11:48.630 align:middle line:84%
may look something
like this, where

00:11:48.630 --> 00:11:50.610 align:middle line:90%
a few firms set low prices.

00:11:50.610 --> 00:11:52.900 align:middle line:90%
Many more firms set high prices.

00:11:52.900 --> 00:11:54.750 align:middle line:84%
Even the consumers who
see the high prices

00:11:54.750 --> 00:11:56.230 align:middle line:90%
don't want to search again.

00:11:56.230 --> 00:11:57.700 align:middle line:90%
So we get two different things.

00:11:57.700 --> 00:12:01.110 align:middle line:84%
We get a higher average price,
and we get more dispersion,

00:12:01.110 --> 00:12:02.580 align:middle line:84%
whereas here we
get a lower average

00:12:02.580 --> 00:12:05.250 align:middle line:90%
price and less dispersion.

00:12:05.250 --> 00:12:07.800 align:middle line:84%
So that's what Alan
would like to say, is,

00:12:07.800 --> 00:12:11.190 align:middle line:84%
can we observe drugs that have
lower search costs versus higher

00:12:11.190 --> 00:12:14.220 align:middle line:84%
search costs and find
out do the ones that

00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:18.485 align:middle line:84%
have lower search costs have
lower and less dispersed prices?

00:12:18.485 --> 00:12:22.400 align:middle line:90%


00:12:22.400 --> 00:12:24.290 align:middle line:90%
It's hard to think--

00:12:24.290 --> 00:12:26.630 align:middle line:84%
you can think of reasons
why some drugs may have

00:12:26.630 --> 00:12:27.870 align:middle line:90%
higher or lower search costs.

00:12:27.870 --> 00:12:31.250 align:middle line:84%
So for instance, drugs
that treat conditions

00:12:31.250 --> 00:12:34.970 align:middle line:84%
that are acute and very
painful or make it difficult

00:12:34.970 --> 00:12:38.120 align:middle line:84%
for you to shop could
have high search costs.

00:12:38.120 --> 00:12:40.970 align:middle line:84%
Drugs that treat sexually
transmitted diseases

00:12:40.970 --> 00:12:44.317 align:middle line:84%
might have high search costs
because you're embarrassed

00:12:44.317 --> 00:12:45.150 align:middle line:90%
about the condition.

00:12:45.150 --> 00:12:47.358 align:middle line:84%
You don't want to go tell
every pharmacy in the town,

00:12:47.358 --> 00:12:49.490 align:middle line:84%
call them up, and say,
what would your cost be

00:12:49.490 --> 00:12:51.680 align:middle line:90%
for buying this prescription?

00:12:51.680 --> 00:12:54.950 align:middle line:84%
But what Alan comes
up with is this idea

00:12:54.950 --> 00:12:56.610 align:middle line:90%
of frequently purchased drugs.

00:12:56.610 --> 00:12:59.030 align:middle line:84%
So you have some drugs,
like antibiotics,

00:12:59.030 --> 00:13:01.670 align:middle line:84%
that you're typically buying
and you're going to use once.

00:13:01.670 --> 00:13:05.985 align:middle line:84%
You have other drugs, like high
blood pressure medications.

00:13:05.985 --> 00:13:07.610 align:middle line:84%
High blood pressure
medications, almost

00:13:07.610 --> 00:13:10.340 align:middle line:84%
everyone who's taking them
is going to take them--

00:13:10.340 --> 00:13:12.900 align:middle line:84%
buy them every month for
the rest of their lives.

00:13:12.900 --> 00:13:14.930 align:middle line:84%
And so if you think
about shopping

00:13:14.930 --> 00:13:17.330 align:middle line:84%
for an antibiotic versus
shopping for high blood pressure

00:13:17.330 --> 00:13:19.973 align:middle line:84%
medication, it pays to shop
around for the high blood

00:13:19.973 --> 00:13:21.390 align:middle line:84%
pressure medication
because you're

00:13:21.390 --> 00:13:23.348 align:middle line:84%
going to be able to
amortize those search costs

00:13:23.348 --> 00:13:24.630 align:middle line:90%
over the rest of your life.

00:13:24.630 --> 00:13:26.190 align:middle line:84%
Whereas the
antibiotics, you're just

00:13:26.190 --> 00:13:28.010 align:middle line:90%
going to buy this thing once.

00:13:28.010 --> 00:13:29.650 align:middle line:84%
You're never going
to buy it again.

00:13:29.650 --> 00:13:31.860 align:middle line:84%
The benefit of
search is smaller.

00:13:31.860 --> 00:13:36.570 align:middle line:84%
So what Alan does is think
about frequently purchased drugs

00:13:36.570 --> 00:13:39.780 align:middle line:84%
as drugs that have
effectively lower search costs

00:13:39.780 --> 00:13:45.030 align:middle line:84%
and examine whether the range in
prices and the level of prices

00:13:45.030 --> 00:13:48.600 align:middle line:84%
are lower for drugs that are
purchased more frequently.

00:13:48.600 --> 00:13:50.800 align:middle line:90%
The range is what's done here.

00:13:50.800 --> 00:13:53.400 align:middle line:84%
So he has four
different models--

00:13:53.400 --> 00:13:56.370 align:middle line:84%
four different measures
of the range of prices,

00:13:56.370 --> 00:14:00.270 align:middle line:84%
the range, standard
deviation, the residual range

00:14:00.270 --> 00:14:01.890 align:middle line:84%
after controlling
for some pharmacy

00:14:01.890 --> 00:14:05.250 align:middle line:84%
fixed effects and other things,
residual standard deviation.

00:14:05.250 --> 00:14:08.460 align:middle line:84%
In all four cases, he shows that
the more frequently purchased

00:14:08.460 --> 00:14:12.330 align:middle line:84%
drugs do have less price
dispersion than the frequently

00:14:12.330 --> 00:14:15.330 align:middle line:90%
purchased drugs.

00:14:15.330 --> 00:14:17.380 align:middle line:84%
He also chose to have
a table for this.

00:14:17.380 --> 00:14:18.330 align:middle line:90%
I do not.

00:14:18.330 --> 00:14:22.800 align:middle line:84%
He also shows that the average
markups for these drugs

00:14:22.800 --> 00:14:25.620 align:middle line:84%
relative to published
wholesale prices

00:14:25.620 --> 00:14:28.300 align:middle line:84%
are lower for the
frequently purchased drugs.

00:14:28.300 --> 00:14:30.360 align:middle line:84%
And then a third
fact, as I said,

00:14:30.360 --> 00:14:33.240 align:middle line:84%
we expect for idiosyncratic
reasons, some drugs

00:14:33.240 --> 00:14:35.100 align:middle line:84%
to have lower or
higher search costs

00:14:35.100 --> 00:14:37.570 align:middle line:84%
apart from this sort
of purchase frequency.

00:14:37.570 --> 00:14:39.210 align:middle line:84%
And what he shows
is that drugs that

00:14:39.210 --> 00:14:41.460 align:middle line:84%
have unexpectedly
high average markups

00:14:41.460 --> 00:14:43.920 align:middle line:84%
also have unexpectedly
high dispersion,

00:14:43.920 --> 00:14:46.950 align:middle line:84%
suggesting that there's also
unobserved variation in search

00:14:46.950 --> 00:14:49.230 align:middle line:84%
costs, and that unobserved
variation in search costs

00:14:49.230 --> 00:14:53.280 align:middle line:84%
is driving some of the price
dispersion and price levels.

00:14:53.280 --> 00:14:57.190 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so I think
it's a very nice paper.

00:14:57.190 --> 00:14:59.100 align:middle line:84%
It's providing very
simple evidence

00:14:59.100 --> 00:15:01.410 align:middle line:84%
saying that these
theories we have,

00:15:01.410 --> 00:15:04.260 align:middle line:84%
like Stahl's model of
price dispersion, do

00:15:04.260 --> 00:15:06.030 align:middle line:84%
appear to be applicable,
and do appear

00:15:06.030 --> 00:15:09.793 align:middle line:84%
that the price dispersion
they predict is there.

00:15:09.793 --> 00:15:11.460 align:middle line:84%
And it appears that
the price dispersion

00:15:11.460 --> 00:15:12.575 align:middle line:90%
is search cost related.

00:15:12.575 --> 00:15:15.220 align:middle line:90%


00:15:15.220 --> 00:15:18.070 align:middle line:84%
Stango and Zinman are
on the opposite side

00:15:18.070 --> 00:15:20.890 align:middle line:84%
of the primary
second motivation,

00:15:20.890 --> 00:15:24.550 align:middle line:84%
trying to look at a question
that we really care about

00:15:24.550 --> 00:15:26.440 align:middle line:90%
and understand.

00:15:26.440 --> 00:15:29.260 align:middle line:84%
Does-- do models
of price dispersion

00:15:29.260 --> 00:15:31.030 align:middle line:84%
help us understand
what's going on?

00:15:31.030 --> 00:15:33.370 align:middle line:84%
And how big are the
magnitudes of price dispersion

00:15:33.370 --> 00:15:35.325 align:middle line:90%
in this application?

00:15:35.325 --> 00:15:37.690 align:middle line:84%
The particular thing
they're talking about

00:15:37.690 --> 00:15:40.150 align:middle line:90%
is credit card debt.

00:15:40.150 --> 00:15:44.230 align:middle line:84%
Credit card debt is
something that many people

00:15:44.230 --> 00:15:45.340 align:middle line:90%
feel is unfortunate.

00:15:45.340 --> 00:15:46.570 align:middle line:84%
There are many people
in the United States

00:15:46.570 --> 00:15:48.278 align:middle line:84%
who pay much more on
credit card interest

00:15:48.278 --> 00:15:52.330 align:middle line:84%
and have gotten themselves
into substantial trouble.

00:15:52.330 --> 00:15:57.490 align:middle line:84%
There was a set of papers in the
1990s set off by this AER paper

00:15:57.490 --> 00:16:01.340 align:middle line:84%
by Larry Ausubel raising what he
called the credit card puzzle.

00:16:01.340 --> 00:16:03.370 align:middle line:84%
And the question is, why
is it that credit card

00:16:03.370 --> 00:16:05.650 align:middle line:84%
interest rates
are so high, given

00:16:05.650 --> 00:16:08.650 align:middle line:84%
that there are a million
banks in the United States?

00:16:08.650 --> 00:16:12.400 align:middle line:84%
Why is it that this competition
between the huge number of banks

00:16:12.400 --> 00:16:15.660 align:middle line:84%
does not dissipate profits
in the credit card industry?

00:16:15.660 --> 00:16:18.600 align:middle line:84%
And how do we get profits that
are so high for the firms?

00:16:18.600 --> 00:16:20.660 align:middle line:84%
The other thing
that Ausubel raised

00:16:20.660 --> 00:16:24.590 align:middle line:84%
was actually
non-dispersion intuition,

00:16:24.590 --> 00:16:27.290 align:middle line:84%
which is that Ausubel reported
that not only are credit card

00:16:27.290 --> 00:16:29.493 align:middle line:84%
rates very high,
but every big bank

00:16:29.493 --> 00:16:31.410 align:middle line:84%
charges very high rates
on their credit cards.

00:16:31.410 --> 00:16:33.262 align:middle line:90%
So there's not price dispersion.

00:16:33.262 --> 00:16:34.970 align:middle line:84%
He would have thought--
he'd sort of say,

00:16:34.970 --> 00:16:38.120 align:middle line:84%
why is it that all these banks
are charging very high interest

00:16:38.120 --> 00:16:38.850 align:middle line:90%
rates?

00:16:38.850 --> 00:16:40.880 align:middle line:84%
Why doesn't one of them
undercut the others

00:16:40.880 --> 00:16:44.960 align:middle line:84%
and try to compete with them,
bring interest rates down?

00:16:44.960 --> 00:16:46.880 align:middle line:84%
Ausubel also reported
that rates were fairly

00:16:46.880 --> 00:16:48.170 align:middle line:90%
insensitive to interest rates.

00:16:48.170 --> 00:16:50.270 align:middle line:90%
So his data covers times when--

00:16:50.270 --> 00:16:54.260 align:middle line:84%
there are times when interest
rates on mortgage loans

00:16:54.260 --> 00:16:56.940 align:middle line:84%
are 10%, times when
those rates are 2%.

00:16:56.940 --> 00:16:59.750 align:middle line:84%
When interest rates on
Treasury bills and mortgages

00:16:59.750 --> 00:17:02.619 align:middle line:84%
are changing, credit card rates
seem to be fairly insensitive.

00:17:02.619 --> 00:17:04.369 align:middle line:84%
And there was another
puzzle he raised is,

00:17:04.369 --> 00:17:06.150 align:middle line:90%
why is it these rates are not--

00:17:06.150 --> 00:17:08.970 align:middle line:84%
why is it they're so fixed, and
there's so little dispersion?

00:17:08.970 --> 00:17:13.589 align:middle line:84%
They don't vary over
time or across banks.

00:17:13.589 --> 00:17:16.170 align:middle line:84%
So Stango and
Zinman are actually

00:17:16.170 --> 00:17:19.530 align:middle line:84%
going to argue that the
basic facts that Ausubel

00:17:19.530 --> 00:17:22.500 align:middle line:90%
had us think about were wrong.

00:17:22.500 --> 00:17:24.359 align:middle line:90%
It's not the case--

00:17:24.359 --> 00:17:28.980 align:middle line:84%
it is true that rates across
banks tend to be fairly similar,

00:17:28.980 --> 00:17:31.680 align:middle line:84%
but what's not true is that
rates across individuals

00:17:31.680 --> 00:17:32.350 align:middle line:90%
are true.

00:17:32.350 --> 00:17:36.270 align:middle line:84%
And so there's this hidden
thing that Ausubel was missing,

00:17:36.270 --> 00:17:38.197 align:middle line:84%
which was that
all of these banks

00:17:38.197 --> 00:17:40.530 align:middle line:84%
have high average interest
rates, but all of these banks

00:17:40.530 --> 00:17:43.020 align:middle line:84%
are also offering many
different interest rates

00:17:43.020 --> 00:17:44.303 align:middle line:90%
to different clients.

00:17:44.303 --> 00:17:46.470 align:middle line:84%
Some of their clients are
paying low interest rates,

00:17:46.470 --> 00:17:48.940 align:middle line:84%
some of their clients are
paying high interest rates.

00:17:48.940 --> 00:17:51.420 align:middle line:84%
And so what Stango and Zinman
are trying to think about

00:17:51.420 --> 00:17:56.550 align:middle line:84%
is document that there's
substantial price dispersion,

00:17:56.550 --> 00:18:01.300 align:middle line:84%
and think about search costs as
a potential explanation for why

00:18:01.300 --> 00:18:03.940 align:middle line:84%
it is that we see these
high interest rates.

00:18:03.940 --> 00:18:06.940 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, some basic
facts in their paper, and I

00:18:06.940 --> 00:18:08.990 align:middle line:84%
should say, where does
their data come from?

00:18:08.990 --> 00:18:11.800 align:middle line:84%
So this is not the sort
of amateur data collection

00:18:11.800 --> 00:18:13.600 align:middle line:90%
we see in Sorensen's paper.

00:18:13.600 --> 00:18:16.510 align:middle line:84%
This is the data collection
where you make connections

00:18:16.510 --> 00:18:20.570 align:middle line:84%
with some firm that has access
to very high quality data.

00:18:20.570 --> 00:18:23.200 align:middle line:84%
So in particular, there's a
company called Lightspeed.

00:18:23.200 --> 00:18:25.190 align:middle line:84%
And there are a few
companies like this.

00:18:25.190 --> 00:18:32.350 align:middle line:84%
What Lightspeed does is it has a
panel of consumers who basically

00:18:32.350 --> 00:18:34.390 align:middle line:84%
open their lives to
Lightspeed in exchange

00:18:34.390 --> 00:18:35.780 align:middle line:90%
for some type of payment.

00:18:35.780 --> 00:18:40.970 align:middle line:84%
So in particular, there are
4,312 customers in their data.

00:18:40.970 --> 00:18:44.500 align:middle line:84%
What these customers do is
they give Lightspeed access

00:18:44.500 --> 00:18:46.190 align:middle line:90%
to their credit card statements.

00:18:46.190 --> 00:18:50.500 align:middle line:84%
So every month, Lightspeed
gets their complete credit card

00:18:50.500 --> 00:18:53.140 align:middle line:84%
statement, everything
they bought,

00:18:53.140 --> 00:18:57.170 align:middle line:84%
how much, what they paid,
how much interest they paid.

00:18:57.170 --> 00:18:58.275 align:middle line:90%
Did they pay a late fee?

00:18:58.275 --> 00:19:00.650 align:middle line:84%
It's basically like they've
got the credit card statement

00:19:00.650 --> 00:19:04.010 align:middle line:90%
for 4,312 people.

00:19:04.010 --> 00:19:07.500 align:middle line:84%
They also have information
on the people in their panel.

00:19:07.500 --> 00:19:10.555 align:middle line:84%
So when people join
their panel, they

00:19:10.555 --> 00:19:11.930 align:middle line:84%
complete a survey
that gives them

00:19:11.930 --> 00:19:13.980 align:middle line:84%
a lot of demographic
information.

00:19:13.980 --> 00:19:16.402 align:middle line:84%
They also know their
consumers' credit scores.

00:19:16.402 --> 00:19:17.360 align:middle line:90%
They've got this from--

00:19:17.360 --> 00:19:21.470 align:middle line:84%
I don't know-- Experian or some
other credit reporting agency.

00:19:21.470 --> 00:19:28.450 align:middle line:84%
So they have some measure of
the credit worthiness of people,

00:19:28.450 --> 00:19:30.950 align:middle line:84%
which you would think would be
a driver of the heterogeneity

00:19:30.950 --> 00:19:33.230 align:middle line:90%
in interest rates being paid.

00:19:33.230 --> 00:19:36.930 align:middle line:84%
So let me start from
some basic facts.

00:19:36.930 --> 00:19:40.520 align:middle line:84%
The first fact is there's
tremendous heterogeneity

00:19:40.520 --> 00:19:43.610 align:middle line:84%
in interest rates
across consumers.

00:19:43.610 --> 00:19:45.410 align:middle line:84%
If you look at the
interquartile range,

00:19:45.410 --> 00:19:49.100 align:middle line:84%
just between the 75th percentile
and the 25th percentile,

00:19:49.100 --> 00:19:53.300 align:middle line:84%
it's 800 basis points, or 8
percentage points per year.

00:19:53.300 --> 00:19:55.520 align:middle line:84%
This is omitting anyone
who has teaser rates

00:19:55.520 --> 00:19:56.490 align:middle line:90%
and who pays in full.

00:19:56.490 --> 00:19:59.030 align:middle line:84%
So these are people who are
paying interest on their credit

00:19:59.030 --> 00:20:00.250 align:middle line:90%
cards every month.

00:20:00.250 --> 00:20:02.300 align:middle line:84%
There's just tremendous
heterogeneity there.

00:20:02.300 --> 00:20:04.942 align:middle line:90%


00:20:04.942 --> 00:20:07.400 align:middle line:84%
Of course, there's going to be
heterogeneity in credit card

00:20:07.400 --> 00:20:09.200 align:middle line:84%
rates, because some people
are bad credit risks.

00:20:09.200 --> 00:20:10.650 align:middle line:84%
Some people are
good credit risks.

00:20:10.650 --> 00:20:12.440 align:middle line:84%
You would think if you were
running a credit card company,

00:20:12.440 --> 00:20:13.670 align:middle line:84%
you would give
lower interest rates

00:20:13.670 --> 00:20:15.440 align:middle line:84%
to people who are
going to pay you back,

00:20:15.440 --> 00:20:17.107 align:middle line:84%
a higher interest
rate to the people who

00:20:17.107 --> 00:20:18.530 align:middle line:90%
you think might default.

00:20:18.530 --> 00:20:22.220 align:middle line:84%
What they find is that default
risk explains about 40%

00:20:22.220 --> 00:20:25.100 align:middle line:90%
of the interest rate variation.

00:20:25.100 --> 00:20:28.220 align:middle line:84%
Other factors, like
offsetting rewards,

00:20:28.220 --> 00:20:30.990 align:middle line:84%
demographics, explain
very, very little.

00:20:30.990 --> 00:20:36.500 align:middle line:84%
So roughly 60% of this
gap is due to what

00:20:36.500 --> 00:20:38.670 align:middle line:84%
appears to be pure,
idiosyncratic heterogeneity.

00:20:38.670 --> 00:20:40.520 align:middle line:84%
People who are similarly
situated, equally

00:20:40.520 --> 00:20:42.350 align:middle line:84%
likely to pay the
bank back, some

00:20:42.350 --> 00:20:44.690 align:middle line:84%
are paying much
more than others.

00:20:44.690 --> 00:20:46.820 align:middle line:84%
And a third fact
I'll talk about later

00:20:46.820 --> 00:20:50.060 align:middle line:84%
is that there's also substantial
within consumer variation

00:20:50.060 --> 00:20:51.000 align:middle line:90%
offers received.

00:20:51.000 --> 00:20:52.790 align:middle line:84%
They have a separate
second data set

00:20:52.790 --> 00:20:55.070 align:middle line:90%
where they have people open--

00:20:55.070 --> 00:20:56.750 align:middle line:84%
they know all of the
credit card mailers

00:20:56.750 --> 00:20:59.810 align:middle line:84%
that are going to
particular individuals.

00:20:59.810 --> 00:21:01.040 align:middle line:90%
People in their data--

00:21:01.040 --> 00:21:02.900 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if this
applies to you--

00:21:02.900 --> 00:21:05.560 align:middle line:84%
get multiple credit
card offers per month.

00:21:05.560 --> 00:21:08.242 align:middle line:84%
They look within one month what
credit card offers people get.

00:21:08.242 --> 00:21:10.200 align:middle line:84%
And there's substantial
variation in the offers

00:21:10.200 --> 00:21:11.910 align:middle line:90%
if you open them up.

00:21:11.910 --> 00:21:13.430 align:middle line:90%
So let me just sort of--

00:21:13.430 --> 00:21:15.420 align:middle line:84%
some summary
statistics, and this

00:21:15.420 --> 00:21:18.690 align:middle line:84%
is a case where I think part
of the interest in this paper

00:21:18.690 --> 00:21:22.890 align:middle line:84%
is this Lightspeed data
gives us this view of what

00:21:22.890 --> 00:21:24.360 align:middle line:90%
is it that consumers are doing.

00:21:24.360 --> 00:21:26.670 align:middle line:84%
And you have sort of
shocking statistics

00:21:26.670 --> 00:21:28.060 align:middle line:90%
about problems people have.

00:21:28.060 --> 00:21:34.800 align:middle line:84%
So they divide the data into the
quartiles of the average balance

00:21:34.800 --> 00:21:38.160 align:middle line:84%
that people have on
their credit card.

00:21:38.160 --> 00:21:40.500 align:middle line:84%
You were probably told
that it's good practice

00:21:40.500 --> 00:21:43.560 align:middle line:84%
to pay off your credit
card bills every month,

00:21:43.560 --> 00:21:46.160 align:middle line:90%
and simply not pay any interest.

00:21:46.160 --> 00:21:51.960 align:middle line:84%
And what they find is that the
25th percentile balance is $499,

00:21:51.960 --> 00:21:55.590 align:middle line:84%
which means the fraction of
people in their data who pay off

00:21:55.590 --> 00:21:59.860 align:middle line:84%
their bill every month is less
than 25% So it's close to 25%,

00:21:59.860 --> 00:22:02.850 align:middle line:84%
but most people have a
balance on their credit cards,

00:22:02.850 --> 00:22:05.790 align:middle line:84%
and run a balance on their
credit cards every month

00:22:05.790 --> 00:22:07.920 align:middle line:90%
and never pay it off.

00:22:07.920 --> 00:22:09.820 align:middle line:90%
What's the upper quartile?

00:22:09.820 --> 00:22:12.390 align:middle line:84%
The upper quartile is people
with credit card balances

00:22:12.390 --> 00:22:17.010 align:middle line:90%
between $4,586 and $62,000.

00:22:17.010 --> 00:22:18.600 align:middle line:84%
People in this
quartile, on average,

00:22:18.600 --> 00:22:22.440 align:middle line:84%
have a revolving balance of
$11,000 on their credit cards.

00:22:22.440 --> 00:22:25.300 align:middle line:84%
They're paying
$2,000 in interest.

00:22:25.300 --> 00:22:28.230 align:middle line:84%
And obviously, these are the
people that people really

00:22:28.230 --> 00:22:31.778 align:middle line:84%
worry about and people who are
making big financial mistakes.

00:22:31.778 --> 00:22:34.320 align:middle line:84%
And is there something we can
do to help people avoid getting

00:22:34.320 --> 00:22:38.970 align:middle line:84%
stuck in this situation where
you're spending $2,000 a month

00:22:38.970 --> 00:22:39.820 align:middle line:90%
on interest?

00:22:39.820 --> 00:22:42.510 align:middle line:84%
You may also notice that the
people who are spending $2,000

00:22:42.510 --> 00:22:45.180 align:middle line:84%
a month in interest,
what's their income?

00:22:45.180 --> 00:22:48.280 align:middle line:84%
A quarter of them have
incomes below $45,000.

00:22:48.280 --> 00:22:52.290 align:middle line:84%
So being income below
$45,000, paying $2,000 a month

00:22:52.290 --> 00:22:55.200 align:middle line:84%
on your credit card, these are
people who've made very big

00:22:55.200 --> 00:22:55.950 align:middle line:90%
mistakes.

00:22:55.950 --> 00:22:58.740 align:middle line:84%
Some of them have incomes
of above $100,000,

00:22:58.740 --> 00:23:02.100 align:middle line:90%
but it's still substantial.

00:23:02.100 --> 00:23:04.980 align:middle line:84%
Also, noteworthy
is in some sense,

00:23:04.980 --> 00:23:07.270 align:middle line:84%
some of the U-shaped
relationships here.

00:23:07.270 --> 00:23:10.020 align:middle line:84%
It's actually people
in the bottom quartile

00:23:10.020 --> 00:23:14.070 align:middle line:84%
and people in the top quartile
who have the best credit scores

00:23:14.070 --> 00:23:16.620 align:middle line:84%
and who are charging
the most every month.

00:23:16.620 --> 00:23:18.720 align:middle line:84%
Some people in the
second quartile--

00:23:18.720 --> 00:23:21.240 align:middle line:84%
people in second quartile
are spending less per month,

00:23:21.240 --> 00:23:23.898 align:middle line:84%
have lower credit reports,
lower credit scores.

00:23:23.898 --> 00:23:25.440 align:middle line:84%
These people are
probably constrained

00:23:25.440 --> 00:23:26.942 align:middle line:90%
in how much they can borrow.

00:23:26.942 --> 00:23:28.650 align:middle line:84%
But it's the people
at the top, actually,

00:23:28.650 --> 00:23:31.980 align:middle line:84%
have fairly good credit scores,
and the credit card companies

00:23:31.980 --> 00:23:38.240 align:middle line:84%
enjoy being able to charge them
$2,000 a month in interest.

00:23:38.240 --> 00:23:40.730 align:middle line:84%
So this fact I told
you about, there's

00:23:40.730 --> 00:23:43.450 align:middle line:84%
tremendous dispersion in
credit card interest rates.

00:23:43.450 --> 00:23:46.370 align:middle line:90%


00:23:46.370 --> 00:23:51.570 align:middle line:84%
Here's the basic raw information
on what people are paying.

00:23:51.570 --> 00:23:56.070 align:middle line:84%
And you can see it's not based
on your revolving balance.

00:23:56.070 --> 00:23:57.830 align:middle line:84%
So people in the
lowest quartile,

00:23:57.830 --> 00:23:59.450 align:middle line:84%
there are people
paying between 12%--

00:23:59.450 --> 00:24:01.888 align:middle line:84%
there are people paying
12%, and people paying 26%.

00:24:01.888 --> 00:24:03.180 align:middle line:90%
So this is the 10th percentile.

00:24:03.180 --> 00:24:04.680 align:middle line:90%
This is the 90th percentile.

00:24:04.680 --> 00:24:07.220 align:middle line:84%
People in the upper quartile
are people paying 11%,

00:24:07.220 --> 00:24:09.150 align:middle line:90%
people paying 26%.

00:24:09.150 --> 00:24:14.270 align:middle line:84%
It seems to be very similar
across those four groups.

00:24:14.270 --> 00:24:16.640 align:middle line:84%
For every level of
revolving balance,

00:24:16.640 --> 00:24:20.150 align:middle line:84%
there are people paying very low
interest rates, people paying

00:24:20.150 --> 00:24:21.670 align:middle line:90%
very, very high interest rates.

00:24:21.670 --> 00:24:26.290 align:middle line:90%


00:24:26.290 --> 00:24:27.790 align:middle line:84%
As I said, the
second fact they have

00:24:27.790 --> 00:24:30.160 align:middle line:84%
is, are people paying very
different interest rates

00:24:30.160 --> 00:24:32.680 align:middle line:84%
because they have very
different credit scores?

00:24:32.680 --> 00:24:36.400 align:middle line:84%
We see here is the
blue dashed line

00:24:36.400 --> 00:24:39.530 align:middle line:84%
is the distribution
of interest rates

00:24:39.530 --> 00:24:42.710 align:middle line:84%
people are being paid
relative to the average rate.

00:24:42.710 --> 00:24:44.000 align:middle line:90%
So 0 is here.

00:24:44.000 --> 00:24:47.470 align:middle line:84%
So there are a number of
people paying interest rates 5%

00:24:47.470 --> 00:24:48.410 align:middle line:90%
below average.

00:24:48.410 --> 00:24:50.800 align:middle line:84%
There are a number of people
paying 10% below average.

00:24:50.800 --> 00:24:54.250 align:middle line:84%
Also, many people paying
0 to 10% above average.

00:24:54.250 --> 00:24:56.930 align:middle line:84%
Not many people pay more than
10% or 12% above average.

00:24:56.930 --> 00:24:59.810 align:middle line:84%
But the blue is a
distribution of interest rates

00:24:59.810 --> 00:25:03.220 align:middle line:84%
people are paying
relative to the average.

00:25:03.220 --> 00:25:05.920 align:middle line:84%
The red is what
you get if you say,

00:25:05.920 --> 00:25:07.960 align:middle line:84%
now I'm going to control
for your credit score

00:25:07.960 --> 00:25:10.558 align:middle line:84%
and ask, once I've controlled
for your credit score,

00:25:10.558 --> 00:25:12.850 align:middle line:84%
what are you paying relative
to the average for someone

00:25:12.850 --> 00:25:15.770 align:middle line:84%
with your credit score
and other attributes?

00:25:15.770 --> 00:25:20.780 align:middle line:84%
And what they find is that this
explains some of the variation.

00:25:20.780 --> 00:25:24.500 align:middle line:84%
The red distribution
is more tightly

00:25:24.500 --> 00:25:27.140 align:middle line:84%
distributed than the
blue distribution,

00:25:27.140 --> 00:25:31.920 align:middle line:84%
but it's explaining perhaps
40% of the variation.

00:25:31.920 --> 00:25:34.940 align:middle line:84%
So some of these tails
go away, but we still

00:25:34.940 --> 00:25:38.300 align:middle line:84%
have very substantial
differences in interest rates

00:25:38.300 --> 00:25:42.800 align:middle line:84%
paid by people who appear to
be similar on all attributes.

00:25:42.800 --> 00:25:47.830 align:middle line:84%
So then the next thing the
paper does is try to say,

00:25:47.830 --> 00:25:51.560 align:middle line:84%
can we tie this to
search intensity?

00:25:51.560 --> 00:25:55.400 align:middle line:84%
And can we find that people
with lower search costs--

00:25:55.400 --> 00:25:58.790 align:middle line:84%
the people who are more likely
to search are the ones paying

00:25:58.790 --> 00:26:02.600 align:middle line:84%
interest rates down here,
and the people who are less--

00:26:02.600 --> 00:26:05.180 align:middle line:84%
have higher search costs end
up getting stuck and paying

00:26:05.180 --> 00:26:07.235 align:middle line:90%
high interest rates up there?

00:26:07.235 --> 00:26:09.980 align:middle line:90%


00:26:09.980 --> 00:26:12.800 align:middle line:84%
It's not as clean as
Alan's paper, where

00:26:12.800 --> 00:26:15.710 align:middle line:84%
Alan has this exogenous
characteristic of a drug that

00:26:15.710 --> 00:26:17.390 align:middle line:90%
shifts its search costs.

00:26:17.390 --> 00:26:19.550 align:middle line:84%
But what they have as a
measure of search costs

00:26:19.550 --> 00:26:23.060 align:middle line:84%
is just a self answer
to a survey question.

00:26:23.060 --> 00:26:27.812 align:middle line:84%
They ask consumers-- so
they not only have access

00:26:27.812 --> 00:26:29.020 align:middle line:90%
to the Lightspeed panel data.

00:26:29.020 --> 00:26:31.145 align:middle line:84%
They have the ability to
ask questions of consumers

00:26:31.145 --> 00:26:32.560 align:middle line:90%
in the Lightspeed panel data.

00:26:32.560 --> 00:26:34.565 align:middle line:84%
And they ask those
consumers, how

00:26:34.565 --> 00:26:36.190 align:middle line:84%
likely are you to
look at a credit card

00:26:36.190 --> 00:26:38.230 align:middle line:90%
offer you get in the mail?

00:26:38.230 --> 00:26:39.760 align:middle line:84%
And you might think
that that would

00:26:39.760 --> 00:26:41.000 align:middle line:90%
be a measure of search costs.

00:26:41.000 --> 00:26:43.180 align:middle line:84%
People who have low
search costs, people

00:26:43.180 --> 00:26:44.890 align:middle line:84%
who like opening
credit card envelopes,

00:26:44.890 --> 00:26:46.802 align:middle line:84%
and look, thinking
about them are

00:26:46.802 --> 00:26:48.010 align:middle line:90%
people with low search costs.

00:26:48.010 --> 00:26:50.657 align:middle line:84%
People who dislike
opening their credit card

00:26:50.657 --> 00:26:53.240 align:middle line:84%
offers they get in the mail are
people with high search costs.

00:26:53.240 --> 00:26:54.792 align:middle line:90%
Is that going to explain things?

00:26:54.792 --> 00:26:57.250 align:middle line:84%
When you think about that,
there's this obvious endogeneity

00:26:57.250 --> 00:26:57.750 align:middle line:90%
concern.

00:26:57.750 --> 00:27:01.090 align:middle line:84%
You ask people, how likely are
you to open a credit card offer

00:27:01.090 --> 00:27:02.260 align:middle line:90%
you get in the mail?

00:27:02.260 --> 00:27:04.810 align:middle line:84%
Well, if you know you
have a bad credit card

00:27:04.810 --> 00:27:06.460 align:middle line:84%
and are paying a
high interest rate,

00:27:06.460 --> 00:27:08.110 align:middle line:84%
you may be more
likely to say, yes,

00:27:08.110 --> 00:27:09.460 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to open up
things, because you're

00:27:09.460 --> 00:27:10.750 align:middle line:90%
looking for a new credit card.

00:27:10.750 --> 00:27:13.067 align:middle line:84%
Whereas if you already have
a really good credit card,

00:27:13.067 --> 00:27:14.650 align:middle line:84%
you may say, no, I
don't open them up.

00:27:14.650 --> 00:27:17.317 align:middle line:84%
And you're not opening them up,
not because you have high search

00:27:17.317 --> 00:27:20.650 align:middle line:84%
costs, but because you know you
already have a very good deal.

00:27:20.650 --> 00:27:22.045 align:middle line:90%
So what they do is--

00:27:22.045 --> 00:27:26.005 align:middle line:90%


00:27:26.005 --> 00:27:29.120 align:middle line:84%
the first thing they do is they
just run this OLS regression.

00:27:29.120 --> 00:27:31.580 align:middle line:84%
And they regress the
APR that you end up

00:27:31.580 --> 00:27:36.600 align:middle line:84%
paying on your self-reported
search intensity.

00:27:36.600 --> 00:27:38.780 align:middle line:84%
And we get a small negative
coefficient, saying,

00:27:38.780 --> 00:27:43.100 align:middle line:84%
people who search more actively
are paying slightly lower rates,

00:27:43.100 --> 00:27:44.890 align:middle line:90%
but then they sort of--

00:27:44.890 --> 00:27:49.250 align:middle line:84%
it has that endogeneity, so
they run this IV regression.

00:27:49.250 --> 00:27:51.890 align:middle line:84%
The IV regression
says that we can

00:27:51.890 --> 00:27:54.890 align:middle line:84%
think about gender and marital
status as instruments for search

00:27:54.890 --> 00:28:00.140 align:middle line:84%
intensity because the offers
that you get in the mail

00:28:00.140 --> 00:28:03.410 align:middle line:84%
should not differ across gender,
across marital status once you

00:28:03.410 --> 00:28:06.230 align:middle line:84%
control for credit
score and other factors,

00:28:06.230 --> 00:28:09.800 align:middle line:84%
because it's illegal to
discriminate against women

00:28:09.800 --> 00:28:12.650 align:middle line:84%
or against married
or unmarried people.

00:28:12.650 --> 00:28:16.670 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, those things should
relate to your search intensity,

00:28:16.670 --> 00:28:19.880 align:middle line:84%
but be unrelated to the
set of raw set of offers

00:28:19.880 --> 00:28:22.430 align:middle line:84%
that you get in the
mail for credit cards.

00:28:22.430 --> 00:28:25.260 align:middle line:84%
When they put that
IV in, now they

00:28:25.260 --> 00:28:28.170 align:middle line:84%
get a very large negative
coefficient, saying,

00:28:28.170 --> 00:28:31.530 align:middle line:84%
people who search
more intensively

00:28:31.530 --> 00:28:33.520 align:middle line:90%
are paying lower interest rates.

00:28:33.520 --> 00:28:38.280 align:middle line:84%
I mean, it's a very large gap
between the OLS and the IV

00:28:38.280 --> 00:28:38.920 align:middle line:90%
estimates.

00:28:38.920 --> 00:28:40.530 align:middle line:84%
And then the
standard error really

00:28:40.530 --> 00:28:41.890 align:middle line:90%
blows up relative to this.

00:28:41.890 --> 00:28:47.920 align:middle line:84%
So you wonder-_ again, it's only
a slightly significant result.

00:28:47.920 --> 00:28:48.810 align:middle line:90%
Why is that?

00:28:48.810 --> 00:28:52.450 align:middle line:84%
Is the reverse causality really
so large that it causes this?

00:28:52.450 --> 00:28:54.780 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, that's the
evidence they provide,

00:28:54.780 --> 00:28:57.390 align:middle line:84%
that it does seem
to be the dispersion

00:28:57.390 --> 00:28:59.250 align:middle line:84%
that people are
paying is related

00:28:59.250 --> 00:29:01.772 align:middle line:84%
to some characteristic that
does their search intensity.

00:29:01.772 --> 00:29:03.480 align:middle line:84%
So you'll see at the
end of this lecture,

00:29:03.480 --> 00:29:05.910 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to come back to
another paper that further

00:29:05.910 --> 00:29:07.620 align:middle line:84%
examines this
question, but I think

00:29:07.620 --> 00:29:11.310 align:middle line:84%
it does clearly reframe
the debate relative to what

00:29:11.310 --> 00:29:14.130 align:middle line:84%
you thought from Ausubel
and other early papers

00:29:14.130 --> 00:29:17.440 align:middle line:84%
to say that there is
just substantial price

00:29:17.440 --> 00:29:19.030 align:middle line:90%
dispersion in credit cards.

00:29:19.030 --> 00:29:21.160 align:middle line:84%
Search costs seem
to be a part of why

00:29:21.160 --> 00:29:22.810 align:middle line:84%
it is that it's
so hard for firms

00:29:22.810 --> 00:29:25.773 align:middle line:84%
to compete in this market is if
you send out lots of credit card

00:29:25.773 --> 00:29:27.190 align:middle line:84%
offers and people
don't open them,

00:29:27.190 --> 00:29:29.410 align:middle line:84%
people don't
consider buying them,

00:29:29.410 --> 00:29:32.110 align:middle line:84%
that may make it one of
the big factors that's

00:29:32.110 --> 00:29:34.980 align:middle line:84%
obstacles to eliminating the
high markups that we see.

00:29:34.980 --> 00:29:41.130 align:middle line:90%


00:29:41.130 --> 00:29:43.410 align:middle line:84%
So moving on, I'm going
to spend much longer

00:29:43.410 --> 00:29:45.510 align:middle line:90%
talking about a paper of mine.

00:29:45.510 --> 00:29:47.850 align:middle line:84%
I talk about it in part
because I know it better.

00:29:47.850 --> 00:29:50.730 align:middle line:84%
It's one of my
favorite of my papers,

00:29:50.730 --> 00:29:53.910 align:middle line:90%
or papers, mine with Sara.

00:29:53.910 --> 00:29:57.270 align:middle line:84%
This is a paper where
we're both trying to--

00:29:57.270 --> 00:29:59.460 align:middle line:90%
we are trying to--

00:29:59.460 --> 00:30:01.770 align:middle line:84%
it's a more talking
about the theory paper

00:30:01.770 --> 00:30:04.950 align:middle line:84%
where we are trying to
understand what causes

00:30:04.950 --> 00:30:07.680 align:middle line:90%
price dispersions and markups.

00:30:07.680 --> 00:30:09.720 align:middle line:84%
I see this-- we
saw it at the time

00:30:09.720 --> 00:30:12.480 align:middle line:84%
as a paper that was
looking forward.

00:30:12.480 --> 00:30:16.650 align:middle line:84%
The question was that in the
early 2000s, search engines,

00:30:16.650 --> 00:30:19.560 align:middle line:84%
price search engines had
recently been invented.

00:30:19.560 --> 00:30:23.040 align:middle line:84%
There were a few markets,
like airplane flights, hotels,

00:30:23.040 --> 00:30:27.270 align:middle line:84%
rental cars, where many people
use price search engines.

00:30:27.270 --> 00:30:31.270 align:middle line:84%
Most other markets, people don't
still use price search engines.

00:30:31.270 --> 00:30:34.830 align:middle line:84%
But our question was, what's
going to happen in the world

00:30:34.830 --> 00:30:38.040 align:middle line:84%
if price search engines
become much better?

00:30:38.040 --> 00:30:41.140 align:middle line:84%
First of all, how are the
airplane flights, and the hotel,

00:30:41.140 --> 00:30:43.550 align:middle line:84%
and rental car markets going
to adjust when flights get--

00:30:43.550 --> 00:30:45.772 align:middle line:84%
when search
technologies get better?

00:30:45.772 --> 00:30:47.980 align:middle line:84%
And what's going to happen
to the rest of the economy

00:30:47.980 --> 00:30:49.620 align:middle line:84%
when search
technologies get better?

00:30:49.620 --> 00:30:52.210 align:middle line:84%
I mean, I think somehow this
still seems topical 20 years

00:30:52.210 --> 00:30:57.340 align:middle line:84%
later, when we see these amazing
AI advances and chat robots,

00:30:57.340 --> 00:30:57.910 align:middle line:90%
and whatever.

00:30:57.910 --> 00:30:59.620 align:middle line:84%
You wonder, am I
going be able to--

00:30:59.620 --> 00:31:01.578 align:middle line:84%
are we going to get to
a point where I can just

00:31:01.578 --> 00:31:04.870 align:middle line:84%
type into Google, what's
the best place for me to buy

00:31:04.870 --> 00:31:05.770 align:middle line:90%
amoxicillin?

00:31:05.770 --> 00:31:09.020 align:middle line:84%
And have amox-- and have
Google say, you're living--

00:31:09.020 --> 00:31:11.180 align:middle line:84%
I can see that you live
in Newton, Massachusetts.

00:31:11.180 --> 00:31:13.510 align:middle line:84%
For you to buy amoxicillin,
the best pharmacy to go to

00:31:13.510 --> 00:31:16.762 align:middle line:84%
is this one that's
on your drive home,

00:31:16.762 --> 00:31:19.220 align:middle line:84%
and it costs this much there,
and this much somewhere else.

00:31:19.220 --> 00:31:21.490 align:middle line:84%
Or are we going to get to
the point where I can say,

00:31:21.490 --> 00:31:25.240 align:middle line:84%
I want to buy a tennis racket,
and then Google just tells me,

00:31:25.240 --> 00:31:26.950 align:middle line:84%
given what I know
about you, this

00:31:26.950 --> 00:31:28.460 align:middle line:84%
would be the best tennis
racket for you to buy.

00:31:28.460 --> 00:31:29.900 align:middle line:84%
And this store has
it at this price.

00:31:29.900 --> 00:31:32.608 align:middle line:84%
This is where you should buy it,
and just get it shipped directly

00:31:32.608 --> 00:31:33.220 align:middle line:90%
to your house.

00:31:33.220 --> 00:31:36.410 align:middle line:84%
So who knows whether we
will ever get to that point.

00:31:36.410 --> 00:31:38.500 align:middle line:84%
But the question, I
think, still remains,

00:31:38.500 --> 00:31:41.830 align:middle line:84%
how do we expect markets to
change when search costs--

00:31:41.830 --> 00:31:44.680 align:middle line:84%
when internet
technologies improve.

00:31:44.680 --> 00:31:46.840 align:middle line:90%
Search costs go down.

00:31:46.840 --> 00:31:51.040 align:middle line:84%
Are we going to see a collapse
in price dispersion, where

00:31:51.040 --> 00:31:55.970 align:middle line:84%
formerly high dispersed prices
become very low tight prices?

00:31:55.970 --> 00:32:00.400 align:middle line:84%
And we mentioned in the paper
the potential Bertrand paradox.

00:32:00.400 --> 00:32:03.460 align:middle line:84%
Real retailers have
substantial fixed costs.

00:32:03.460 --> 00:32:06.640 align:middle line:84%
The fixed costs of running a
store are something like 20%.

00:32:06.640 --> 00:32:10.420 align:middle line:84%
So if you can't earn 20% markups
over your wholesale costs

00:32:10.420 --> 00:32:12.500 align:middle line:84%
on goods, you're going
to go out of business.

00:32:12.500 --> 00:32:14.900 align:middle line:84%
Even if you're Walmart,
you're super efficient,

00:32:14.900 --> 00:32:17.470 align:middle line:84%
you need to have 20% markups
of your wholesale cost

00:32:17.470 --> 00:32:20.770 align:middle line:84%
to cover all the fixed costs
of having a physical store,

00:32:20.770 --> 00:32:22.040 align:middle line:90%
and things like that.

00:32:22.040 --> 00:32:26.020 align:middle line:84%
And so the question is, how is
retail and other business going

00:32:26.020 --> 00:32:28.840 align:middle line:84%
to survive when
the internet makes

00:32:28.840 --> 00:32:30.073 align:middle line:90%
price search more efficient?

00:32:30.073 --> 00:32:32.740 align:middle line:84%
And I guess, one of the answers
we're going to say in this paper

00:32:32.740 --> 00:32:36.310 align:middle line:84%
is that, models of
search traditionally

00:32:36.310 --> 00:32:38.563 align:middle line:84%
thought of consumer search
as a one-sided problem,

00:32:38.563 --> 00:32:40.480 align:middle line:84%
where these consumers
have these search costs.

00:32:40.480 --> 00:32:42.370 align:middle line:90%
The search costs are fixed.

00:32:42.370 --> 00:32:44.910 align:middle line:84%
And then they search, and
that fixed search cost

00:32:44.910 --> 00:32:46.282 align:middle line:90%
determines the markups.

00:32:46.282 --> 00:32:48.740 align:middle line:84%
The thing we're going to think
about is actually two-sided.

00:32:48.740 --> 00:32:50.420 align:middle line:84%
You have the search
engine is trying

00:32:50.420 --> 00:32:52.420 align:middle line:84%
to make search more
efficient, and the consumers

00:32:52.420 --> 00:32:54.378 align:middle line:84%
are trying to make their
search more efficient.

00:32:54.378 --> 00:32:58.090 align:middle line:84%
And at the same time, the
stores are on the other side,

00:32:58.090 --> 00:32:59.780 align:middle line:84%
trying to make search
less efficient.

00:32:59.780 --> 00:33:03.792 align:middle line:84%
And so we can think of search
costs as not being exogenous,

00:33:03.792 --> 00:33:05.500 align:middle line:84%
or not being just a
result of technology,

00:33:05.500 --> 00:33:08.410 align:middle line:84%
but being an outcome of
a two-side-- equilibrium

00:33:08.410 --> 00:33:10.092 align:middle line:84%
of a two-sided game,
where some people

00:33:10.092 --> 00:33:11.800 align:middle line:84%
are trying to make
search more efficient,

00:33:11.800 --> 00:33:14.420 align:middle line:84%
other people are trying to
make search less efficient,

00:33:14.420 --> 00:33:16.300 align:middle line:84%
and this balance
between search and what

00:33:16.300 --> 00:33:19.540 align:middle line:84%
we call obfuscation ends
up determining price levels

00:33:19.540 --> 00:33:21.360 align:middle line:90%
and markets.

00:33:21.360 --> 00:33:24.720 align:middle line:84%
So as I said, this
is a paper where

00:33:24.720 --> 00:33:28.770 align:middle line:84%
we're focused on this theory
of, how would obfuscation work?

00:33:28.770 --> 00:33:32.550 align:middle line:84%
We go to a particular narrow
corner of the internet,

00:33:32.550 --> 00:33:34.793 align:middle line:84%
where everything
is much simpler,

00:33:34.793 --> 00:33:36.210 align:middle line:84%
and it makes it
much simpler to do

00:33:36.210 --> 00:33:39.730 align:middle line:84%
the analysis than it would if we
look at a more popular product.

00:33:39.730 --> 00:33:43.050 align:middle line:84%
And so in particular, we
look at a price search

00:33:43.050 --> 00:33:45.520 align:middle line:90%
engine called pricewatch.com.

00:33:45.520 --> 00:33:48.420 align:middle line:84%
I think it still exists,
although it's long

00:33:48.420 --> 00:33:49.300 align:middle line:90%
past its heyday.

00:33:49.300 --> 00:33:52.800 align:middle line:84%
But in the early 2000s,
Pricewatch was this simple

00:33:52.800 --> 00:33:58.620 align:middle line:84%
database search engine that
people who were sophisticated

00:33:58.620 --> 00:34:00.580 align:middle line:84%
would use to find
things that they needed.

00:34:00.580 --> 00:34:03.000 align:middle line:84%
So for instance, if you
were to go to the computer

00:34:03.000 --> 00:34:06.060 align:middle line:84%
guys in the MIT Economics
department and say,

00:34:06.060 --> 00:34:07.410 align:middle line:90%
I have a computer.

00:34:07.410 --> 00:34:09.449 align:middle line:90%
I'm getting low memory errors.

00:34:09.449 --> 00:34:13.350 align:middle line:84%
Could I just buy some extra
memory and plug it into the--

00:34:13.350 --> 00:34:16.020 align:middle line:84%
plug some extra memory modules
into this part of my circuit

00:34:16.020 --> 00:34:20.610 align:middle line:84%
board and increase the memory
capacity of my computer?

00:34:20.610 --> 00:34:24.310 align:middle line:84%
People who worked in IT offices
might use pricewatch.com

00:34:24.310 --> 00:34:27.100 align:middle line:84%
to find an inexpensive
place to buy memory that you

00:34:27.100 --> 00:34:29.710 align:middle line:90%
could add to your computer.

00:34:29.710 --> 00:34:30.670 align:middle line:90%
What did it look like?

00:34:30.670 --> 00:34:31.929 align:middle line:90%
And again, this is--

00:34:31.929 --> 00:34:32.900 align:middle line:90%
think 2000.

00:34:32.900 --> 00:34:34.960 align:middle line:84%
The world was a much
more primitive place.

00:34:34.960 --> 00:34:38.980 align:middle line:84%
People had lower bandwidth
on their home internet.

00:34:38.980 --> 00:34:41.330 align:middle line:84%
Things just had simple
text-based designs.

00:34:41.330 --> 00:34:42.889 align:middle line:90%
So this was Pricewatch--

00:34:42.889 --> 00:34:45.679 align:middle line:84%
very proud that it was
established in 1995.

00:34:45.679 --> 00:34:49.150 align:middle line:84%
And you can see the
pixelated graphics there.

00:34:49.150 --> 00:34:51.429 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, you'd go
to pricewatch.com.

00:34:51.429 --> 00:34:53.710 align:middle line:84%
And it would say,
these are the things

00:34:53.710 --> 00:34:54.949 align:middle line:90%
you can buy through our site.

00:34:54.949 --> 00:34:58.220 align:middle line:84%
You can buy computers, PCs with
or without an operating system.

00:34:58.220 --> 00:34:59.110 align:middle line:90%
You can buy CPUs.

00:34:59.110 --> 00:35:01.210 align:middle line:90%
You can buy motherboards.

00:35:01.210 --> 00:35:03.170 align:middle line:90%
We're going to be buying memory.

00:35:03.170 --> 00:35:05.140 align:middle line:84%
So RAM memory
versus flash cards,

00:35:05.140 --> 00:35:06.560 align:middle line:90%
we're going to be buying RAM.

00:35:06.560 --> 00:35:08.230 align:middle line:90%
So anyway, I go to this page.

00:35:08.230 --> 00:35:10.480 align:middle line:90%
I click on RAM.

00:35:10.480 --> 00:35:12.340 align:middle line:84%
When I click on
RAM, it brings up

00:35:12.340 --> 00:35:14.860 align:middle line:84%
this page, which shows me all
the different kinds of RAM

00:35:14.860 --> 00:35:16.300 align:middle line:90%
I can buy.

00:35:16.300 --> 00:35:18.730 align:middle line:84%
Again, you need to be
somewhat sophisticated.

00:35:18.730 --> 00:35:20.450 align:middle line:90%
Do you need DDR memory?

00:35:20.450 --> 00:35:23.350 align:middle line:84%
Do you need notebook
DDR2 memory?

00:35:23.350 --> 00:35:25.318 align:middle line:84%
These are all the memory
modules you can buy.

00:35:25.318 --> 00:35:27.110 align:middle line:84%
They're going to have
various descriptions.

00:35:27.110 --> 00:35:30.850 align:middle line:84%
So the 512 megabytes, 4
gigabytes, 2 gigabytes,

00:35:30.850 --> 00:35:33.890 align:middle line:84%
that's the storage capacity
of the memory module.

00:35:33.890 --> 00:35:40.300 align:middle line:84%
This 4000, 3500, 3200,
those are describing

00:35:40.300 --> 00:35:44.290 align:middle line:84%
the speed with which
the memory communicates

00:35:44.290 --> 00:35:45.760 align:middle line:90%
with the motherboard.

00:35:45.760 --> 00:35:50.080 align:middle line:84%
The DDR, DDR2, those are the
other aspect of the thing.

00:35:50.080 --> 00:35:53.680 align:middle line:84%
Basically, you need to
know what type of memory

00:35:53.680 --> 00:35:55.150 align:middle line:90%
you need for your computer.

00:35:55.150 --> 00:35:58.490 align:middle line:84%
My computer needs
DDR PC3200 memory.

00:35:58.490 --> 00:35:59.680 align:middle line:90%
It is a choice then.

00:35:59.680 --> 00:36:02.623 align:middle line:84%
Do I buy 4 gigabytes,
2 gigabytes,

00:36:02.623 --> 00:36:04.540 align:middle line:84%
1 gigabyte, based on how
much I want to spend,

00:36:04.540 --> 00:36:05.990 align:middle line:90%
how much memory I want to add?

00:36:05.990 --> 00:36:08.380 align:middle line:84%
But the site is
designed for people

00:36:08.380 --> 00:36:11.280 align:middle line:84%
who are sophisticated
in knowing,

00:36:11.280 --> 00:36:15.650 align:middle line:84%
what memory do I need
for my motherboard?

00:36:15.650 --> 00:36:17.970 align:middle line:84%
This is now getting
to actual data.

00:36:17.970 --> 00:36:21.110 align:middle line:84%
So this was taken
a bit later, when

00:36:21.110 --> 00:36:24.050 align:middle line:84%
you can see people buying
4 megabyte and 2 megabyte--

00:36:24.050 --> 00:36:25.670 align:middle line:90%
or gigabyte modules.

00:36:25.670 --> 00:36:28.160 align:middle line:84%
Back in 1999, what
people would buy

00:36:28.160 --> 00:36:30.800 align:middle line:84%
is either 128
megabytes of memory

00:36:30.800 --> 00:36:33.900 align:middle line:90%
or 256 megabytes of memory.

00:36:33.900 --> 00:36:37.730 align:middle line:84%
So I clicked on one particular
type of memory, 128 megabyte

00:36:37.730 --> 00:36:39.620 align:middle line:90%
modules, that follow the--

00:36:39.620 --> 00:36:41.480 align:middle line:90%
PC100 would be the--

00:36:41.480 --> 00:36:45.960 align:middle line:84%
100 would be the speed which it
communicates with your computer.

00:36:45.960 --> 00:36:47.600 align:middle line:84%
So you know you
need PC100 memory.

00:36:47.600 --> 00:36:49.370 align:middle line:90%
You want to buy 128 megabytes.

00:36:49.370 --> 00:36:50.570 align:middle line:90%
You click on this link.

00:36:50.570 --> 00:36:52.490 align:middle line:90%
And it brings up this page.

00:36:52.490 --> 00:36:55.370 align:middle line:84%
This page just has a
set of offers for where

00:36:55.370 --> 00:36:56.790 align:middle line:90%
you could buy the memory from.

00:36:56.790 --> 00:36:58.940 align:middle line:84%
I can buy it from
Computer Craft, Inc,

00:36:58.940 --> 00:37:01.650 align:middle line:84%
from Connect Computers,
from First Choice Memory.

00:37:01.650 --> 00:37:03.530 align:middle line:84%
This retailer is
located in Florida.

00:37:03.530 --> 00:37:05.702 align:middle line:84%
This one is in
California, and so on.

00:37:05.702 --> 00:37:07.160 align:middle line:84%
And then the price
at which they'll

00:37:07.160 --> 00:37:12.630 align:middle line:84%
sell me the memory is 68,
69, 70, 72, 74, 74, 74, 75.

00:37:12.630 --> 00:37:15.630 align:middle line:84%
And then how much they'll
charge me for shipping,

00:37:15.630 --> 00:37:18.150 align:middle line:84%
it's usually-- at the
time, Pricewatch had a cap.

00:37:18.150 --> 00:37:20.190 align:middle line:84%
You couldn't charge
more than $11.

00:37:20.190 --> 00:37:22.100 align:middle line:84%
And so many of the
firms are some number

00:37:22.100 --> 00:37:25.670 align:middle line:90%
that's slightly less than $11.

00:37:25.670 --> 00:37:27.350 align:middle line:84%
Again, it was a
primitive period,

00:37:27.350 --> 00:37:29.420 align:middle line:84%
where if I wanted
to buy the memory,

00:37:29.420 --> 00:37:33.110 align:middle line:84%
I could then click on
Computer Craft Inc.'s website.

00:37:33.110 --> 00:37:35.780 align:middle line:84%
It would take me
not to-- it's not

00:37:35.780 --> 00:37:38.160 align:middle line:84%
like this would add
the memory to my cart.

00:37:38.160 --> 00:37:39.450 align:middle line:90%
I would click on that button.

00:37:39.450 --> 00:37:41.607 align:middle line:84%
I would typically get to
the Computer Craft site.

00:37:41.607 --> 00:37:43.940 align:middle line:84%
And then I'd have to search
around on the Computer Craft

00:37:43.940 --> 00:37:48.240 align:middle line:84%
site to find where is their
128 megabyte PC100 memory.

00:37:48.240 --> 00:37:50.760 align:middle line:84%
Can I find it at
that price of $68

00:37:50.760 --> 00:37:53.780 align:middle line:84%
that Pricewatch is telling
me that they offer it at.

00:37:53.780 --> 00:37:56.370 align:middle line:84%
Pricewatch was not
scraping these numbers.

00:37:56.370 --> 00:37:58.640 align:middle line:84%
The retailers were having
to enter these numbers

00:37:58.640 --> 00:38:00.300 align:middle line:90%
into a database at Pricewatch.

00:38:00.300 --> 00:38:01.880 align:middle line:84%
So if you're one
of these retailers,

00:38:01.880 --> 00:38:04.490 align:middle line:84%
you would get up in the morning,
decide what your price is,

00:38:04.490 --> 00:38:06.105 align:middle line:84%
upload the new
price to Pricewatch,

00:38:06.105 --> 00:38:08.605 align:middle line:84%
change it on your website, and
that's what you would charge.

00:38:08.605 --> 00:38:12.760 align:middle line:90%


00:38:12.760 --> 00:38:15.820 align:middle line:84%
So basic observations
on Pricewatch

00:38:15.820 --> 00:38:21.010 align:middle line:84%
is there was a tremendous amount
of competition on Pricewatch.

00:38:21.010 --> 00:38:22.630 align:middle line:90%
Prices were very low.

00:38:22.630 --> 00:38:27.110 align:middle line:84%
At this time when this
memory module cost $68,

00:38:27.110 --> 00:38:29.350 align:middle line:84%
if I'd gone to Best Buy
to buy the same thing,

00:38:29.350 --> 00:38:30.920 align:middle line:90%
I might have paid $100.

00:38:30.920 --> 00:38:34.480 align:middle line:84%
So markups were very
low on Pricewatch.

00:38:34.480 --> 00:38:36.790 align:middle line:90%
But there was some dispersion.

00:38:36.790 --> 00:38:40.990 align:middle line:84%
And it was clearly not at all
the frictionless ideal of,

00:38:40.990 --> 00:38:42.370 align:middle line:90%
I click there.

00:38:42.370 --> 00:38:43.940 align:middle line:84%
Everyone's in
Bertrand competition.

00:38:43.940 --> 00:38:45.430 align:middle line:90%
I get exactly what I want.

00:38:45.430 --> 00:38:48.460 align:middle line:84%
Two experiences were
common when you used it.

00:38:48.460 --> 00:38:52.650 align:middle line:84%
First, as I said, you didn't go
directly to the landing page.

00:38:52.650 --> 00:38:55.710 align:middle line:84%
Even when you did go directly to
the page where you clicked on,

00:38:55.710 --> 00:38:59.310 align:middle line:84%
you would find these pages
that would be annoying

00:38:59.310 --> 00:39:01.990 align:middle line:84%
and time consuming to buy
what you wanted to buy.

00:39:01.990 --> 00:39:04.410 align:middle line:84%
So this was an example
from a bit later, where

00:39:04.410 --> 00:39:07.890 align:middle line:84%
I clicked on a offer
from topshop.com

00:39:07.890 --> 00:39:12.670 align:middle line:84%
to buy some memory module that
was supposed to cost $53.81.

00:39:12.670 --> 00:39:16.500 align:middle line:84%
I clicked on a link, saying,
buy this module for $53.81.

00:39:16.500 --> 00:39:19.350 align:middle line:84%
And they give me this
page that tells me--

00:39:19.350 --> 00:39:21.250 align:middle line:84%
and there's actually
more than this page.

00:39:21.250 --> 00:39:26.440 align:middle line:84%
This page saying, price with all
the selected options is $90.36.

00:39:26.440 --> 00:39:29.920 align:middle line:84%
And so, why is it
that it's $90.36?

00:39:29.920 --> 00:39:32.022 align:middle line:84%
What they've done
is this page, it's

00:39:32.022 --> 00:39:33.480 align:middle line:84%
got all these other
things that you

00:39:33.480 --> 00:39:36.850 align:middle line:84%
can click on to add to
the cost of your module.

00:39:36.850 --> 00:39:39.840 align:middle line:84%
And some of them are
just pure annoyances.

00:39:39.840 --> 00:39:42.030 align:middle line:84%
Somehow, they've pre-checked
for me "Bonus buy,

00:39:42.030 --> 00:39:44.710 align:middle line:84%
10 pack of hand
thumbscrews for my case."

00:39:44.710 --> 00:39:48.030 align:middle line:84%
So if I had hand thumbscrews
to open up the computer

00:39:48.030 --> 00:39:50.770 align:middle line:84%
case in which I wanted
to change my memory,

00:39:50.770 --> 00:39:53.600 align:middle line:84%
why I would need new
thumbscrews, I can't imagine.

00:39:53.600 --> 00:39:56.090 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, for $4.95, I can
get those 10 thumbscrews.

00:39:56.090 --> 00:39:57.910 align:middle line:84%
I just have to go
through, read that,

00:39:57.910 --> 00:39:59.210 align:middle line:90%
and say, no, I don't want that.

00:39:59.210 --> 00:40:02.680 align:middle line:84%
Uncheck that box, and it
takes $4.95 off the price.

00:40:02.680 --> 00:40:04.900 align:middle line:84%
Others of these are things
that it's less clear

00:40:04.900 --> 00:40:05.960 align:middle line:90%
that I should uncheck.

00:40:05.960 --> 00:40:09.730 align:middle line:84%
So this is-- take advantage of
these special offers-- memory

00:40:09.730 --> 00:40:11.650 align:middle line:90%
upgrade, CAS 2.5 upgrade.

00:40:11.650 --> 00:40:13.690 align:middle line:84%
Improve performance
and help things.

00:40:13.690 --> 00:40:16.900 align:middle line:84%
They're charging
me $6.35 from that.

00:40:16.900 --> 00:40:18.500 align:middle line:90%
This is another example--

00:40:18.500 --> 00:40:21.700 align:middle line:84%
six layer stability, more
layer, better design--

00:40:21.700 --> 00:40:23.950 align:middle line:90%
not in perfect English, $8.37.

00:40:23.950 --> 00:40:28.070 align:middle line:84%
But then they also have these
others that are more concerning.

00:40:28.070 --> 00:40:31.950 align:middle line:84%
Pre-test, standard pre-test,
avoid costly return agreements,

00:40:31.950 --> 00:40:33.170 align:middle line:90%
$6.97.

00:40:33.170 --> 00:40:35.390 align:middle line:84%
So they're telling
me is that if I

00:40:35.390 --> 00:40:39.290 align:middle line:84%
want them to test the product--
they're shipping it to me.

00:40:39.290 --> 00:40:41.850 align:middle line:84%
Before they ship it to me
and make sure it works,

00:40:41.850 --> 00:40:43.555 align:middle line:84%
I have to pay $7
to get them to test

00:40:43.555 --> 00:40:45.680 align:middle line:84%
the product they're going
to sell me, and make sure

00:40:45.680 --> 00:40:46.560 align:middle line:90%
that it works.

00:40:46.560 --> 00:40:48.410 align:middle line:84%
And if I don't ask
them to test it for me

00:40:48.410 --> 00:40:49.970 align:middle line:84%
and make sure that
it works, they're

00:40:49.970 --> 00:40:52.940 align:middle line:84%
going to charge me to
ship it back to them.

00:40:52.940 --> 00:40:55.760 align:middle line:84%
And so it may be that
you look at this offer

00:40:55.760 --> 00:40:58.350 align:middle line:84%
and realize this offer is not
what I thought this offer was.

00:40:58.350 --> 00:40:59.892 align:middle line:84%
I should think of
this offer as being

00:40:59.892 --> 00:41:02.690 align:middle line:90%
$7 higher than this offer was.

00:41:02.690 --> 00:41:06.710 align:middle line:84%
And in some sense, I'm going to
have to just spend a long time

00:41:06.710 --> 00:41:09.440 align:middle line:84%
reading the fine print of Tough
Shop's offers, where I trust,

00:41:09.440 --> 00:41:11.097 align:middle line:90%
do I want to buy this product?

00:41:11.097 --> 00:41:12.180 align:middle line:90%
Do I not want to buy this?

00:41:12.180 --> 00:41:16.265 align:middle line:84%
And it's just going to
take me a while to shop.

00:41:16.265 --> 00:41:20.570 align:middle line:84%
The second thing
that was common was

00:41:20.570 --> 00:41:24.200 align:middle line:84%
offers that were not designed
to just delay and annoy you,

00:41:24.200 --> 00:41:26.540 align:middle line:84%
but ones where it
was clearly aimed

00:41:26.540 --> 00:41:29.330 align:middle line:84%
to get you to shift away
from buying something

00:41:29.330 --> 00:41:30.880 align:middle line:90%
you bought into something else.

00:41:30.880 --> 00:41:37.520 align:middle line:84%
If you remember back to when I
talked about competitive price

00:41:37.520 --> 00:41:39.840 align:middle line:84%
dispersion, I talked
about add-on pricing.

00:41:39.840 --> 00:41:44.870 align:middle line:84%
That is, if you have two
firms selling two products,

00:41:44.870 --> 00:41:47.900 align:middle line:84%
models of price dispersion
were totally well--

00:41:47.900 --> 00:41:49.730 align:middle line:84%
sorry-- models of
price discrimination

00:41:49.730 --> 00:41:52.190 align:middle line:84%
were totally well with
differentiated products

00:41:52.190 --> 00:41:53.280 align:middle line:90%
and multiple firms.

00:41:53.280 --> 00:41:56.370 align:middle line:84%
And you can have two firms
competing against each other.

00:41:56.370 --> 00:41:58.140 align:middle line:90%
They advertise all their prices.

00:41:58.140 --> 00:42:01.760 align:middle line:84%
They sell low quality goods
at low prices, high quality

00:42:01.760 --> 00:42:03.120 align:middle line:90%
goods at high prices.

00:42:03.120 --> 00:42:07.460 align:middle line:84%
And that can be rational if the
consumers for the low quality

00:42:07.460 --> 00:42:09.600 align:middle line:84%
goods are more price
sensitive across firms.

00:42:09.600 --> 00:42:12.020 align:middle line:84%
And so you have an
equilibrium where

00:42:12.020 --> 00:42:15.500 align:middle line:84%
it's like the competition
on two-line model.

00:42:15.500 --> 00:42:18.680 align:middle line:84%
For low quality goods, firms
are very undifferentiated.

00:42:18.680 --> 00:42:20.820 align:middle line:84%
For the high quality goods,
they're differentiated.

00:42:20.820 --> 00:42:22.680 align:middle line:84%
These goods are sold
at a low markup.

00:42:22.680 --> 00:42:25.700 align:middle line:84%
These goods are sold
at a high markup.

00:42:25.700 --> 00:42:27.680 align:middle line:84%
And one thing I
said when I talked

00:42:27.680 --> 00:42:30.710 align:middle line:84%
about that is one way in
which firms in this model

00:42:30.710 --> 00:42:34.430 align:middle line:84%
can raise prices is you
can think of another game

00:42:34.430 --> 00:42:37.700 align:middle line:84%
where you advertise your
low quality product,

00:42:37.700 --> 00:42:39.590 align:middle line:84%
and then you have an
unadvertised price

00:42:39.590 --> 00:42:42.290 align:middle line:90%
for a higher quality product.

00:42:42.290 --> 00:42:44.227 align:middle line:84%
In that game where
you only show people

00:42:44.227 --> 00:42:45.810 align:middle line:84%
the price for the
high quality product

00:42:45.810 --> 00:42:50.670 align:middle line:84%
once they show up at the store,
I gave this model that said,

00:42:50.670 --> 00:42:53.380 align:middle line:84%
markups are going to be
even higher in equilibrium.

00:42:53.380 --> 00:42:55.860 align:middle line:84%
And the reason is
that firms are going

00:42:55.860 --> 00:42:58.650 align:middle line:84%
to charge the ex post monopoly
price for the upgrades.

00:42:58.650 --> 00:43:01.170 align:middle line:90%


00:43:01.170 --> 00:43:04.020 align:middle line:84%
That causes a wedge between--
when you force the prices

00:43:04.020 --> 00:43:07.080 align:middle line:84%
of the low and high quality
goods further apart,

00:43:07.080 --> 00:43:08.760 align:middle line:84%
there's this adverse
selection effect,

00:43:08.760 --> 00:43:10.960 align:middle line:90%
where you don't want to get--

00:43:10.960 --> 00:43:12.900 align:middle line:84%
you don't want to sell
to the cheapskates who

00:43:12.900 --> 00:43:15.390 align:middle line:84%
are going to buy your
rental car for $19.95 a day,

00:43:15.390 --> 00:43:17.980 align:middle line:84%
and not get the insurance,
and not get the car seat.

00:43:17.980 --> 00:43:20.550 align:middle line:84%
And because there's this
adverse selection effect where

00:43:20.550 --> 00:43:22.560 align:middle line:84%
it's the cheapskates who
don't buy the insurance

00:43:22.560 --> 00:43:26.510 align:middle line:84%
and the rental-- and the car
seats, you then want to dump--

00:43:26.510 --> 00:43:29.820 align:middle line:84%
you go from wanting to undercut
the rival to get consumers

00:43:29.820 --> 00:43:33.700 align:middle line:84%
to wanting to overcut the rival
to dump the cheapskates on them.

00:43:33.700 --> 00:43:36.180 align:middle line:84%
Markups for both products
can end up-- markups

00:43:36.180 --> 00:43:37.800 align:middle line:84%
for the sort of--
average markups

00:43:37.800 --> 00:43:39.540 align:middle line:90%
can go up in equilibrium.

00:43:39.540 --> 00:43:42.970 align:middle line:84%
We see what looks like
an add-on strategy here.

00:43:42.970 --> 00:43:46.260 align:middle line:84%
So you've clicked on this
product I want to buy.

00:43:46.260 --> 00:43:49.200 align:middle line:84%
I clicked on, I want to buy
OEM 512 megabyte memory.

00:43:49.200 --> 00:43:51.540 align:middle line:84%
They actually put the check
mark in the correct box.

00:43:51.540 --> 00:43:53.490 align:middle line:84%
This is what I said
I wanted to buy.

00:43:53.490 --> 00:43:57.020 align:middle line:84%
But then they tell me
that I can spend $15 more

00:43:57.020 --> 00:44:00.140 align:middle line:84%
and get this product,
or I can spend $25 more

00:44:00.140 --> 00:44:03.710 align:middle line:84%
and get this product, which I'm
going to think of as add-ons.

00:44:03.710 --> 00:44:04.740 align:middle line:90%
What do they do?

00:44:04.740 --> 00:44:09.050 align:middle line:84%
They tell me a lot about why
the better products are better.

00:44:09.050 --> 00:44:11.610 align:middle line:84%
So for instance, this
one is CAS 3 latency.

00:44:11.610 --> 00:44:13.470 align:middle line:90%
This is CAS 2.5 latency.

00:44:13.470 --> 00:44:15.750 align:middle line:90%
This is CAS 2.5 latency.

00:44:15.750 --> 00:44:17.580 align:middle line:90%
This one has a four-layer board.

00:44:17.580 --> 00:44:20.750 align:middle line:84%
These each have
six-layer boards.

00:44:20.750 --> 00:44:24.958 align:middle line:84%
These have OEM DRAM
downgrade chips,

00:44:24.958 --> 00:44:26.750 align:middle line:84%
whereas these have
industry standard chips,

00:44:26.750 --> 00:44:29.180 align:middle line:84%
whereas these have even
better brand name chips.

00:44:29.180 --> 00:44:31.030 align:middle line:84%
Samsung and Micron
are major brands.

00:44:31.030 --> 00:44:34.730 align:middle line:90%


00:44:34.730 --> 00:44:36.430 align:middle line:90%
These have a restocking fee.

00:44:36.430 --> 00:44:39.360 align:middle line:84%
These have no restocking fee--
satisfaction and compatibility

00:44:39.360 --> 00:44:41.370 align:middle line:90%
fully guaranteed.

00:44:41.370 --> 00:44:42.500 align:middle line:90%
In fact, one thing that--

00:44:42.500 --> 00:44:44.870 align:middle line:84%
for fun, I clicked on
some of these things.

00:44:44.870 --> 00:44:46.700 align:middle line:84%
This one has verify
compatibility

00:44:46.700 --> 00:44:48.740 align:middle line:90%
with your memory configurator.

00:44:48.740 --> 00:44:51.960 align:middle line:84%
I clicked on this, and entered
a zillion different computers.

00:44:51.960 --> 00:44:54.980 align:middle line:84%
Every consumer said this seemed
to be a button that just says,

00:44:54.980 --> 00:44:56.303 align:middle line:90%
can't verify compatibility.

00:44:56.303 --> 00:44:58.220 align:middle line:84%
So no matter what you
typed in, in some sense,

00:44:58.220 --> 00:45:00.530 align:middle line:84%
it would just say always
is not compatible.

00:45:00.530 --> 00:45:03.290 align:middle line:84%
So clearly, this
page is designed

00:45:03.290 --> 00:45:05.738 align:middle line:84%
to get us to buy this
instead of buy that,

00:45:05.738 --> 00:45:07.280 align:middle line:84%
and just telling
you all the reasons.

00:45:07.280 --> 00:45:09.073 align:middle line:84%
These words like
OEM downgrade chips.

00:45:09.073 --> 00:45:10.490 align:middle line:84%
I don't even know
what that means.

00:45:10.490 --> 00:45:12.000 align:middle line:90%
But I know it's bad.

00:45:12.000 --> 00:45:14.550 align:middle line:84%
It's not trying to teach
us about the products.

00:45:14.550 --> 00:45:17.120 align:middle line:84%
It's not trying to tell
me why is CAS 2.5 latency

00:45:17.120 --> 00:45:18.540 align:middle line:90%
better than CAS 3 latency.

00:45:18.540 --> 00:45:21.210 align:middle line:84%
Or why is a six-layer board
better than a four-layer board?

00:45:21.210 --> 00:45:22.610 align:middle line:90%
Should you care about that?

00:45:22.610 --> 00:45:23.990 align:middle line:84%
But it does seem
like it's trying

00:45:23.990 --> 00:45:26.570 align:middle line:84%
to convince us to
buy this product

00:45:26.570 --> 00:45:29.215 align:middle line:84%
and pay $25 extra instead
of buying that product.

00:45:29.215 --> 00:45:32.040 align:middle line:90%


00:45:32.040 --> 00:45:35.280 align:middle line:84%
So what we're going
to argue in this paper

00:45:35.280 --> 00:45:39.992 align:middle line:84%
is that if you really had
Bertrand competition for memory

00:45:39.992 --> 00:45:41.700 align:middle line:84%
modules and they were
being sold at cost,

00:45:41.700 --> 00:45:43.780 align:middle line:84%
the retailers couldn't
cover their fixed costs.

00:45:43.780 --> 00:45:45.490 align:middle line:90%
The market wouldn't exist.

00:45:45.490 --> 00:45:46.840 align:middle line:90%
The market does exist.

00:45:46.840 --> 00:45:47.620 align:middle line:90%
Why is that?

00:45:47.620 --> 00:45:50.970 align:middle line:84%
Well, our thought is
that firms are engaged.

00:45:50.970 --> 00:45:54.720 align:middle line:84%
While Pricewatch has
invested in technology

00:45:54.720 --> 00:45:57.120 align:middle line:84%
to reduce search
costs, the firms

00:45:57.120 --> 00:45:59.520 align:middle line:84%
are fighting back
with obfuscation

00:45:59.520 --> 00:46:02.550 align:middle line:84%
to try to raise search costs and
use those higher search costs

00:46:02.550 --> 00:46:06.080 align:middle line:90%
to keep the increased markups.

00:46:06.080 --> 00:46:08.940 align:middle line:84%
We can think of two different
ways you could do that.

00:46:08.940 --> 00:46:11.910 align:middle line:84%
One is-- the simplest
theory of obfuscation

00:46:11.910 --> 00:46:17.190 align:middle line:84%
is we know that you get higher
markups and more dispersion

00:46:17.190 --> 00:46:20.260 align:middle line:84%
when s is higher
than when s is lower.

00:46:20.260 --> 00:46:23.730 align:middle line:84%
So maybe what firms are doing
is just making s higher.

00:46:23.730 --> 00:46:27.420 align:middle line:84%
If I make you spend 5 minutes
clicking-- reading through--

00:46:27.420 --> 00:46:30.250 align:middle line:84%
unclicking the thumbscrews,
unclicking other things,

00:46:30.250 --> 00:46:31.968 align:middle line:84%
I spend 5 minutes
clicking through doing

00:46:31.968 --> 00:46:34.510 align:middle line:84%
all the things where I finally
found out the price, I'm like,

00:46:34.510 --> 00:46:37.010 align:middle line:84%
do I really want to go back and
go through another 5 minutes

00:46:37.010 --> 00:46:39.820 align:middle line:84%
with the next website, and find
out what their real price is.

00:46:39.820 --> 00:46:43.000 align:middle line:84%
So by just making shopping
more time consuming,

00:46:43.000 --> 00:46:44.200 align:middle line:90%
I've raised the s.

00:46:44.200 --> 00:46:46.600 align:middle line:84%
Raising the s is going to
raise the equilibrium price

00:46:46.600 --> 00:46:48.150 align:middle line:90%
distribution.

00:46:48.150 --> 00:46:50.700 align:middle line:84%
And then the second
thought would be--

00:46:50.700 --> 00:46:53.160 align:middle line:84%
this could be what I mentioned
before, this add-on pricing

00:46:53.160 --> 00:46:56.490 align:middle line:84%
situation where we
know that equilibrium

00:46:56.490 --> 00:46:59.400 align:middle line:84%
prices with add-on pricing are
higher than equilibrium prices

00:46:59.400 --> 00:47:01.780 align:middle line:84%
when all goods prices
are advertised.

00:47:01.780 --> 00:47:04.410 align:middle line:84%
Maybe what firms are trying to
do is shift the equilibrium--

00:47:04.410 --> 00:47:05.280 align:middle line:90%
shift the--

00:47:05.280 --> 00:47:09.150 align:middle line:84%
change the game from a game in
which firms advertise all prices

00:47:09.150 --> 00:47:13.200 align:middle line:84%
to games in which firms
advertise base goods,

00:47:13.200 --> 00:47:15.430 align:middle line:90%
and then invent add-ons.

00:47:15.430 --> 00:47:17.790 align:middle line:84%
And that model, the
larger is the add-on

00:47:17.790 --> 00:47:19.755 align:middle line:84%
as a fraction of the
price, the higher

00:47:19.755 --> 00:47:21.460 align:middle line:84%
are the equilibrium
markups going to be.

00:47:21.460 --> 00:47:24.180 align:middle line:84%
So firms are in some
sense inventing add-ons

00:47:24.180 --> 00:47:26.340 align:middle line:84%
or inventing inferior
products that the add-ons

00:47:26.340 --> 00:47:27.510 align:middle line:90%
can be even larger.

00:47:27.510 --> 00:47:30.160 align:middle line:84%
And that can be a way to
raise equilibrium prices.

00:47:30.160 --> 00:47:34.080 align:middle line:84%
And what we're going to try
to do in this paper is look

00:47:34.080 --> 00:47:36.330 align:middle line:84%
at demand and look at what
demand looks like, and see,

00:47:36.330 --> 00:47:40.590 align:middle line:84%
can this model add-on pricing
explain the equilibrium markups

00:47:40.590 --> 00:47:43.690 align:middle line:90%
that we end up with?

00:47:43.690 --> 00:47:48.470 align:middle line:84%
So the paper, we're going to
look at four different products.

00:47:48.470 --> 00:47:52.810 align:middle line:84%
The four different products
are 128 megabyte PC100 memory

00:47:52.810 --> 00:47:55.720 align:middle line:84%
modules, 100 megabyte
PC133 modules,

00:47:55.720 --> 00:47:58.660 align:middle line:90%
and 256 megabyte versions.

00:47:58.660 --> 00:48:01.545 align:middle line:84%
We're going to treat these four
categories differently, and just

00:48:01.545 --> 00:48:02.920 align:middle line:84%
basically do it
as an opportunity

00:48:02.920 --> 00:48:05.148 align:middle line:90%
to have four separate products.

00:48:05.148 --> 00:48:06.940 align:middle line:84%
We'll analyze each one
separately, and just

00:48:06.940 --> 00:48:08.648 align:middle line:84%
get some reinforcement
of the conclusions

00:48:08.648 --> 00:48:10.000 align:middle line:90%
we would get from one.

00:48:10.000 --> 00:48:11.590 align:middle line:84%
But we do spend
most of the time--

00:48:11.590 --> 00:48:15.290 align:middle line:84%
I'll focus today on 128
megabyte PC100 memory modules.

00:48:15.290 --> 00:48:18.790 align:middle line:84%
That was the product that was
most popular and stayed alive

00:48:18.790 --> 00:48:23.320 align:middle line:84%
for longest over the
one-year data collection.

00:48:23.320 --> 00:48:25.870 align:middle line:90%
So what is our data collection?

00:48:25.870 --> 00:48:29.080 align:middle line:84%
Within each of these
categories, there's

00:48:29.080 --> 00:48:33.790 align:middle line:84%
one retailer that we
got internal data from.

00:48:33.790 --> 00:48:35.770 align:middle line:84%
And then we have
data, external data

00:48:35.770 --> 00:48:38.320 align:middle line:84%
from all the other
retailers from Pricewatch.

00:48:38.320 --> 00:48:41.140 align:middle line:84%
The one retailer that we had
this special data connection

00:48:41.140 --> 00:48:43.850 align:middle line:84%
with, that retailer
sold three prices--

00:48:43.850 --> 00:48:45.810 align:middle line:84%
three products
within each category.

00:48:45.810 --> 00:48:48.440 align:middle line:84%
We'll call them the low quality
product, the medium quality

00:48:48.440 --> 00:48:50.490 align:middle line:84%
product, the high
quality product.

00:48:50.490 --> 00:48:53.300 align:middle line:84%
You can roughly think of
the low quality product

00:48:53.300 --> 00:48:55.020 align:middle line:84%
as something that
looks like this.

00:48:55.020 --> 00:48:57.170 align:middle line:84%
The medium quality
product looks like this.

00:48:57.170 --> 00:48:59.750 align:middle line:84%
The high quality
product looks like this.

00:48:59.750 --> 00:49:05.030 align:middle line:84%
Something to know about them
is in this market, the medium

00:49:05.030 --> 00:49:07.550 align:middle line:84%
quality products really
didn't cost much more

00:49:07.550 --> 00:49:09.320 align:middle line:90%
than the low quality products.

00:49:09.320 --> 00:49:12.410 align:middle line:84%
Often if these
cost $60 wholesale,

00:49:12.410 --> 00:49:15.020 align:middle line:90%
these would cost $61 wholesale.

00:49:15.020 --> 00:49:17.840 align:middle line:84%
The retailer we got the data
from said, in fact, sometimes

00:49:17.840 --> 00:49:20.990 align:middle line:84%
he would even sell
someone the $60 product,

00:49:20.990 --> 00:49:23.630 align:middle line:84%
and then ship the
$61 product, just

00:49:23.630 --> 00:49:26.540 align:middle line:84%
because dealing with the
hassles and the customer--

00:49:26.540 --> 00:49:30.260 align:middle line:84%
customer disappointment when
they got the low quality

00:49:30.260 --> 00:49:31.920 align:middle line:90%
product, then it was broken.

00:49:31.920 --> 00:49:32.673 align:middle line:90%
They returned it.

00:49:32.673 --> 00:49:33.840 align:middle line:90%
It wasn't worth his hassles.

00:49:33.840 --> 00:49:36.423 align:middle line:84%
And you sometimes sell them a
better product than they bought,

00:49:36.423 --> 00:49:39.950 align:middle line:84%
just because these really didn't
cost much more than these ones.

00:49:39.950 --> 00:49:44.070 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, but he had the
three products available.

00:49:44.070 --> 00:49:46.560 align:middle line:84%
The high quality products
that have the name brand

00:49:46.560 --> 00:49:49.843 align:middle line:84%
chips in them did cost
substantially more.

00:49:49.843 --> 00:49:51.510 align:middle line:84%
When you're thinking
about this, though,

00:49:51.510 --> 00:49:54.450 align:middle line:84%
note that high quality
versus low quality,

00:49:54.450 --> 00:49:56.010 align:middle line:90%
it's really a bundle.

00:49:56.010 --> 00:50:00.870 align:middle line:84%
So it's not the case that you
can compare my medium quality

00:50:00.870 --> 00:50:03.600 align:middle line:84%
product from firm A and medium
quality product from firm B,

00:50:03.600 --> 00:50:07.240 align:middle line:84%
because quality is this
many, many dimensional thing.

00:50:07.240 --> 00:50:08.890 align:middle line:84%
What's the restocking
fee policy?

00:50:08.890 --> 00:50:10.290 align:middle line:90%
What's the shipping policy?

00:50:10.290 --> 00:50:13.150 align:middle line:84%
How many layers
does the board have?

00:50:13.150 --> 00:50:15.030 align:middle line:90%
What's the CAS latency?

00:50:15.030 --> 00:50:19.240 align:middle line:84%
These are not something that's
comparable across retailers.

00:50:19.240 --> 00:50:21.690 align:middle line:84%
So we can really--
every different retailer

00:50:21.690 --> 00:50:23.640 align:middle line:84%
may have its own low,
medium, and high quality

00:50:23.640 --> 00:50:25.390 align:middle line:84%
that don't correspond
with the other ones.

00:50:25.390 --> 00:50:28.080 align:middle line:84%
But so anyway, our retailer had
these three different quality

00:50:28.080 --> 00:50:29.710 align:middle line:90%
levels that were consistent.

00:50:29.710 --> 00:50:32.360 align:middle line:90%


00:50:32.360 --> 00:50:36.920 align:middle line:84%
The price data that we get is
we went to the pricewatch.com

00:50:36.920 --> 00:50:37.490 align:middle line:90%
website.

00:50:37.490 --> 00:50:39.950 align:middle line:84%
And we just wrote an early
web scraping program,

00:50:39.950 --> 00:50:43.490 align:middle line:84%
and just basically downloaded
the raw HTML files,

00:50:43.490 --> 00:50:47.120 align:middle line:84%
and got the 12 or 24 lowest
prices in each category.

00:50:47.120 --> 00:50:49.370 align:middle line:84%
We did that hourly,
and we did that hourly

00:50:49.370 --> 00:50:51.440 align:middle line:90%
over the course of a year.

00:50:51.440 --> 00:50:53.720 align:middle line:84%
Presumably, all the
prices in that data

00:50:53.720 --> 00:50:55.490 align:middle line:90%
are for low quality products.

00:50:55.490 --> 00:50:58.140 align:middle line:84%
If the high quality
products cost $15 more,

00:50:58.140 --> 00:51:03.640 align:middle line:84%
they would never show up in the
lowest 12 or 24 lowest prices.

00:51:03.640 --> 00:51:06.680 align:middle line:84%
We have quantity data
from one retailer.

00:51:06.680 --> 00:51:10.210 align:middle line:84%
So that one retailer we had
contact with owned websites A

00:51:10.210 --> 00:51:14.020 align:middle line:84%
and B. For those websites, we
have the medium and high quality

00:51:14.020 --> 00:51:15.370 align:middle line:90%
prices available.

00:51:15.370 --> 00:51:19.480 align:middle line:84%
And we have the number
of modules of each type

00:51:19.480 --> 00:51:20.630 align:middle line:90%
that it sold.

00:51:20.630 --> 00:51:22.850 align:middle line:84%
Typically, we're going to
use this at a daily level.

00:51:22.850 --> 00:51:24.250 align:middle line:84%
We're going to
aggregate up to-- we

00:51:24.250 --> 00:51:25.870 align:middle line:84%
have the real-time
sales data, but we're

00:51:25.870 --> 00:51:27.745 align:middle line:84%
going to aggregate up
to the real-time sales,

00:51:27.745 --> 00:51:29.360 align:middle line:90%
to the daily sales.

00:51:29.360 --> 00:51:31.810 align:middle line:84%
And then, also, something
that's very rare

00:51:31.810 --> 00:51:34.850 align:middle line:84%
is we have tremendous
cost data here.

00:51:34.850 --> 00:51:37.420 align:middle line:90%
So we actually have the raw--

00:51:37.420 --> 00:51:40.840 align:middle line:84%
we have all of the
invoices that these--

00:51:40.840 --> 00:51:42.110 align:middle line:90%
from this retailer.

00:51:42.110 --> 00:51:45.160 align:middle line:84%
So we know every time that
it bought modules wholesale,

00:51:45.160 --> 00:51:46.450 align:middle line:90%
what did it buy.

00:51:46.450 --> 00:51:48.760 align:middle line:84%
And what's interesting
about this business

00:51:48.760 --> 00:51:51.740 align:middle line:84%
is this was basically a
negative inventory business.

00:51:51.740 --> 00:51:54.320 align:middle line:84%
This is a business where
prices were falling over time.

00:51:54.320 --> 00:51:56.055 align:middle line:84%
You didn't want
to hold inventory.

00:51:56.055 --> 00:51:57.430 align:middle line:84%
And so what the
retailer would do

00:51:57.430 --> 00:51:59.590 align:middle line:84%
is basically every day
would turn on its computers,

00:51:59.590 --> 00:52:01.580 align:middle line:84%
and would start
selling memory modules.

00:52:01.580 --> 00:52:04.430 align:middle line:84%
And at the end of the day,
or at 3:00 in the afternoon,

00:52:04.430 --> 00:52:07.610 align:middle line:84%
it would say, I've already
sold 12 of this module,

00:52:07.610 --> 00:52:11.130 align:middle line:84%
and 24 of this one, and 36 of
this one, and 14 of this one.

00:52:11.130 --> 00:52:15.890 align:middle line:84%
And then it would buy them
wholesale that afternoon,

00:52:15.890 --> 00:52:18.890 align:middle line:84%
drive a truck up to city
of industry in California,

00:52:18.890 --> 00:52:23.060 align:middle line:84%
go to a wholesale distributor,
and buy the 340 memory modules

00:52:23.060 --> 00:52:25.170 align:middle line:84%
that it had sold
that day online.

00:52:25.170 --> 00:52:30.870 align:middle line:84%
Drive the truck back to
the firm's retail office.

00:52:30.870 --> 00:52:32.900 align:middle line:84%
And then the warehouse
workers would then

00:52:32.900 --> 00:52:34.460 align:middle line:84%
start putting those
things in boxes,

00:52:34.460 --> 00:52:36.260 align:middle line:84%
and shipping them
out the next day.

00:52:36.260 --> 00:52:38.900 align:middle line:84%
So basically, as
a result, it was

00:52:38.900 --> 00:52:42.390 align:middle line:84%
buying things wholesale every
day, or every two or three days.

00:52:42.390 --> 00:52:45.260 align:middle line:84%
And we have very high
frequency cost data,

00:52:45.260 --> 00:52:48.650 align:middle line:84%
which is rare in understanding
how the cost to the retailer

00:52:48.650 --> 00:52:49.150 align:middle line:90%
varied.

00:52:49.150 --> 00:52:51.820 align:middle line:90%


00:52:51.820 --> 00:52:56.590 align:middle line:84%
Just for some basic
picture of the industry,

00:52:56.590 --> 00:52:59.980 align:middle line:84%
this shows the price of our
128-megabyte modules over

00:52:59.980 --> 00:53:02.490 align:middle line:84%
the course of the year from
which we collected data.

00:53:02.490 --> 00:53:05.110 align:middle line:90%
So they start out at about $80.

00:53:05.110 --> 00:53:07.760 align:middle line:90%
Prices rose to about $120.

00:53:07.760 --> 00:53:10.040 align:middle line:84%
And then with technological
progress, they drop.

00:53:10.040 --> 00:53:12.040 align:middle line:84%
And by the end of the
sample, those same modules

00:53:12.040 --> 00:53:14.500 align:middle line:84%
that were $20 at the beginning
or like $80 at the beginning

00:53:14.500 --> 00:53:15.890 align:middle line:90%
are $25.

00:53:15.890 --> 00:53:17.840 align:middle line:84%
This is actually an
interesting event here.

00:53:17.840 --> 00:53:22.330 align:middle line:84%
This price spike
was due to collusion

00:53:22.330 --> 00:53:24.740 align:middle line:90%
by the memory manufacturers.

00:53:24.740 --> 00:53:30.190 align:middle line:84%
So there was actually an
antitrust case or legal case

00:53:30.190 --> 00:53:32.128 align:middle line:84%
against these companies
for price fixing.

00:53:32.128 --> 00:53:33.670 align:middle line:84%
And companies were
actually, I think,

00:53:33.670 --> 00:53:35.860 align:middle line:90%
found guilty for price fixing.

00:53:35.860 --> 00:53:38.200 align:middle line:84%
And they managed to raise
prices dramatically,

00:53:38.200 --> 00:53:43.060 align:middle line:84%
but only for a short time
before the collusive agreement

00:53:43.060 --> 00:53:44.770 align:middle line:90%
fell apart.

00:53:44.770 --> 00:53:47.560 align:middle line:84%
And actually, this is one
of the cases, actually, one

00:53:47.560 --> 00:53:49.900 align:middle line:84%
of the rare times I made
some money on my research

00:53:49.900 --> 00:53:52.520 align:middle line:84%
where I was contacted
by a law firm,

00:53:52.520 --> 00:53:54.870 align:middle line:84%
looking for high frequency
data on these memory prices.

00:53:54.870 --> 00:53:57.500 align:middle line:84%
And I just got this phone call
out of the blue from someone who

00:53:57.500 --> 00:54:00.830 align:middle line:84%
said, we're representing
some company that's

00:54:00.830 --> 00:54:03.050 align:middle line:84%
suing Micron or whomever
about these things,

00:54:03.050 --> 00:54:05.150 align:middle line:84%
and we wanted to get
access to your data.

00:54:05.150 --> 00:54:08.040 align:middle line:84%
How much will you charge
for your memory data?

00:54:08.040 --> 00:54:10.550 align:middle line:84%
And I was like, charge
for my memory data?

00:54:10.550 --> 00:54:13.370 align:middle line:84%
I just give it away to
everybody all the time.

00:54:13.370 --> 00:54:15.560 align:middle line:84%
I haven't put it on my
website at the time,

00:54:15.560 --> 00:54:17.780 align:middle line:84%
but I normally just
give it away for free.

00:54:17.780 --> 00:54:20.750 align:middle line:84%
And they're like,
how much do I charge?

00:54:20.750 --> 00:54:21.965 align:middle line:90%
I don't know. $5,000?

00:54:21.965 --> 00:54:23.340 align:middle line:84%
And the guy was
like, yeah, sure.

00:54:23.340 --> 00:54:23.910 align:middle line:90%
OK, thanks.

00:54:23.910 --> 00:54:24.720 align:middle line:90%
We'll send you the check.

00:54:24.720 --> 00:54:26.887 align:middle line:84%
And I was like, oh, I guess
maybe I should have said

00:54:26.887 --> 00:54:27.490 align:middle line:90%
$50,000.

00:54:27.490 --> 00:54:28.260 align:middle line:90%
So I don't know.

00:54:28.260 --> 00:54:31.262 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, these guys
were price fixing.

00:54:31.262 --> 00:54:32.720 align:middle line:84%
The price fixing
scheme fell apart,

00:54:32.720 --> 00:54:36.170 align:middle line:84%
and then memory prices continued
on that downward trend.

00:54:36.170 --> 00:54:38.000 align:middle line:84%
What's notable about
the downward trend

00:54:38.000 --> 00:54:40.223 align:middle line:90%
for this paper is--

00:54:40.223 --> 00:54:41.890 align:middle line:84%
this is showing you,
I think, the 12th--

00:54:41.890 --> 00:54:44.740 align:middle line:84%
the lowest price and the 12th
lowest price for the memory over

00:54:44.740 --> 00:54:45.570 align:middle line:90%
time.

00:54:45.570 --> 00:54:47.960 align:middle line:84%
You can see that prices
are changing dramatically.

00:54:47.960 --> 00:54:51.040 align:middle line:84%
This graph here is
our retailer's price.

00:54:51.040 --> 00:54:54.460 align:middle line:84%
And our retailer changes
its price occasionally,

00:54:54.460 --> 00:54:58.253 align:middle line:84%
but as a result of changing its
price occasionally, every time

00:54:58.253 --> 00:54:59.920 align:middle line:84%
it changes its price,
it may drop and be

00:54:59.920 --> 00:55:01.212 align:middle line:90%
the lowest price in the market.

00:55:01.212 --> 00:55:02.830 align:middle line:84%
And then by leaving
its price fixed,

00:55:02.830 --> 00:55:05.290 align:middle line:84%
it's drifting, you
can think, downward

00:55:05.290 --> 00:55:08.680 align:middle line:84%
on that price watch list,
where I leave my price fixed.

00:55:08.680 --> 00:55:11.640 align:middle line:84%
And I go from having the lowest
price to having the 10th lowest

00:55:11.640 --> 00:55:12.140 align:middle line:90%
price.

00:55:12.140 --> 00:55:14.182 align:middle line:84%
I lower it again to have
the second lowest price,

00:55:14.182 --> 00:55:15.910 align:middle line:90%
and then my price drifts across.

00:55:15.910 --> 00:55:20.080 align:middle line:84%
And every time it drifts across
as prices elsewhere are falling,

00:55:20.080 --> 00:55:23.390 align:middle line:84%
I end up getting bumped up and
down on that Pricewatch list.

00:55:23.390 --> 00:55:27.520 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I've just
reset my price.

00:55:27.520 --> 00:55:28.540 align:middle line:90%
And I set my price.

00:55:28.540 --> 00:55:30.560 align:middle line:90%
And I'm here at $69.

00:55:30.560 --> 00:55:33.550 align:middle line:84%
But then as I stay at $69,
and this guy jumps to $68,

00:55:33.550 --> 00:55:36.520 align:middle line:84%
and this one goes to $67,
and this one goes to $66,

00:55:36.520 --> 00:55:39.010 align:middle line:84%
I fall down the list, and
then I reset my price.

00:55:39.010 --> 00:55:40.500 align:middle line:90%
And I jump back up.

00:55:40.500 --> 00:55:43.015 align:middle line:84%
And we think that's very
nice for identification

00:55:43.015 --> 00:55:44.473 align:middle line:84%
if we're trying to
estimate demand.

00:55:44.473 --> 00:55:46.980 align:middle line:90%


00:55:46.980 --> 00:55:50.190 align:middle line:84%
We're basically regressing
demand on your price.

00:55:50.190 --> 00:55:53.760 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to think of
the price, your relative price

00:55:53.760 --> 00:55:55.240 align:middle line:90%
as exogenous variation.

00:55:55.240 --> 00:55:58.713 align:middle line:84%
It's not that I'm deciding
here to be the lowest price,

00:55:58.713 --> 00:56:00.880 align:middle line:84%
and I'm deciding here to
be the second lowest price.

00:56:00.880 --> 00:56:02.280 align:middle line:84%
I'm trying here to be
the eighth lowest price,

00:56:02.280 --> 00:56:03.940 align:middle line:84%
and here to be the
10th lowest price.

00:56:03.940 --> 00:56:06.060 align:middle line:84%
It's basically the firm's
getting buffeted around

00:56:06.060 --> 00:56:07.480 align:middle line:90%
by the randomness in prices.

00:56:07.480 --> 00:56:09.420 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to
think of that with where

00:56:09.420 --> 00:56:12.000 align:middle line:84%
you are within the distribution
as being largely random

00:56:12.000 --> 00:56:15.870 align:middle line:90%
from the firm's perspective.

00:56:15.870 --> 00:56:23.990 align:middle line:84%
So some summary statistics, so
for the 128-megabyte PC modules,

00:56:23.990 --> 00:56:25.010 align:middle line:90%
again, we have two--

00:56:25.010 --> 00:56:26.560 align:middle line:84%
we have one year of
data, but then we

00:56:26.560 --> 00:56:28.430 align:middle line:90%
have two different websites.

00:56:28.430 --> 00:56:35.590 align:middle line:84%
So days times websites, 365,
we end up with 680 website day

00:56:35.590 --> 00:56:37.120 align:middle line:90%
observations.

00:56:37.120 --> 00:56:39.790 align:middle line:90%
What do the memory modules cost?

00:56:39.790 --> 00:56:44.380 align:middle line:84%
So the low quality modules
generally cost, on average, $67.

00:56:44.380 --> 00:56:47.870 align:middle line:84%
But obviously, remember, it
was a big change over time.

00:56:47.870 --> 00:56:51.280 align:middle line:84%
Medium quality modules
are about $25 more.

00:56:51.280 --> 00:56:53.540 align:middle line:84%
High quality modules
are about $50 more.

00:56:53.540 --> 00:56:58.570 align:middle line:84%
So very, very substantial
add-on strategy here.

00:56:58.570 --> 00:57:00.850 align:middle line:84%
The low quality
modules are usually not

00:57:00.850 --> 00:57:03.500 align:middle line:84%
very different from the
lowest price in the market.

00:57:03.500 --> 00:57:05.600 align:middle line:84%
The lowest price in
the market is $63.

00:57:05.600 --> 00:57:08.440 align:middle line:90%
This firm is charging $66.

00:57:08.440 --> 00:57:10.790 align:middle line:84%
What do people do when
faced with these prices?

00:57:10.790 --> 00:57:13.780 align:middle line:84%
Well, given that they are
charging an awful lot of money

00:57:13.780 --> 00:57:16.120 align:middle line:84%
for those add-ons, most
people are choosing

00:57:16.120 --> 00:57:17.830 align:middle line:90%
to buy the low quality product.

00:57:17.830 --> 00:57:22.730 align:middle line:84%
They sell on average
12.8 per website per day.

00:57:22.730 --> 00:57:25.520 align:middle line:84%
Medium quality modules
are selling 2.4.

00:57:25.520 --> 00:57:27.840 align:middle line:84%
High quality modules
are selling 2.0.

00:57:27.840 --> 00:57:30.720 align:middle line:84%
So most people buy the
low quality modules.

00:57:30.720 --> 00:57:34.303 align:middle line:84%
But a number of people buy
medium and high quality modules.

00:57:34.303 --> 00:57:35.720 align:middle line:84%
Some people have
also asked about,

00:57:35.720 --> 00:57:37.440 align:middle line:84%
is this just some
really tiny market?

00:57:37.440 --> 00:57:39.173 align:middle line:84%
Well, what's going
on is this firm

00:57:39.173 --> 00:57:41.090 align:middle line:84%
has a website that has
hundreds, and hundreds,

00:57:41.090 --> 00:57:42.132 align:middle line:90%
and hundreds of products.

00:57:42.132 --> 00:57:52.400 align:middle line:84%
So it's selling 17 PC100
128-megabyte modules per website

00:57:52.400 --> 00:57:53.220 align:middle line:90%
per day.

00:57:53.220 --> 00:57:56.090 align:middle line:84%
So it's selling 17 on website
A. It's selling 17 on website B.

00:57:56.090 --> 00:57:59.870 align:middle line:84%
But then it's selling
many 256-megabyte modules,

00:57:59.870 --> 00:58:03.930 align:middle line:84%
and many PC133 modules, and
many PC whatever modules.

00:58:03.930 --> 00:58:06.440 align:middle line:84%
So this is one of
many, many products,

00:58:06.440 --> 00:58:08.510 align:middle line:84%
but we just pick out
this daily sales data,

00:58:08.510 --> 00:58:11.880 align:middle line:84%
because this is the one that
had things bought consistently.

00:58:11.880 --> 00:58:14.532 align:middle line:90%


00:58:14.532 --> 00:58:16.240 align:middle line:84%
The main thing we want
to do in the paper

00:58:16.240 --> 00:58:20.230 align:middle line:84%
is just analyze what
is the demand curve.

00:58:20.230 --> 00:58:23.140 align:middle line:84%
With all the obfuscation
out there, competition

00:58:23.140 --> 00:58:27.730 align:middle line:84%
between the sites, the
attempt of this website

00:58:27.730 --> 00:58:30.130 align:middle line:84%
to convince people
to buy upgrades,

00:58:30.130 --> 00:58:32.740 align:middle line:84%
what does demand look like and
what do all the cross-price

00:58:32.740 --> 00:58:34.720 align:middle line:90%
elasticities look like?

00:58:34.720 --> 00:58:36.850 align:middle line:84%
We do this in a
very simple thing.

00:58:36.850 --> 00:58:41.230 align:middle line:84%
I think what we would like
to do is, in some sense,

00:58:41.230 --> 00:58:47.650 align:middle line:84%
we want to just regress log Q
equals alpha 0 minus alpha 1 log

00:58:47.650 --> 00:58:51.790 align:middle line:90%
P and so on.

00:58:51.790 --> 00:58:54.280 align:middle line:84%
You can't run a
regression of log Q

00:58:54.280 --> 00:58:58.300 align:middle line:84%
on log P when the Q's are
discrete things that are often

00:58:58.300 --> 00:58:59.290 align:middle line:90%
0.

00:58:59.290 --> 00:59:01.725 align:middle line:84%
So what we do instead
is, in some sense, the--

00:59:01.725 --> 00:59:05.040 align:middle line:90%


00:59:05.040 --> 00:59:09.810 align:middle line:84%
you can think of it as log Q
equals x beta plus epsilon.

00:59:09.810 --> 00:59:13.020 align:middle line:84%
Instead of running log Q
equals x beta plus epsilon,

00:59:13.020 --> 00:59:15.160 align:middle line:90%
we just exponentiate both sides.

00:59:15.160 --> 00:59:17.970 align:middle line:84%
We'll have Q equals
e to the x beta times

00:59:17.970 --> 00:59:21.750 align:middle line:84%
u, where u is a multiplicative
shock with expectation

00:59:21.750 --> 00:59:25.230 align:middle line:90%
of u given x equals 1.

00:59:25.230 --> 00:59:27.210 align:middle line:90%
What is on the right side?

00:59:27.210 --> 00:59:31.440 align:middle line:90%
So we're regressing log Q on--

00:59:31.440 --> 00:59:33.407 align:middle line:84%
the log of the price
of low quality memory,

00:59:33.407 --> 00:59:35.490 align:middle line:84%
the log of the price of
the medium quality memory,

00:59:35.490 --> 00:59:38.660 align:middle line:84%
the log of the price
of high quality memory,

00:59:38.660 --> 00:59:40.167 align:middle line:90%
other covariates.

00:59:40.167 --> 00:59:41.750 align:middle line:84%
Also, particularly
what we've included

00:59:41.750 --> 00:59:44.270 align:middle line:84%
to control for
the sales is, what

00:59:44.270 --> 00:59:46.980 align:middle line:84%
is the rank of your low
quality memory on the screen?

00:59:46.980 --> 00:59:51.240 align:middle line:84%
This turns out to be a
very powerful predictor.

00:59:51.240 --> 00:59:53.090 align:middle line:84%
If you're in the first
spot on that screen,

00:59:53.090 --> 00:59:54.440 align:middle line:84%
you sell much more
memory than if you're

00:59:54.440 --> 00:59:56.880 align:middle line:84%
in the second, or third, or
fourth spot on the screen.

00:59:56.880 --> 01:00:01.130 align:middle line:84%
This is, in some sense, how we
control for rivals' prices is

01:00:01.130 --> 01:00:07.070 align:middle line:84%
I'm regressing sales at website
A of medium quality memory

01:00:07.070 --> 01:00:10.070 align:middle line:84%
on the prices at website A for
low, medium, and high quality

01:00:10.070 --> 01:00:10.920 align:middle line:90%
memory.

01:00:10.920 --> 01:00:13.550 align:middle line:84%
And then I'm controlling
for competitors' prices

01:00:13.550 --> 01:00:14.887 align:middle line:90%
through these two variables.

01:00:14.887 --> 01:00:16.470 align:middle line:84%
What's the lowest
price on the screen?

01:00:16.470 --> 01:00:18.080 align:middle line:90%
How high am I relative to that?

01:00:18.080 --> 01:00:21.630 align:middle line:84%
And what's my rank
on that screen?

01:00:21.630 --> 01:00:24.170 align:middle line:84%
So in some sense, you
might want to include

01:00:24.170 --> 01:00:25.337 align:middle line:90%
all the competitors' prices.

01:00:25.337 --> 01:00:26.878 align:middle line:84%
That would, obviously,
be infeasible.

01:00:26.878 --> 01:00:29.150 align:middle line:84%
So I include, what's your
rank on the screen, as well

01:00:29.150 --> 01:00:30.690 align:middle line:90%
as my prices here.

01:00:30.690 --> 01:00:33.860 align:middle line:84%
And notice that I'm putting low,
medium, and high quality prices

01:00:33.860 --> 01:00:35.280 align:middle line:90%
on the right side.

01:00:35.280 --> 01:00:37.790 align:middle line:84%
Whether the left side
has low quality memory

01:00:37.790 --> 01:00:40.680 align:middle line:84%
as a dependent
variable, medium quality

01:00:40.680 --> 01:00:43.270 align:middle line:84%
memory, or high quality memory
as a dependent variable.

01:00:43.270 --> 01:00:45.930 align:middle line:84%
And I'm doing that
because people

01:00:45.930 --> 01:00:48.880 align:middle line:84%
are going to substitute
between the firm's offerings.

01:00:48.880 --> 01:00:50.670 align:middle line:84%
If it's charging
$25, the upgrade

01:00:50.670 --> 01:00:52.503 align:middle line:84%
is going to sell fewer
upgrades than if it's

01:00:52.503 --> 01:00:53.850 align:middle line:90%
charging $10 for the upgrade.

01:00:53.850 --> 01:00:57.990 align:middle line:84%
It'll sell many fewer if it's
charging $50 for the upgrade.

01:00:57.990 --> 01:01:01.080 align:middle line:84%
Our primary
specification is just

01:01:01.080 --> 01:01:04.140 align:middle line:84%
basically doing
the OLS equivalent,

01:01:04.140 --> 01:01:06.030 align:middle line:84%
treating the prices
on the right side

01:01:06.030 --> 01:01:09.490 align:middle line:84%
as exogenous for the
reasons I explained earlier.

01:01:09.490 --> 01:01:13.140 align:middle line:84%
Just expectation of u
given the x is equal to 1.

01:01:13.140 --> 01:01:17.820 align:middle line:84%
We do also in the paper have a
couple different IV strategies.

01:01:17.820 --> 01:01:22.170 align:middle line:84%
One IV strategy is using the
costs as instrument for prices.

01:01:22.170 --> 01:01:25.230 align:middle line:84%
Costs are a classic
instrument for prices.

01:01:25.230 --> 01:01:29.580 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, we use that wholesale
cost data to get an IV.

01:01:29.580 --> 01:01:32.430 align:middle line:84%
The other thing we do
is also use the prices

01:01:32.430 --> 01:01:34.650 align:middle line:84%
for other products
as instrument IV.

01:01:34.650 --> 01:01:37.530 align:middle line:84%
The explanation for that
one is if you imagine

01:01:37.530 --> 01:01:39.180 align:middle line:90%
the firm comes in for the day.

01:01:39.180 --> 01:01:42.480 align:middle line:84%
It sees that two of
its warehouse employees

01:01:42.480 --> 01:01:45.420 align:middle line:84%
call in sick, which is not
at all an uncommon thing

01:01:45.420 --> 01:01:47.640 align:middle line:84%
when you're paying
minimum wage workers.

01:01:47.640 --> 01:01:50.430 align:middle line:84%
If two of your workers are
sick, what you do is you know,

01:01:50.430 --> 01:01:52.380 align:middle line:90%
you can't package as many boxes.

01:01:52.380 --> 01:01:55.440 align:middle line:84%
So you just try to sell
fewer goods that day

01:01:55.440 --> 01:01:57.280 align:middle line:90%
by raising your prices.

01:01:57.280 --> 01:02:00.330 align:middle line:84%
You raise your prices, sell
a few items at a high markup

01:02:00.330 --> 01:02:02.440 align:middle line:84%
rather than a lot of
items at a low markup.

01:02:02.440 --> 01:02:05.970 align:middle line:84%
And so I can use
the PC133 prices

01:02:05.970 --> 01:02:08.230 align:middle line:84%
as an instrument
for PC100 prices,

01:02:08.230 --> 01:02:09.737 align:middle line:84%
because if your
employees are sick,

01:02:09.737 --> 01:02:11.320 align:middle line:84%
you would probably
raise both of them.

01:02:11.320 --> 01:02:13.830 align:middle line:84%
And obviously, the
PC133 prices aren't

01:02:13.830 --> 01:02:16.230 align:middle line:84%
going to reflect demand
for PC100, which is

01:02:16.230 --> 01:02:18.127 align:middle line:90%
bought by separate consumers.

01:02:18.127 --> 01:02:19.710 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, I think
mostly we're going

01:02:19.710 --> 01:02:23.220 align:middle line:84%
to say we think this is a case
where within the distribution

01:02:23.220 --> 01:02:25.810 align:middle line:84%
price variation really
is as good as random.

01:02:25.810 --> 01:02:29.580 align:middle line:84%
And we're mostly going to
estimate this by, well, its GMM,

01:02:29.580 --> 01:02:31.530 align:middle line:84%
but it's like OLS, where
we're just treating

01:02:31.530 --> 01:02:34.830 align:middle line:90%
those prices as exogenous.

01:02:34.830 --> 01:02:36.080 align:middle line:90%
What are some of the findings?

01:02:36.080 --> 01:02:40.420 align:middle line:84%
So first, demand for
low quality memory

01:02:40.420 --> 01:02:42.940 align:middle line:90%
is very, very price sensitive.

01:02:42.940 --> 01:02:46.840 align:middle line:84%
If your rank changes
from 1 to 2 to 3,

01:02:46.840 --> 01:02:48.880 align:middle line:84%
every time you drop
down on that list,

01:02:48.880 --> 01:02:52.380 align:middle line:90%
you sell many, many fewer units.

01:02:52.380 --> 01:02:56.630 align:middle line:84%
We're going to report that price
elasticities are as high as 33.

01:02:56.630 --> 01:03:00.820 align:middle line:84%
So you raise your prices
by 1% from $100 to $101,

01:03:00.820 --> 01:03:02.600 align:middle line:84%
a third of your
demand goes away.

01:03:02.600 --> 01:03:04.420 align:middle line:84%
You raise price
from $101 to $102,

01:03:04.420 --> 01:03:06.760 align:middle line:84%
another third of your
demand goes away again.

01:03:06.760 --> 01:03:08.780 align:middle line:84%
$102 to $103, a third of
your demand goes away.

01:03:08.780 --> 01:03:11.950 align:middle line:84%
So it's just incredibly price
sensitive demand for these firms

01:03:11.950 --> 01:03:13.610 align:middle line:90%
caused by this rank effect.

01:03:13.610 --> 01:03:17.906 align:middle line:84%
As I go from 1 to 2 to 3 to 4,
I just keep losing customers.

01:03:17.906 --> 01:03:20.460 align:middle line:90%


01:03:20.460 --> 01:03:23.690 align:middle line:84%
The second thing is that--
interesting finding is there's

01:03:23.690 --> 01:03:25.440 align:middle line:90%
a big loss leader effect here.

01:03:25.440 --> 01:03:29.810 align:middle line:84%
So I'm selling this low quality
memory basically at cost.

01:03:29.810 --> 01:03:33.680 align:middle line:84%
But when I'm-- by selling
this low quality memory,

01:03:33.680 --> 01:03:35.360 align:middle line:90%
I get to the top of the list.

01:03:35.360 --> 01:03:36.470 align:middle line:90%
And being at the top--

01:03:36.470 --> 01:03:38.262 align:middle line:90%
moving down the list--

01:03:38.262 --> 01:03:39.720 align:middle line:84%
when I move to the
top of the list,

01:03:39.720 --> 01:03:42.150 align:middle line:84%
I not only sell much
more low quality memory.

01:03:42.150 --> 01:03:44.190 align:middle line:84%
I also am able to talk
some of those people

01:03:44.190 --> 01:03:46.607 align:middle line:84%
into buying medium quality
memory and some of those people

01:03:46.607 --> 01:03:47.690 align:middle line:90%
into high quality memory.

01:03:47.690 --> 01:03:50.400 align:middle line:84%
So being high on
the screen, I sell

01:03:50.400 --> 01:03:53.070 align:middle line:84%
more of the product that
makes my price high,

01:03:53.070 --> 01:03:55.750 align:middle line:84%
but holding the price of the
medium quality memory fixed,

01:03:55.750 --> 01:03:58.170 align:middle line:84%
having a low price
for low quality memory

01:03:58.170 --> 01:03:59.850 align:middle line:90%
has this big loss leader effect.

01:03:59.850 --> 01:04:02.220 align:middle line:84%
And I sell much more
medium quality memory,

01:04:02.220 --> 01:04:04.080 align:middle line:84%
and much more high
quality memory

01:04:04.080 --> 01:04:07.310 align:middle line:84%
when I'm up at the
top of that list.

01:04:07.310 --> 01:04:09.050 align:middle line:84%
And this, again,
it's controlling

01:04:09.050 --> 01:04:11.493 align:middle line:84%
for the price of my medium
quality memory itself.

01:04:11.493 --> 01:04:13.910 align:middle line:84%
So it's holding that price of
medium quality memory fixed.

01:04:13.910 --> 01:04:15.785 align:middle line:84%
When I set a low price
on low quality memory,

01:04:15.785 --> 01:04:17.868 align:middle line:84%
I just sell much more of
the medium quality memory

01:04:17.868 --> 01:04:19.540 align:middle line:84%
because I'm drawing
people to my site.

01:04:19.540 --> 01:04:22.310 align:middle line:90%


01:04:22.310 --> 01:04:26.600 align:middle line:84%
I mentioned that the reason
add-on pricing can raise profits

01:04:26.600 --> 01:04:29.180 align:middle line:84%
and equilibrium a
few lectures ago

01:04:29.180 --> 01:04:32.280 align:middle line:84%
is because of this
adverse selection effect.

01:04:32.280 --> 01:04:35.100 align:middle line:84%
When the cheapskates
who come to your website

01:04:35.100 --> 01:04:37.920 align:middle line:84%
to buy the low quality product
won't buy the upgrades,

01:04:37.920 --> 01:04:41.170 align:middle line:84%
you don't want those cheapskates
buying stuff at a loss.

01:04:41.170 --> 01:04:43.920 align:middle line:84%
And so that causes firms to
have this incentive, flipping

01:04:43.920 --> 01:04:45.870 align:middle line:84%
from undercutting
to overcutting,

01:04:45.870 --> 01:04:48.400 align:middle line:84%
and raises equilibrium
price levels.

01:04:48.400 --> 01:04:51.370 align:middle line:84%
Here we're finding evidence of
that adverse selection effect.

01:04:51.370 --> 01:04:54.570 align:middle line:84%
So increasing this
variable by one

01:04:54.570 --> 01:04:58.830 align:middle line:84%
raises low quality sales by
129%, medium quality sales

01:04:58.830 --> 01:05:03.120 align:middle line:84%
by 77%, high quality
sales by 51%.

01:05:03.120 --> 01:05:06.280 align:middle line:84%
So I'm selling more medium
and high quality memory.

01:05:06.280 --> 01:05:09.090 align:middle line:84%
My product mix is getting
worse, and worse, and worse,

01:05:09.090 --> 01:05:12.540 align:middle line:84%
and a larger and larger
fraction of my sales

01:05:12.540 --> 01:05:17.080 align:middle line:84%
are the low quality, low profit
goods as I move up on the list.

01:05:17.080 --> 01:05:19.500 align:middle line:84%
So this is the adverse
selection effect

01:05:19.500 --> 01:05:23.070 align:middle line:84%
we talked about, that
it is clear evidence

01:05:23.070 --> 01:05:25.280 align:middle line:84%
that adverse selection
effect exists in this market.

01:05:25.280 --> 01:05:28.268 align:middle line:90%


01:05:28.268 --> 01:05:30.800 align:middle line:84%
In addition to
these rank effects,

01:05:30.800 --> 01:05:33.440 align:middle line:84%
the own price effects
are significant as well.

01:05:33.440 --> 01:05:36.200 align:middle line:84%
So when I raise my price
for medium quality memory,

01:05:36.200 --> 01:05:37.980 align:middle line:84%
I do sell less medium
quality memory.

01:05:37.980 --> 01:05:41.300 align:middle line:84%
When I raise my price for high
quality memory, I do sell less.

01:05:41.300 --> 01:05:43.530 align:middle line:84%
Those elasticities
are like 5 or 6.

01:05:43.530 --> 01:05:48.090 align:middle line:84%
So if I raise my medium quality
price by $1, I sell 5% less.

01:05:48.090 --> 01:05:49.250 align:middle line:90%
I raise it by $5.

01:05:49.250 --> 01:05:53.610 align:middle line:84%
I sell-- sorry-- by
5%, I sell 25% less.

01:05:53.610 --> 01:05:55.520 align:middle line:84%
So this does appear
to be substitution

01:05:55.520 --> 01:05:59.870 align:middle line:84%
within the website, where if
those upgrades are 15, 30,

01:05:59.870 --> 01:06:02.510 align:middle line:84%
or those upgrades are 30,
50, I sell much fewer when

01:06:02.510 --> 01:06:06.620 align:middle line:90%
I have higher prices.

01:06:06.620 --> 01:06:13.370 align:middle line:84%
So when we add together these
two effects, the rank effects

01:06:13.370 --> 01:06:16.383 align:middle line:84%
and the own price effects,
this is giving us the effects

01:06:16.383 --> 01:06:17.550 align:middle line:90%
of the different categories.

01:06:17.550 --> 01:06:20.220 align:middle line:84%
But I'll focus on the
128 megabyte PC100.

01:06:20.220 --> 01:06:23.180 align:middle line:84%
What I get is this
incredibly elastic demand

01:06:23.180 --> 01:06:27.810 align:middle line:84%
for the low quality memory,
and then somewhat elastic cost

01:06:27.810 --> 01:06:29.730 align:middle line:90%
for the other memory.

01:06:29.730 --> 01:06:32.280 align:middle line:84%
I get these two notable
off-diagonal elements

01:06:32.280 --> 01:06:34.470 align:middle line:84%
saying that there's
that loss leader effect.

01:06:34.470 --> 01:06:36.180 align:middle line:84%
The other off-diagonal
ones down here

01:06:36.180 --> 01:06:37.920 align:middle line:84%
are positive, as
you would expect.

01:06:37.920 --> 01:06:40.980 align:middle line:84%
When I raise my price on
medium quality memory,

01:06:40.980 --> 01:06:43.308 align:middle line:84%
I sell a little bit
more low quality memory,

01:06:43.308 --> 01:06:45.850 align:middle line:84%
because we'll substitute away
from the medium quality memory.

01:06:45.850 --> 01:06:47.850 align:middle line:84%
I'll sell a little bit
more high quality memory.

01:06:47.850 --> 01:06:49.505 align:middle line:84%
But again, these
are small effects.

01:06:49.505 --> 01:06:54.150 align:middle line:90%


01:06:54.150 --> 01:06:56.280 align:middle line:90%
I said that.

01:06:56.280 --> 01:06:58.830 align:middle line:84%
Those are the loss
leader effects.

01:06:58.830 --> 01:06:59.945 align:middle line:90%
I also said that.

01:06:59.945 --> 01:07:03.570 align:middle line:90%


01:07:03.570 --> 01:07:05.060 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so we've
got this-- again,

01:07:05.060 --> 01:07:07.730 align:middle line:84%
just summarizing this
super elastic demand

01:07:07.730 --> 01:07:11.150 align:middle line:84%
for the low quality memory
and price sensitive, but not

01:07:11.150 --> 01:07:14.833 align:middle line:84%
nearly as elastic demand for the
medium and high quality memory.

01:07:14.833 --> 01:07:16.250 align:middle line:84%
Naively, this is
going to make you

01:07:16.250 --> 01:07:17.360 align:middle line:84%
think that these
products are going

01:07:17.360 --> 01:07:19.850 align:middle line:84%
to be sold at higher markups
because demand is less price

01:07:19.850 --> 01:07:23.140 align:middle line:90%
sensitive.

01:07:23.140 --> 01:07:26.890 align:middle line:84%
Question now-- I think the
paper's biggest contribution is

01:07:26.890 --> 01:07:32.570 align:middle line:84%
saying, we know that this add-on
pricing theory is correct.

01:07:32.570 --> 01:07:34.360 align:middle line:84%
If you sell products,
if you have--

01:07:34.360 --> 01:07:38.740 align:middle line:84%
get people to compete by
only advertising low quality

01:07:38.740 --> 01:07:40.210 align:middle line:90%
products, and then having to--

01:07:40.210 --> 01:07:43.027 align:middle line:84%
selling the add-ons at
high monopoly prices,

01:07:43.027 --> 01:07:44.860 align:middle line:84%
we're going to get
higher equilibrium prices

01:07:44.860 --> 01:07:47.352 align:middle line:90%
than we would get without that.

01:07:47.352 --> 01:07:52.490 align:middle line:84%
The question is, how large is
that effect quantitatively?

01:07:52.490 --> 01:07:55.420 align:middle line:84%
Does that affect--
is that effect large

01:07:55.420 --> 01:07:57.560 align:middle line:84%
enough quantitatively to
explain what's going on?

01:07:57.560 --> 01:07:59.570 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, how big
are the markups here?

01:07:59.570 --> 01:08:02.710 align:middle line:84%
Are they explained by this
add-on pricing theory?

01:08:02.710 --> 01:08:06.130 align:middle line:84%
To do that, the paper has a
generalization of what was

01:08:06.130 --> 01:08:10.930 align:middle line:84%
in the QJE paper, noting that a
formula for price cost margins--

01:08:10.930 --> 01:08:14.920 align:middle line:84%
if you remember, standard
single good price--

01:08:14.920 --> 01:08:19.359 align:middle line:84%
standard single good
monopoly pricing

01:08:19.359 --> 01:08:23.050 align:middle line:84%
is price minus cost over
price equals minus 1

01:08:23.050 --> 01:08:26.109 align:middle line:90%
over the elasticity of demand.

01:08:26.109 --> 01:08:30.979 align:middle line:84%
What I note here is that there's
a generalization of that model,

01:08:30.979 --> 01:08:33.859 align:middle line:84%
of that formula to a situation
where you have a firm selling

01:08:33.859 --> 01:08:36.529 align:middle line:90%
both a base good and an add-on.

01:08:36.529 --> 01:08:40.399 align:middle line:84%
And what this is, so
this is a generalization

01:08:40.399 --> 01:08:41.700 align:middle line:90%
of the price cost margin.

01:08:41.700 --> 01:08:44.540 align:middle line:84%
So this is the price
for low quality memory

01:08:44.540 --> 01:08:47.750 align:middle line:84%
plus the x is going
to be the fraction who

01:08:47.750 --> 01:08:50.930 align:middle line:84%
upgrade when you're competing
against another firm,

01:08:50.930 --> 01:08:52.189 align:middle line:90%
and your price is p1L.

01:08:52.189 --> 01:08:53.899 align:middle line:90%
The other firm's price is p2L.

01:08:53.899 --> 01:08:58.010 align:middle line:84%
So this is price plus fractional
upgrade times the margin

01:08:58.010 --> 01:08:59.430 align:middle line:90%
times the upgrade price.

01:08:59.430 --> 01:09:02.720 align:middle line:84%
You can think of this as
what's your equilibrium price

01:09:02.720 --> 01:09:04.573 align:middle line:84%
per customer,
taking into account

01:09:04.573 --> 01:09:05.990 align:middle line:84%
that some pay more
than they think

01:09:05.990 --> 01:09:08.420 align:middle line:84%
they're going to pay when
they click on that link,

01:09:08.420 --> 01:09:10.729 align:middle line:84%
minus the cost of
providing that good,

01:09:10.729 --> 01:09:12.643 align:middle line:84%
which is the cost of
low quality memory,

01:09:12.643 --> 01:09:14.810 align:middle line:84%
plus the fractional upgrade
times the cost of buying

01:09:14.810 --> 01:09:15.899 align:middle line:90%
the upgrade.

01:09:15.899 --> 01:09:17.850 align:middle line:84%
And then, again,
this is the price.

01:09:17.850 --> 01:09:20.300 align:middle line:84%
So it's just price
minus cost over price,

01:09:20.300 --> 01:09:23.060 align:middle line:84%
where in price, I've included
the fraction upgrading

01:09:23.060 --> 01:09:24.210 align:middle line:90%
times the upgrade price.

01:09:24.210 --> 01:09:26.760 align:middle line:84%
So this is per
consumer amount paid.

01:09:26.760 --> 01:09:31.580 align:middle line:84%
What you get is that price
minus cost over price is minus 1

01:09:31.580 --> 01:09:35.250 align:middle line:84%
over epsilon times
1 plus something.

01:09:35.250 --> 01:09:37.550 align:middle line:84%
And I sometimes refer to
this as the adverse selection

01:09:37.550 --> 01:09:38.729 align:middle line:90%
multiplier.

01:09:38.729 --> 01:09:41.640 align:middle line:84%
The worse is the adverse
selection multiplier,

01:09:41.640 --> 01:09:46.410 align:middle line:84%
the higher is your markup
relative to 1 over epsilon.

01:09:46.410 --> 01:09:49.580 align:middle line:84%
The key term here
is this one, which

01:09:49.580 --> 01:09:53.779 align:middle line:84%
is, what's the upgrade
profit times how

01:09:53.779 --> 01:09:58.280 align:middle line:84%
does the fraction who
choose to upgrade increase

01:09:58.280 --> 01:10:00.150 align:middle line:90%
when your price increases?

01:10:00.150 --> 01:10:02.210 align:middle line:84%
So as you get further
down on the screen,

01:10:02.210 --> 01:10:03.950 align:middle line:84%
have less of an
adverse selection,

01:10:03.950 --> 01:10:06.780 align:middle line:84%
how much more successful
are you at selling upgrades?

01:10:06.780 --> 01:10:10.820 align:middle line:84%
And this formula predicts
that as the adverse selection

01:10:10.820 --> 01:10:13.280 align:middle line:84%
problem gets worse,
the dx / dp is bigger.

01:10:13.280 --> 01:10:15.720 align:middle line:84%
And the p upgrade
minus cu is bigger.

01:10:15.720 --> 01:10:18.500 align:middle line:84%
You're going to have a larger
margin relative to the minus 1

01:10:18.500 --> 01:10:22.010 align:middle line:84%
over epsilon, which is the
naive level you would expect

01:10:22.010 --> 01:10:24.260 align:middle line:90%
from the single good intuition.

01:10:24.260 --> 01:10:27.260 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so the paper
shows that this is true

01:10:27.260 --> 01:10:30.320 align:middle line:84%
if you define epsilon
to be this thing we call

01:10:30.320 --> 01:10:32.390 align:middle line:84%
the overall
elasticity, which looks

01:10:32.390 --> 01:10:36.140 align:middle line:84%
an awful lot like a
derivative of low quality

01:10:36.140 --> 01:10:38.185 align:middle line:84%
demand with respect
to low quality price.

01:10:38.185 --> 01:10:42.950 align:middle line:90%


01:10:42.950 --> 01:10:44.630 align:middle line:84%
What we do is--
again, this paper

01:10:44.630 --> 01:10:47.600 align:middle line:84%
is unusual in that we
have this cost data.

01:10:47.600 --> 01:10:50.900 align:middle line:84%
So we have the daily cost
for each of these modules.

01:10:50.900 --> 01:10:53.750 align:middle line:84%
We also know just from talking
to the proprietor of the firm,

01:10:53.750 --> 01:10:55.190 align:middle line:90%
what does it cost--

01:10:55.190 --> 01:10:56.673 align:middle line:90%
it's not rocket science.

01:10:56.673 --> 01:10:58.340 align:middle line:84%
What does it cost to
hire a minimum wage

01:10:58.340 --> 01:11:01.130 align:middle line:84%
worker to take the memory
module off the shelf,

01:11:01.130 --> 01:11:04.460 align:middle line:84%
put it in a cardboard box,
put one piece of tape on it,

01:11:04.460 --> 01:11:06.230 align:middle line:84%
run it through
the postage meter,

01:11:06.230 --> 01:11:07.682 align:middle line:90%
and send it to the customer?

01:11:07.682 --> 01:11:09.140 align:middle line:84%
And so the firm
was able to give us

01:11:09.140 --> 01:11:12.740 align:middle line:84%
very good estimates for what
is the cost of sending a memory

01:11:12.740 --> 01:11:13.340 align:middle line:90%
module--

01:11:13.340 --> 01:11:17.120 align:middle line:84%
of packing a memory module into
a cardboard box and sending it.

01:11:17.120 --> 01:11:19.470 align:middle line:84%
So we're able to
estimate markups here.

01:11:19.470 --> 01:11:23.540 align:middle line:84%
And what we find is that,
qualitatively, these loss

01:11:23.540 --> 01:11:25.970 align:middle line:84%
leaders, the low quality
memory, it's being-- it actually

01:11:25.970 --> 01:11:28.140 align:middle line:84%
is being sold at a
loss most of the time.

01:11:28.140 --> 01:11:32.210 align:middle line:84%
So here it's approximately
zero markups.

01:11:32.210 --> 01:11:36.630 align:middle line:84%
But low quality memory's
average markup is minus 0.7%.

01:11:36.630 --> 01:11:39.270 align:middle line:84%
Medium quality memory
has got a 17% markup.

01:11:39.270 --> 01:11:44.050 align:middle line:84%
High quality memory
has got a 27% markup.

01:11:44.050 --> 01:11:47.340 align:middle line:84%
So overall, when you add up the
12 units of this, two of this,

01:11:47.340 --> 01:11:52.360 align:middle line:84%
two of this, the
average mark up is 7%.

01:11:52.360 --> 01:11:53.830 align:middle line:84%
How does that
compare with what you

01:11:53.830 --> 01:11:57.220 align:middle line:84%
would have expected had
this add-on strategy not

01:11:57.220 --> 01:11:58.070 align:middle line:90%
been going on?

01:11:58.070 --> 01:12:04.010 align:middle line:84%
Well, the overall
elasticity is minus 23.9.

01:12:04.010 --> 01:12:07.180 align:middle line:84%
So if you had the
simple price minus cost

01:12:07.180 --> 01:12:09.400 align:middle line:84%
over price is 1 over
epsilon pricing,

01:12:09.400 --> 01:12:13.660 align:middle line:90%
you'd expect a 4.2% markup.

01:12:13.660 --> 01:12:17.080 align:middle line:90%
And what we see is 7.7.

01:12:17.080 --> 01:12:20.750 align:middle line:84%
Can we explain why we're
seeing 7.7 instead of 4.2?

01:12:20.750 --> 01:12:24.250 align:middle line:84%
Well, if each of the things
in this adverse selection

01:12:24.250 --> 01:12:26.740 align:middle line:84%
multiplier are also
things that we're

01:12:26.740 --> 01:12:28.660 align:middle line:90%
estimating our demand system.

01:12:28.660 --> 01:12:32.740 align:middle line:84%
So when I estimate those,
I find that my estimate

01:12:32.740 --> 01:12:35.300 align:middle line:84%
for the adverse selection
multiplier is two.

01:12:35.300 --> 01:12:38.770 align:middle line:84%
So when we estimated
this demand system

01:12:38.770 --> 01:12:41.020 align:middle line:84%
without using any
supply side information,

01:12:41.020 --> 01:12:43.990 align:middle line:84%
without imposing that the
firms were maximizing profits,

01:12:43.990 --> 01:12:46.450 align:middle line:84%
and yet what we find is that
when we look at the estimated

01:12:46.450 --> 01:12:49.890 align:middle line:84%
parameters, with the
estimated parameters,

01:12:49.890 --> 01:12:52.380 align:middle line:84%
the equilibrium
markup prediction

01:12:52.380 --> 01:12:54.745 align:middle line:84%
is almost exactly the
true markup prediction.

01:12:54.745 --> 01:12:57.120 align:middle line:84%
So this is saying that, we
estimate the adverse selection

01:12:57.120 --> 01:12:58.530 align:middle line:90%
multiplier is two.

01:12:58.530 --> 01:13:01.860 align:middle line:84%
And that does seem to explain
why profits margins are

01:13:01.860 --> 01:13:04.650 align:middle line:90%
7% instead of 4.2%.

01:13:04.650 --> 01:13:07.180 align:middle line:84%
We do this across several
different categories.

01:13:07.180 --> 01:13:09.720 align:middle line:84%
It predicts that the adverse
selection is even more

01:13:09.720 --> 01:13:12.160 align:middle line:90%
important for PC133 memory.

01:13:12.160 --> 01:13:15.230 align:middle line:84%
It predicts that it's about
the same for the 256 megabyte

01:13:15.230 --> 01:13:15.730 align:middle line:90%
memory.

01:13:15.730 --> 01:13:20.730 align:middle line:84%
And in each case, we find that
the 1 over epsilon formula

01:13:20.730 --> 01:13:22.920 align:middle line:90%
would say you'd get 3.6% markup.

01:13:22.920 --> 01:13:25.480 align:middle line:90%
We actually get an 11.5% markup.

01:13:25.480 --> 01:13:28.170 align:middle line:84%
But that's perfectly explained
by this adverse selection

01:13:28.170 --> 01:13:32.400 align:middle line:84%
multiplier that says, yes, it
should multiply markups by 3.5%.

01:13:32.400 --> 01:13:34.650 align:middle line:84%
So I think the basic
takeaway from this paper

01:13:34.650 --> 01:13:39.680 align:middle line:84%
is that one technique that
firms are using for obfuscation

01:13:39.680 --> 01:13:44.100 align:middle line:84%
here is, well, one technique
is making things annoying.

01:13:44.100 --> 01:13:47.960 align:middle line:84%
That's going into what
determines these--

01:13:47.960 --> 01:13:49.940 align:middle line:84%
what determines the
sensitivity to rank

01:13:49.940 --> 01:13:51.295 align:middle line:90%
and how quickly people give up.

01:13:51.295 --> 01:13:53.480 align:middle line:84%
The second thing that's
going into margins

01:13:53.480 --> 01:13:57.890 align:middle line:84%
is creating this
adverse selection effect

01:13:57.890 --> 01:13:59.390 align:middle line:90%
by offering add-ons.

01:13:59.390 --> 01:14:02.180 align:middle line:84%
And the amount by
which markups go

01:14:02.180 --> 01:14:04.490 align:middle line:84%
up relative to the
single good model

01:14:04.490 --> 01:14:06.203 align:middle line:84%
seems to be almost
exactly in line

01:14:06.203 --> 01:14:07.620 align:middle line:84%
with the adverse
selection effect.

01:14:07.620 --> 01:14:08.990 align:middle line:84%
So it seems like,
in some sense, we

01:14:08.990 --> 01:14:11.157 align:middle line:84%
don't need anything other
than the adverse selection

01:14:11.157 --> 01:14:12.890 align:middle line:84%
effect and the level
of search costs

01:14:12.890 --> 01:14:14.760 align:middle line:90%
to explain what's going on here.

01:14:14.760 --> 01:14:18.360 align:middle line:84%
It does seem like the adverse
selection has an effect.

01:14:18.360 --> 01:14:21.300 align:middle line:84%
It has an effect as big as
we would expect it to be.

01:14:21.300 --> 01:14:25.280 align:middle line:84%
And it's not a puzzle why
markups in this market

01:14:25.280 --> 01:14:30.290 align:middle line:84%
end up what they are, which is
markups are quite low, but not

01:14:30.290 --> 01:14:33.080 align:middle line:84%
so low that these firms,
which are bare bones

01:14:33.080 --> 01:14:35.320 align:middle line:84%
firms with no advertising,
can't survive.

01:14:35.320 --> 01:14:40.350 align:middle line:90%


01:14:40.350 --> 01:14:41.900 align:middle line:84%
And I think the
rest of these slides

01:14:41.900 --> 01:14:43.560 align:middle line:84%
just have the same
things I just said.

01:14:43.560 --> 01:14:46.250 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, here's where
you see the low quality

01:14:46.250 --> 01:14:49.520 align:middle line:84%
memory has got negative, and
the others have positive.

01:14:49.520 --> 01:14:51.750 align:middle line:84%
There's the adverse
selection effect.

01:14:51.750 --> 01:14:55.370 align:middle line:90%
There are the predictions.

01:14:55.370 --> 01:14:58.490 align:middle line:84%
And then this summary
of the conclusions

01:14:58.490 --> 01:15:00.793 align:middle line:84%
that I told you that
observed markups

01:15:00.793 --> 01:15:02.960 align:middle line:84%
are very close to ones you'd
predict from the demand

01:15:02.960 --> 01:15:04.580 align:middle line:90%
estimates.

01:15:04.580 --> 01:15:07.760 align:middle line:84%
Adverse selection is roughly
doubling average markups.

01:15:07.760 --> 01:15:11.150 align:middle line:84%
And markups for low quality
products are very low.

01:15:11.150 --> 01:15:15.710 align:middle line:84%
So in my final 15 minutes, I'm
going to give my final paper,

01:15:15.710 --> 01:15:19.093 align:middle line:84%
which is Galenianos and
Gavazza's paper about

01:15:19.093 --> 01:15:21.260 align:middle line:84%
"Regulatory Intervention
in Consumer Search Markets,

01:15:21.260 --> 01:15:23.000 align:middle line:90%
The Case of Credit Cards."

01:15:23.000 --> 01:15:24.740 align:middle line:84%
One thing that I often
do in this course

01:15:24.740 --> 01:15:27.200 align:middle line:84%
is tell students
that it's a mistake

01:15:27.200 --> 01:15:29.420 align:middle line:84%
to be too ambitious when
you're trying to come up

01:15:29.420 --> 01:15:31.100 align:middle line:84%
with paper topics,
or you don't need

01:15:31.100 --> 01:15:33.650 align:middle line:90%
to be so original and creative.

01:15:33.650 --> 01:15:35.810 align:middle line:84%
It's a great idea to just
take someone else's paper

01:15:35.810 --> 01:15:37.460 align:middle line:84%
and improve it, because
if you take someone who's

01:15:37.460 --> 01:15:39.790 align:middle line:84%
at the state of the art, you
improve what they're doing,

01:15:39.790 --> 01:15:41.450 align:middle line:84%
you know you have a
state of the art paper,

01:15:41.450 --> 01:15:42.800 align:middle line:84%
whereas if you
start from scratch,

01:15:42.800 --> 01:15:44.758 align:middle line:84%
you're going to end up--
you're going to end up

01:15:44.758 --> 01:15:47.300 align:middle line:90%
very much behind the frontier.

01:15:47.300 --> 01:15:50.180 align:middle line:84%
Normally, this is advice for
writing theoretical papers,

01:15:50.180 --> 01:15:51.885 align:middle line:84%
because you take a
theoretical paper.

01:15:51.885 --> 01:15:53.510 align:middle line:84%
You can just-- it's
a well-known thing.

01:15:53.510 --> 01:15:54.870 align:middle line:90%
You take a theoretical model.

01:15:54.870 --> 01:15:56.010 align:middle line:90%
You improve on that.

01:15:56.010 --> 01:15:59.633 align:middle line:84%
You've got a new
and better model.

01:15:59.633 --> 01:16:01.050 align:middle line:84%
The thing I want
to point out here

01:16:01.050 --> 01:16:03.600 align:middle line:84%
is one can do that in
empirical work as well.

01:16:03.600 --> 01:16:06.390 align:middle line:84%
One of my earliest
published papers

01:16:06.390 --> 01:16:08.160 align:middle line:90%
was a paper where I took--

01:16:08.160 --> 01:16:11.070 align:middle line:84%
you'll see later in the semester
where I took a published

01:16:11.070 --> 01:16:12.520 align:middle line:90%
empirical paper by Rob Porter.

01:16:12.520 --> 01:16:15.490 align:middle line:84%
I just did some
additional things on it.

01:16:15.490 --> 01:16:18.240 align:middle line:84%
That was one of my
first published papers.

01:16:18.240 --> 01:16:20.910 align:middle line:84%
Galenianos and Gavazza
decades later are actually

01:16:20.910 --> 01:16:23.192 align:middle line:84%
doing the same thing here,
with the idea that they

01:16:23.192 --> 01:16:24.900 align:middle line:84%
must have read Stango
and Zinman's paper,

01:16:24.900 --> 01:16:26.317 align:middle line:84%
and they thought
Stango and Zinman

01:16:26.317 --> 01:16:29.400 align:middle line:84%
is a very nice paper, showing
that search costs seem

01:16:29.400 --> 01:16:31.060 align:middle line:90%
to be related to markups.

01:16:31.060 --> 01:16:34.140 align:middle line:84%
And what they're
trying to do is, say,

01:16:34.140 --> 01:16:36.180 align:middle line:84%
Stango and Zinman
said that search costs

01:16:36.180 --> 01:16:37.480 align:middle line:90%
are related to markups.

01:16:37.480 --> 01:16:40.200 align:middle line:84%
Can we actually turn
their paper into

01:16:40.200 --> 01:16:43.290 align:middle line:84%
a structural,
quantitative paper,

01:16:43.290 --> 01:16:46.952 align:middle line:84%
where we say, can
search costs explain

01:16:46.952 --> 01:16:48.660 align:middle line:84%
the magnitudes of
everything that they're

01:16:48.660 --> 01:16:49.577 align:middle line:90%
finding in their data?

01:16:49.577 --> 01:16:52.650 align:middle line:84%
Or how would we say, is
their data really consistent

01:16:52.650 --> 01:16:56.340 align:middle line:84%
with the search cost explanation
versus another explanation?

01:16:56.340 --> 01:16:58.990 align:middle line:84%
It's also, I think,
a very nice paper

01:16:58.990 --> 01:17:02.560 align:middle line:84%
in that it's doing
something we don't do often.

01:17:02.560 --> 01:17:04.540 align:middle line:84%
It's doing something
different in IO.

01:17:04.540 --> 01:17:07.060 align:middle line:84%
Like many people have this
view that every IO paper has

01:17:07.060 --> 01:17:09.460 align:middle line:84%
to have this really, really
complicated individual level

01:17:09.460 --> 01:17:13.570 align:middle line:84%
data set and estimate this
sophisticated model observation

01:17:13.570 --> 01:17:14.780 align:middle line:90%
by observation.

01:17:14.780 --> 01:17:17.770 align:middle line:84%
And what Galenianos and
Gavazza do in this paper

01:17:17.770 --> 01:17:21.470 align:middle line:84%
is they're basically doing
a macro style calibration.

01:17:21.470 --> 01:17:24.730 align:middle line:84%
And I think it's not
what other people would

01:17:24.730 --> 01:17:27.250 align:middle line:84%
have done with this data,
but I think that it really

01:17:27.250 --> 01:17:29.500 align:middle line:84%
makes the case that doing
a calibration like this

01:17:29.500 --> 01:17:32.020 align:middle line:84%
can be a very interesting
way to think about

01:17:32.020 --> 01:17:35.120 align:middle line:84%
whether a theory really
explains the data or not.

01:17:35.120 --> 01:17:36.880 align:middle line:84%
And do we always need
to do the same kind

01:17:36.880 --> 01:17:38.330 align:middle line:90%
of structural estimations we do?

01:17:38.330 --> 01:17:42.830 align:middle line:84%
And I think it's a
very elegant paper.

01:17:42.830 --> 01:17:46.150 align:middle line:84%
It also makes some nice
technical observations.

01:17:46.150 --> 01:17:48.018 align:middle line:84%
And so anyway, I'll
do it quickly here,

01:17:48.018 --> 01:17:49.310 align:middle line:90%
because I don't have much time.

01:17:49.310 --> 01:17:55.920 align:middle line:84%
So let me say, the basic thing
is what they show in the paper--

01:17:55.920 --> 01:17:57.760 align:middle line:84%
think back to what
Stango and Zinman showed.

01:17:57.760 --> 01:17:59.718 align:middle line:84%
What Stango and Zinman
showed is that consumers

01:17:59.718 --> 01:18:03.750 align:middle line:84%
get very different offers
in their offer database,

01:18:03.750 --> 01:18:06.810 align:middle line:84%
and that consumers end up
paying very different amounts.

01:18:06.810 --> 01:18:11.240 align:middle line:84%
And so we have these
sort of two data sets.

01:18:11.240 --> 01:18:14.120 align:middle line:84%
We have this offered
data set, and then we

01:18:14.120 --> 01:18:17.000 align:middle line:84%
have the sort of
what consumers--

01:18:17.000 --> 01:18:22.070 align:middle line:84%
the accepted offers, what
consumers end up paying.

01:18:22.070 --> 01:18:25.970 align:middle line:84%
And the starting point
for Galenianos and Gavazza

01:18:25.970 --> 01:18:32.030 align:middle line:84%
is think about we have these
two separate data sets.

01:18:32.030 --> 01:18:36.110 align:middle line:84%
The offers are going to
lead to accepted offers

01:18:36.110 --> 01:18:37.740 align:middle line:90%
based on consumer preferences.

01:18:37.740 --> 01:18:40.400 align:middle line:84%
So if you think about what
consumer preferences look like,

01:18:40.400 --> 01:18:43.310 align:middle line:84%
what are their search costs, how
much they view these offers as

01:18:43.310 --> 01:18:47.000 align:middle line:84%
differentiated, that's going
to lead us to some mapping

01:18:47.000 --> 01:18:49.010 align:middle line:84%
that maps offers
that they receive

01:18:49.010 --> 01:18:50.630 align:middle line:90%
into what offers they accept.

01:18:50.630 --> 01:18:52.880 align:middle line:84%
And notice that you can think
about two different ways

01:18:52.880 --> 01:18:54.680 align:middle line:90%
to explain the mapping.

01:18:54.680 --> 01:18:57.680 align:middle line:84%
We know that these offers
are quite dispersed.

01:18:57.680 --> 01:19:00.350 align:middle line:84%
And we know that these accepted
offers are fairly dispersed

01:19:00.350 --> 01:19:01.820 align:middle line:90%
and quite high.

01:19:01.820 --> 01:19:04.290 align:middle line:84%
You could explain this
mapping in two different ways.

01:19:04.290 --> 01:19:06.437 align:middle line:84%
One way would be that
consumers have search costs.

01:19:06.437 --> 01:19:08.520 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, they don't open
a lot of the envelopes.

01:19:08.520 --> 01:19:10.520 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, they don't all
take the lowest offer.

01:19:10.520 --> 01:19:12.262 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, they
pay high prices,

01:19:12.262 --> 01:19:14.720 align:middle line:84%
or they pay somewhat dispersed
prices, because some of them

01:19:14.720 --> 01:19:18.620 align:middle line:84%
open the low envelope
first and pay that one.

01:19:18.620 --> 01:19:21.350 align:middle line:84%
The other way to explain
this same fact would

01:19:21.350 --> 01:19:23.857 align:middle line:84%
be with consumers just have
very idiosyncratic preferences.

01:19:23.857 --> 01:19:25.190 align:middle line:90%
They get these different offers.

01:19:25.190 --> 01:19:26.630 align:middle line:90%
They open all of them.

01:19:26.630 --> 01:19:28.550 align:middle line:84%
But they care a lot
whether their credit card

01:19:28.550 --> 01:19:31.020 align:middle line:84%
says Bank of America
or Capital One on it.

01:19:31.020 --> 01:19:33.210 align:middle line:84%
Therefore, some
of them pay much,

01:19:33.210 --> 01:19:35.690 align:middle line:84%
much more than they need
to, even after having opened

01:19:35.690 --> 01:19:38.190 align:middle line:84%
all the envelopes, because they
really like that credit card

01:19:38.190 --> 01:19:40.545 align:middle line:90%
company.

01:19:40.545 --> 01:19:43.170 align:middle line:84%
[INAUDIBLE] note
is that we really

01:19:43.170 --> 01:19:45.120 align:middle line:84%
can't just from thinking
about this mapping

01:19:45.120 --> 01:19:48.750 align:middle line:84%
tell the offer, the
differentiation explanation,

01:19:48.750 --> 01:19:50.920 align:middle line:84%
apart from the search
cost explanation.

01:19:50.920 --> 01:19:54.690 align:middle line:84%
But there's also a mapping that
goes in this direction, where

01:19:54.690 --> 01:19:59.310 align:middle line:84%
if you know what consumers
are going to accept,

01:19:59.310 --> 01:20:01.620 align:middle line:84%
the firms who are
profit maximizing

01:20:01.620 --> 01:20:04.180 align:middle line:84%
should be choosing
their offers optimally.

01:20:04.180 --> 01:20:09.420 align:middle line:84%
And if consumers had these very
strong idiosyncratic preferences

01:20:09.420 --> 01:20:11.490 align:middle line:84%
and viewed the offers
as very differentiated,

01:20:11.490 --> 01:20:14.170 align:middle line:84%
then all the firms would
be sending high offers.

01:20:14.170 --> 01:20:16.920 align:middle line:84%
Whereas if it's the
search cost explanation,

01:20:16.920 --> 01:20:19.020 align:middle line:84%
the search cost
explanation could perhaps

01:20:19.020 --> 01:20:22.110 align:middle line:84%
better explain why consumers
are sending low offers.

01:20:22.110 --> 01:20:24.793 align:middle line:84%
That's sort of
intuitive, that we

01:20:24.793 --> 01:20:26.460 align:middle line:84%
need the search cost
to get the firms be

01:20:26.460 --> 01:20:30.580 align:middle line:84%
setting these low and
heterogeneous offers.

01:20:30.580 --> 01:20:34.480 align:middle line:84%
But Galenianos and Gavazza are
trying to say, quantitatively,

01:20:34.480 --> 01:20:35.330 align:middle line:90%
is this true?

01:20:35.330 --> 01:20:41.440 align:middle line:84%
Can we say that a model with
search costs can explain

01:20:41.440 --> 01:20:44.650 align:middle line:84%
the data quantitatively, whereas
a model with differentiated

01:20:44.650 --> 01:20:46.210 align:middle line:90%
products cannot?

01:20:46.210 --> 01:20:47.740 align:middle line:84%
So what they do
is they write down

01:20:47.740 --> 01:20:49.810 align:middle line:84%
a model that's simple
enough that they

01:20:49.810 --> 01:20:52.300 align:middle line:84%
can estimate what
the equilibrium is

01:20:52.300 --> 01:20:53.600 align:middle line:90%
for a set of parameters.

01:20:53.600 --> 01:20:55.540 align:middle line:84%
And then they say,
we're going to calibrate

01:20:55.540 --> 01:20:58.550 align:middle line:84%
the parameters to match some
macro moments in the data.

01:20:58.550 --> 01:21:01.250 align:middle line:84%
And then see if we
can fit the data.

01:21:01.250 --> 01:21:03.000 align:middle line:84%
So what does the
model look like?

01:21:03.000 --> 01:21:07.950 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, the model has
buyers who have value z.

01:21:07.950 --> 01:21:10.930 align:middle line:84%
z is distributed according
to some distribution

01:21:10.930 --> 01:21:12.600 align:middle line:90%
m on some interval.

01:21:12.600 --> 01:21:16.240 align:middle line:84%
So this is your value of having
a credit card from any firm

01:21:16.240 --> 01:21:17.170 align:middle line:90%
whatsoever.

01:21:17.170 --> 01:21:18.700 align:middle line:84%
In their model,
some customers will

01:21:18.700 --> 01:21:21.070 align:middle line:84%
have z relatively low--
won't get a credit card,

01:21:21.070 --> 01:21:24.070 align:middle line:90%
or won't buy any offer.

01:21:24.070 --> 01:21:26.660 align:middle line:84%
There's some lenders
who have mass l.

01:21:26.660 --> 01:21:31.010 align:middle line:84%
These lenders have heterogeneous
costs of supplying funds, k,

01:21:31.010 --> 01:21:33.580 align:middle line:84%
distributed g on k
lower bar, k upper bar.

01:21:33.580 --> 01:21:36.230 align:middle line:90%


01:21:36.230 --> 01:21:40.302 align:middle line:84%
As I said, these
heterogeneous costs

01:21:40.302 --> 01:21:42.260 align:middle line:84%
are going to be the way
they generate the price

01:21:42.260 --> 01:21:44.060 align:middle line:90%
dispersion in the data.

01:21:44.060 --> 01:21:48.440 align:middle line:84%
In the Stahl model, you can
generate price dispersion by--

01:21:48.440 --> 01:21:51.320 align:middle line:84%
generate price dispersion by all
the firms are exactly identical,

01:21:51.320 --> 01:21:53.400 align:middle line:84%
and they just
randomize over prices.

01:21:53.400 --> 01:21:57.207 align:middle line:84%
But there's an earlier
literature on price dispersion,

01:21:57.207 --> 01:21:59.540 align:middle line:84%
going back to Jennifer Reinganum
has one of the earliest

01:21:59.540 --> 01:22:03.170 align:middle line:84%
models of this variety,
where you can also

01:22:03.170 --> 01:22:06.050 align:middle line:84%
get price dispersion as a
pure strategy phenomenon

01:22:06.050 --> 01:22:09.870 align:middle line:84%
by simply allowing the firms to
have asymmetric different costs.

01:22:09.870 --> 01:22:13.250 align:middle line:84%
And then where the firms with
identical costs randomize,

01:22:13.250 --> 01:22:14.900 align:middle line:84%
you can have a
purification of that,

01:22:14.900 --> 01:22:17.180 align:middle line:84%
where the firms with low
costs set low prices.

01:22:17.180 --> 01:22:19.110 align:middle line:84%
Firms with high costs
set high prices.

01:22:19.110 --> 01:22:22.860 align:middle line:84%
So they're going to add
this lender heterogeneity.

01:22:22.860 --> 01:22:25.520 align:middle line:84%
And then, interesting
model here is

01:22:25.520 --> 01:22:28.340 align:middle line:84%
that rather than having buyers
choose an equilibrium, how

01:22:28.340 --> 01:22:31.430 align:middle line:84%
many offers to get,
the buyers are going

01:22:31.430 --> 01:22:34.250 align:middle line:90%
to exert an effort level s.

01:22:34.250 --> 01:22:36.560 align:middle line:84%
And then if you exert
effort level s-- you

01:22:36.560 --> 01:22:39.620 align:middle line:84%
can think about I'm going to
spend one hour on my computer,

01:22:39.620 --> 01:22:43.850 align:middle line:84%
looking up credit cards online,
and see what offers I can find.

01:22:43.850 --> 01:22:46.890 align:middle line:84%
They have a Poisson
random number of offers.

01:22:46.890 --> 01:22:54.680 align:middle line:84%
So if I spend time s
searching, I get s of--

01:22:54.680 --> 01:22:57.980 align:middle line:84%
in expectation, I
get s times L offers,

01:22:57.980 --> 01:23:01.610 align:middle line:84%
but I get a Poisson
random number of offers.

01:23:01.610 --> 01:23:03.385 align:middle line:84%
So I may actually
find zero offers.

01:23:03.385 --> 01:23:04.010 align:middle line:90%
I may find one.

01:23:04.010 --> 01:23:04.730 align:middle line:90%
I may find two.

01:23:04.730 --> 01:23:07.190 align:middle line:90%
I may find three.

01:23:07.190 --> 01:23:10.190 align:middle line:84%
Doing this effort
has a nice feature

01:23:10.190 --> 01:23:12.058 align:middle line:90%
that s is a continuous variable.

01:23:12.058 --> 01:23:13.850 align:middle line:84%
I can do a first order
condition for what's

01:23:13.850 --> 01:23:17.210 align:middle line:84%
the optimal amount of
search effort s to make.

01:23:17.210 --> 01:23:19.820 align:middle line:84%
You might think, well,
yes, you made s continuous,

01:23:19.820 --> 01:23:21.290 align:middle line:84%
but now you've got
this nightmare,

01:23:21.290 --> 01:23:22.880 align:middle line:90%
where instead of people having--

01:23:22.880 --> 01:23:26.570 align:middle line:84%
all having one offer, or
two, or three, you've got--

01:23:26.570 --> 01:23:28.860 align:middle line:84%
I'm getting this Poisson
random number of offers.

01:23:28.860 --> 01:23:30.560 align:middle line:84%
How am I going to
do that at all?

01:23:30.560 --> 01:23:32.930 align:middle line:84%
An observation in
this paper is that--

01:23:32.930 --> 01:23:35.540 align:middle line:84%
and there also been some
others who have recently have

01:23:35.540 --> 01:23:38.900 align:middle line:84%
done the same thing, is that
this Poisson random number

01:23:38.900 --> 01:23:41.690 align:middle line:84%
of quotes can actually
be a more tractable model

01:23:41.690 --> 01:23:44.040 align:middle line:84%
than having a deterministic
number of quotes.

01:23:44.040 --> 01:23:46.550 align:middle line:84%
And so because it's got
this feature that effort

01:23:46.550 --> 01:23:49.460 align:middle line:84%
is continuous, giving us
a continuous first order

01:23:49.460 --> 01:23:53.570 align:middle line:84%
condition, and the
expected value to search

01:23:53.570 --> 01:23:56.260 align:middle line:84%
is actually tractable, this
can be a very nice framework.

01:23:56.260 --> 01:23:58.010 align:middle line:84%
And I think other
people are going to want

01:23:58.010 --> 01:23:59.490 align:middle line:90%
to do this in the future.

01:23:59.490 --> 01:24:01.280 align:middle line:84%
And let me say, also,
if you remember back

01:24:01.280 --> 01:24:04.190 align:middle line:84%
to intuition for Stahl,
to get heterogeneity

01:24:04.190 --> 01:24:08.060 align:middle line:84%
in accepted offers,
what Stahl did is

01:24:08.060 --> 01:24:09.710 align:middle line:90%
have some people who get very--

01:24:09.710 --> 01:24:11.990 align:middle line:84%
some people who have
negative or low search

01:24:11.990 --> 01:24:13.410 align:middle line:90%
costs, who search every firm.

01:24:13.410 --> 01:24:16.820 align:middle line:84%
Some people with high search
costs search all the firms.

01:24:16.820 --> 01:24:18.950 align:middle line:84%
In Galenianos and
Gavazza, they don't

01:24:18.950 --> 01:24:21.380 align:middle line:84%
have randomness in
the search costs,

01:24:21.380 --> 01:24:24.740 align:middle line:84%
but what they have is this
randomness in the value z.

01:24:24.740 --> 01:24:26.600 align:middle line:84%
If you have a high
value for a credit card,

01:24:26.600 --> 01:24:29.000 align:middle line:84%
you're going to want
to get a lot of quotes,

01:24:29.000 --> 01:24:31.980 align:middle line:84%
because you know you
need to buy at least one.

01:24:31.980 --> 01:24:33.980 align:middle line:84%
If you have a low
value z, you're

01:24:33.980 --> 01:24:35.570 align:middle line:84%
going to do a little
search effort,

01:24:35.570 --> 01:24:37.760 align:middle line:84%
because you're going to
be perfectly happy to not

01:24:37.760 --> 01:24:39.030 align:middle line:90%
buy from anyone.

01:24:39.030 --> 01:24:43.290 align:middle line:84%
And it's the
heterogeneity in z that's

01:24:43.290 --> 01:24:45.290 align:middle line:84%
going to lead to heterogeneity
in search effort.

01:24:45.290 --> 01:24:47.930 align:middle line:84%
That's going to lead to
equilibrium price dispersion.

01:24:47.930 --> 01:24:50.990 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so going over
the model quickly,

01:24:50.990 --> 01:24:53.360 align:middle line:84%
buyers have this payoff
from getting a card, which

01:24:53.360 --> 01:24:57.410 align:middle line:84%
is their raw value from getting
a card minus the disutility

01:24:57.410 --> 01:25:00.620 align:middle line:84%
of the interest rate minus
an epsilon, which allows

01:25:00.620 --> 01:25:02.690 align:middle line:84%
for this sort of we're
going to have the horse

01:25:02.690 --> 01:25:06.420 align:middle line:84%
race between search costs
versus product differentiation.

01:25:06.420 --> 01:25:09.650 align:middle line:84%
So they allow for
product differentiation.

01:25:09.650 --> 01:25:14.875 align:middle line:84%
They write Fc for the CDF of a
random draw of this plus this.

01:25:14.875 --> 01:25:16.250 align:middle line:84%
That's obviously
something that's

01:25:16.250 --> 01:25:17.900 align:middle line:90%
determined in equilibrium.

01:25:17.900 --> 01:25:24.470 align:middle line:84%
And then the seller's payoff is
going to be the probability of--

01:25:24.470 --> 01:25:26.690 align:middle line:84%
the probability that
someone buys from you

01:25:26.690 --> 01:25:29.480 align:middle line:84%
if your interest rate
is R times the payoff

01:25:29.480 --> 01:25:31.730 align:middle line:84%
you get if your interest
rate is R, which is R times 1

01:25:31.730 --> 01:25:34.890 align:middle line:84%
minus rho, minus k, which
is the cost of capital.

01:25:34.890 --> 01:25:37.310 align:middle line:84%
And I see I've got
a misprint here.

01:25:37.310 --> 01:25:39.680 align:middle line:84%
R-- rho would be the
default probability here,

01:25:39.680 --> 01:25:42.590 align:middle line:84%
because your payoff is
R times the probability

01:25:42.590 --> 01:25:44.970 align:middle line:84%
the customer pays you
back, which is 1 minus rho.

01:25:44.970 --> 01:25:49.340 align:middle line:84%
So rho would be the
default probability.

01:25:49.340 --> 01:25:52.070 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, as I said, the
Poisson search model

01:25:52.070 --> 01:25:54.770 align:middle line:90%
is surprisingly tractable.

01:25:54.770 --> 01:25:56.550 align:middle line:90%
Effort is a continuous variable.

01:25:56.550 --> 01:25:58.310 align:middle line:84%
And then when you
think about what's

01:25:58.310 --> 01:26:02.660 align:middle line:84%
the probability that a consumer
accepts an offer with total cost

01:26:02.660 --> 01:26:06.680 align:middle line:84%
c, the probability
that a consumer accepts

01:26:06.680 --> 01:26:09.440 align:middle line:84%
an offer with total
cost to them of c,

01:26:09.440 --> 01:26:12.500 align:middle line:84%
you're summing over the
number of offers they receive.

01:26:12.500 --> 01:26:14.210 align:middle line:90%
n equals 0 to infinity.

01:26:14.210 --> 01:26:16.700 align:middle line:84%
So n is the number of
offers they receive.

01:26:16.700 --> 01:26:21.620 align:middle line:84%
If they receive n
offers other than yours,

01:26:21.620 --> 01:26:24.710 align:middle line:84%
they're going to buy your
product with probability 1 minus

01:26:24.710 --> 01:26:26.430 align:middle line:90%
F of c to the n-th power.

01:26:26.430 --> 01:26:30.470 align:middle line:84%
They only buy your product if
every other product they get

01:26:30.470 --> 01:26:31.740 align:middle line:90%
has higher cost to them.

01:26:31.740 --> 01:26:34.430 align:middle line:84%
Or again, cost includes
the financial cost

01:26:34.430 --> 01:26:36.870 align:middle line:84%
and the idiosyncratic
preference cost.

01:26:36.870 --> 01:26:39.860 align:middle line:84%
So it's the sum of
e to the minus--

01:26:39.860 --> 01:26:41.720 align:middle line:84%
so this is the
Poisson probability

01:26:41.720 --> 01:26:45.560 align:middle line:84%
that if they do effort, I
guess if they do effort, a sub

01:26:45.560 --> 01:26:49.350 align:middle line:84%
z, alpha sub z, they
get n costs that's

01:26:49.350 --> 01:26:52.870 align:middle line:84%
e to the minus alpha z, alpha
z to the n over n factorial.

01:26:52.870 --> 01:26:55.200 align:middle line:84%
If you look at
this, it's this sum.

01:26:55.200 --> 01:26:58.080 align:middle line:84%
And you notice it's got e to the
minus alpha z over n factorial,

01:26:58.080 --> 01:27:00.880 align:middle line:84%
alpha z to the n, 1
minus F of c to the n.

01:27:00.880 --> 01:27:04.180 align:middle line:84%
So it's just like this
and this go together.

01:27:04.180 --> 01:27:06.600 align:middle line:84%
And so the probability
they buy from you is just

01:27:06.600 --> 01:27:10.070 align:middle line:84%
e to the minus alpha
z times F of c.

01:27:10.070 --> 01:27:15.530 align:middle line:84%
And then when I set a price, I'm
setting an interest rate of--

01:27:15.530 --> 01:27:17.600 align:middle line:84%
I'm not setting
a cost c to them.

01:27:17.600 --> 01:27:20.100 align:middle line:84%
I'm setting an interest rate,
and then they have an epsilon.

01:27:20.100 --> 01:27:22.235 align:middle line:84%
So there is an integral
over the epsilon

01:27:22.235 --> 01:27:25.010 align:middle line:84%
to determine the
probability of purchase.

01:27:25.010 --> 01:27:27.380 align:middle line:84%
But anyway, we get
this sort of nice

01:27:27.380 --> 01:27:29.780 align:middle line:84%
closed form for the
probability of purchase

01:27:29.780 --> 01:27:33.710 align:middle line:84%
as a function of
the interest rate

01:27:33.710 --> 01:27:35.940 align:middle line:84%
that I do charge
on a credit card.

01:27:35.940 --> 01:27:37.760 align:middle line:90%
And so this sort of like--

01:27:37.760 --> 01:27:39.700 align:middle line:84%
in some sense, this
tractability of the model

01:27:39.700 --> 01:27:42.200 align:middle line:84%
is just a key step that makes
it possible to solve the model

01:27:42.200 --> 01:27:44.450 align:middle line:90%
given any set of parameters.

01:27:44.450 --> 01:27:45.600 align:middle line:90%
What do they do?

01:27:45.600 --> 01:27:49.660 align:middle line:84%
Well, they make parametric forms
for many of the primitives.

01:27:49.660 --> 01:27:52.160 align:middle line:84%
For example, assuming that the
buyer's value distribution is

01:27:52.160 --> 01:27:54.830 align:middle line:84%
log normal with parameters
to be estimated.

01:27:54.830 --> 01:27:58.310 align:middle line:84%
I guess this is off a working
paper version that had 11 model

01:27:58.310 --> 01:28:00.030 align:middle line:90%
parameters and 15 moments.

01:28:00.030 --> 01:28:02.042 align:middle line:84%
I think the published
version of the paper has--

01:28:02.042 --> 01:28:03.500 align:middle line:84%
referees always
ask you to do more.

01:28:03.500 --> 01:28:05.540 align:middle line:84%
I think the published
paper has 24 model--

01:28:05.540 --> 01:28:08.490 align:middle line:84%
28 model parameters, and
36 moments to be matched.

01:28:08.490 --> 01:28:13.140 align:middle line:84%
But basically, you're
setting model parameters--

01:28:13.140 --> 01:28:16.170 align:middle line:84%
parameters of the various
distributions that are there

01:28:16.170 --> 01:28:21.070 align:middle line:84%
to match a bunch of
moments in the data, like,

01:28:21.070 --> 01:28:25.730 align:middle line:84%
what is the 10th percentile
of the accepted offers,

01:28:25.730 --> 01:28:28.240 align:middle line:84%
median accepted
offer, 90% offer?

01:28:28.240 --> 01:28:30.783 align:middle line:84%
What fraction of consumers are
getting at least two offers?

01:28:30.783 --> 01:28:32.200 align:middle line:84%
What's the median
number of offers

01:28:32.200 --> 01:28:33.650 align:middle line:90%
conditional on getting two?

01:28:33.650 --> 01:28:36.790 align:middle line:84%
So you're trying to
explain simultaneously

01:28:36.790 --> 01:28:40.660 align:middle line:84%
what are the
accepted offers here

01:28:40.660 --> 01:28:43.040 align:middle line:84%
and what are the
offers being sent.

01:28:43.040 --> 01:28:45.440 align:middle line:84%
We're not trying to explain
them customer by customer.

01:28:45.440 --> 01:28:47.230 align:middle line:84%
We're just trying to
say, I want a model

01:28:47.230 --> 01:28:50.200 align:middle line:84%
that gives this amount
of distribution that's

01:28:50.200 --> 01:28:53.890 align:middle line:84%
observed in the offers and the
amount of dispersion that's

01:28:53.890 --> 01:28:55.760 align:middle line:90%
observed in the accepted offers.

01:28:55.760 --> 01:28:59.050 align:middle line:84%
And I need to match
other parameters

01:28:59.050 --> 01:29:00.490 align:middle line:90%
that I know from the data.

01:29:00.490 --> 01:29:03.640 align:middle line:84%
Anyway, so what they do is
they estimate these things.

01:29:03.640 --> 01:29:05.560 align:middle line:84%
And then the-- I
won't go through it,

01:29:05.560 --> 01:29:07.570 align:middle line:84%
but the main thing
they then have

01:29:07.570 --> 01:29:09.370 align:middle line:84%
is with this
calibrated model, they

01:29:09.370 --> 01:29:11.020 align:middle line:90%
talk about a counterfactual.

01:29:11.020 --> 01:29:14.050 align:middle line:84%
Counterfactual is, what would
be the effect of capping

01:29:14.050 --> 01:29:15.320 align:middle line:90%
credit card interest rates?

01:29:15.320 --> 01:29:18.800 align:middle line:84%
So many people pay 35%
interest on their credit cards.

01:29:18.800 --> 01:29:22.310 align:middle line:84%
You could ask, suppose in
instead of charging everybody

01:29:22.310 --> 01:29:24.710 align:middle line:84%
35% on the credit
cards, you put a law

01:29:24.710 --> 01:29:30.860 align:middle line:84%
that says you can't charge more
than 27.5%, or 25%, or 22%?

01:29:30.860 --> 01:29:32.550 align:middle line:84%
That's going to
have two effects.

01:29:32.550 --> 01:29:34.040 align:middle line:84%
One is that some
people are going

01:29:34.040 --> 01:29:35.450 align:middle line:84%
to pay much less on
their credit cards,

01:29:35.450 --> 01:29:36.560 align:middle line:84%
because they're going
to get an offer that

01:29:36.560 --> 01:29:37.680 align:middle line:90%
has a lower rate in the mail.

01:29:37.680 --> 01:29:39.660 align:middle line:84%
They're going to buy an
offer that is a lower rate.

01:29:39.660 --> 01:29:42.350 align:middle line:84%
Some people are going to be hurt
by that, because they can't get

01:29:42.350 --> 01:29:45.050 align:middle line:84%
a credit card, because
people would not

01:29:45.050 --> 01:29:47.640 align:middle line:84%
be willing to lend to them
at the lower 25% rate.

01:29:47.640 --> 01:29:50.510 align:middle line:84%
They're just going to get
no credit card at all.

01:29:50.510 --> 01:29:52.130 align:middle line:84%
What they find--
do I have things

01:29:52.130 --> 01:29:55.280 align:middle line:84%
here-- is that they
find that actually here,

01:29:55.280 --> 01:29:59.030 align:middle line:84%
their model predicts that laws
capping credit card interest

01:29:59.030 --> 01:30:00.530 align:middle line:90%
rates should work well.

01:30:00.530 --> 01:30:03.440 align:middle line:84%
What we get is that when
you cap interest rates

01:30:03.440 --> 01:30:11.244 align:middle line:84%
at 27.5% with these parameters,
that's good for consumers.

01:30:11.244 --> 01:30:13.930 align:middle line:84%
Yes, it is true that some
consumers are locked out

01:30:13.930 --> 01:30:16.540 align:middle line:84%
of the market, but the number
of consumers who are locked out

01:30:16.540 --> 01:30:19.300 align:middle line:84%
of the market is fairly small,
and many other consumers

01:30:19.300 --> 01:30:21.980 align:middle line:84%
benefit from the drop
in interest rates.

01:30:21.980 --> 01:30:23.740 align:middle line:90%
They do notice that--

01:30:23.740 --> 01:30:26.950 align:middle line:84%
they do have results
showing that these are not

01:30:26.950 --> 01:30:27.940 align:middle line:90%
monotone effects.

01:30:27.940 --> 01:30:31.720 align:middle line:90%


01:30:31.720 --> 01:30:34.690 align:middle line:84%
Lowering the-- what's
best for consumers

01:30:34.690 --> 01:30:36.440 align:middle line:84%
is not to have a cap,
obviously, of zero,

01:30:36.440 --> 01:30:37.940 align:middle line:84%
because then no one
would be served.

01:30:37.940 --> 01:30:39.398 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, in
their data, I think

01:30:39.398 --> 01:30:42.640 align:middle line:84%
they show that consumers are
better off with a 27.5% cap

01:30:42.640 --> 01:30:43.360 align:middle line:90%
than with no cap.

01:30:43.360 --> 01:30:45.740 align:middle line:84%
They're better off
still with a 25% cap.

01:30:45.740 --> 01:30:49.030 align:middle line:84%
When you go to 22.5%, consumers
start to be worse off.

01:30:49.030 --> 01:30:54.010 align:middle line:84%
So anyway, I think it's a very
nice paper in two dimensions.

01:30:54.010 --> 01:30:55.510 align:middle line:90%
It's got this sort of--

01:30:55.510 --> 01:30:58.420 align:middle line:84%
it's got originality, and
thinking of doing a calibration,

01:30:58.420 --> 01:31:01.060 align:middle line:84%
rather than doing what
we normally do in IO.

01:31:01.060 --> 01:31:04.330 align:middle line:84%
It really makes the case that
this model quantitatively

01:31:04.330 --> 01:31:05.870 align:middle line:90%
can explain what goes on.

01:31:05.870 --> 01:31:08.810 align:middle line:84%
And several of the key moments
can be explained simultaneously

01:31:08.810 --> 01:31:09.677 align:middle line:90%
by this model.

01:31:09.677 --> 01:31:11.510 align:middle line:84%
And then it has this
sort of counterfactual,

01:31:11.510 --> 01:31:14.297 align:middle line:84%
saying that, here's a policy
relevant question we care about.

01:31:14.297 --> 01:31:16.130 align:middle line:84%
What would happen if
you cap interest rates?

01:31:16.130 --> 01:31:17.960 align:middle line:84%
And with their
model, they predict

01:31:17.960 --> 01:31:21.920 align:middle line:84%
that capping interest rates at
still high, but moderate rates

01:31:21.920 --> 01:31:23.810 align:middle line:90%
would be good for consumers.

01:31:23.810 --> 01:31:26.370 align:middle line:90%
So that's all I've got for you.

01:31:26.370 --> 01:31:28.730 align:middle line:84%
So I guess what
we're going to do,

01:31:28.730 --> 01:31:30.680 align:middle line:84%
we have a bit of a
non-standard timing

01:31:30.680 --> 01:31:33.350 align:middle line:90%
in the physical class at MIT.

01:31:33.350 --> 01:31:35.970 align:middle line:84%
Jean Tirole is going to come
give guest lectures next week.

01:31:35.970 --> 01:31:37.820 align:middle line:84%
So we'll have two
lectures from Jean,

01:31:37.820 --> 01:31:39.920 align:middle line:84%
and then Tobias will
come back the week after

01:31:39.920 --> 01:31:42.170 align:middle line:84%
and talk about structural
work on search.

01:31:42.170 --> 01:31:44.000 align:middle line:84%
I guess in the OCW
course, for those of you

01:31:44.000 --> 01:31:45.500 align:middle line:84%
who are watching
this there, I think

01:31:45.500 --> 01:31:48.530 align:middle line:84%
we may flip back to the
natural order, and have--

01:31:48.530 --> 01:31:50.750 align:middle line:84%
next lecture will be the
third lecture on search,

01:31:50.750 --> 01:31:52.370 align:middle line:84%
and then we'll have
the guest lectures

01:31:52.370 --> 01:31:54.850 align:middle line:90%
by Jean on platform markets.

01:31:54.850 --> 01:32:13.000 align:middle line:90%